ATSWA.put("PS", {"intro":"Public Sector Accounting is the most legalistic paper in Part II. Almost every answer rests on a statute, a regulation or an IPSAS, and the examiners reward candidates who can cite the authority as well as describe the practice. The chapters below therefore name the section of the Constitution, the Act or the Standard wherever one governs — and set out the statutory financial statements in the exact form in which they must be presented.","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Nature and Scope of Public Sector Accounting","brief":"What the public sector is, the six-stage process public sector accounting actually runs through, why it exists (its real objectives, not just \"accountability\" as a slogan), who reads it, the five bases it can be done on, and the alphabet soup of international and Nigerian bodies that set its rules — including the FRCN, whose full establishing Act this study text quotes almost in its entirety.","outcomes":["Define and explain terminology in public sector accounting","Identify the objectives of public sector accounting","Understand and appreciate the users of public sector accounting information","Identify the concepts and principles applicable to public sector accounting","Identify the five bases of public sector accounting","Distinguish a non-profit-oriented organisation from a profit-oriented one","Explain the objectives and details of the professional pronouncements on public sector accounting, including the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria"],"secs":[{"n":"1.1","t":"What \"public sector\" means, and the six things public sector accounting actually does","b":[{"p":"The simplest definition of **\"public sector\"** is \"all organisations which are not privately owned and operated, but which are established, run and financed by government on behalf of the public.\" This definition conveys the idea that the public sector consists of organisations where control lies in the hands of the public, as opposed to private owners, and whose objectives involve the provision of services, where profit-making is not a primary objective. Performance measurement in the public sector is made harder by three things at once: the lack of a profit motive, the presence of multiple objectives pursued simultaneously, and the presence of **intangible services**, whose benefits are difficult to quantify — you can price a bag of cement; you cannot straightforwardly price \"a safer neighbourhood.\""},{"def":{"t":"Public sector accounting","d":"a process of recording, communicating, summarising, analysing and interpreting government financial statements and statistics in aggregate and in detail; it is concerned with the receipt, custody, disbursement and rendering of stewardship on public funds entrusted."}},{"p":"That definition is worth learning in full — examiners frequently ask for it close to verbatim, and \"receipt, custody, disbursement and rendering of stewardship\" is where the marks sit. But the definition alone is compressed; the study text unpacks it into **six stages**, and it is worth understanding each one rather than only reciting the label, because a question can just as easily ask \"what does recording involve?\" as it can ask for the definition itself."},{"ol":["**Recording** — documenting the financial transactions and activities in the necessary books of account, e.g. the cashbook, the ledger and the vote book.","**Analysing** — separating transactions according to their distinct nature, and posting them under the appropriate heads and sub-heads.","**Classifying** — grouping the transactions into revenue and expense descriptions, and bringing them under major classes as \"Revenue Head\" and \"Sub-heads\" with their relevant code numbers of accounts.","**Summarising** — bringing together all the classes of accounts and preparing them into reports periodically, as statutorily or organisationally required.","**Communicating** — making available financial reports on all the government financial activities, from the necessary accounting summaries, to various interested parties. The style of communication adopted should be unambiguous, lucid and, as far as possible, devoid of jargon.","**Interpreting** — the process that ends the chain, giving explanations on what has been reported in the various financial statements and reports, as regards the overall operations and performance of the relevant government organisation(s). This is to enable the necessary parties and users to take relevant decisions based on their assessments of the reports."]},{"key":"Notice the chain ends at **interpretation**, not at reporting — exactly the same lesson as Chapter 1 of Financial Accounting, where accounting only finishes once somebody has been told something useful. A government that publishes a report nobody can understand has completed five of these six stages and failed at the one that actually mattered."}]},{"n":"1.2","t":"Nature, objectives and characteristics","b":[{"h3":"Nature"},{"p":"The term **\"Public Sector\"** refers to all organisations which are created, administered and financed by Government, from the taxpayers' money, on behalf of the members of the public. Such establishments are referred to as the **\"three tiers\"** — Government, Companies, Parastatals and other public agencies created by the Nation's Constitution, Acts of Parliament and Bye-Laws."},{"h3":"The main objectives of public sector accounting"},{"p":"Five objectives, each worth understanding rather than memorising as a bare phrase:"},{"ol":["**Determining the legitimacy of transactions and their compliance with the statutes and accepted norms.** Public sector disbursements should accord with the provisions of the Appropriation Act and Financial Regulations. There should be due authorisation for all payments so as to avoid misappropriation.","**Providing evidence of stewardship.** The act of rendering stewardship is being able to account transparently and diligently for resources entrusted. Government and public sector officers are obliged to display due diligence and a sense of probity in the collection and disposal of public funds.","**Assisting planning and control.** The future is full of risks and uncertainties. Therefore, mapping out strategic plans prevents an organisation from \"drowning in the tides.\" Plans of action provide the focus of activities which are being pursued — the unforeseen circumstance is built into plans so as to prevent, or at least reduce, corporate failure. Public sector establishments should act in accordance with the **\"mandate theory\"** of governance. Control measures are adjuncts to skilful planning; they assist in avoiding unnecessary deviations from the pursuit of the original objectives set.","**Ensuring objective and timely reporting.** Users of public sector accounting information are anxious to bridge their knowledge gaps on government activities. They therefore treasure prompt and accurate statistics with which to evaluate government performance.","**Evaluating the costs incurred and the benefits derivable.** In public sector organisations, it is difficult to measure costs and benefits in financial terms. The analysis of cost-benefit assesses the economic and social advantages (benefits) and disadvantages or inconveniences (costs) of alternative courses of action, to ensure that the welfare of the citizens is well provided for."]},{"p":"Beyond these five, the study text lists a further set of **other objectives**:"},{"ol":["Enhancing the appraisal of the efficiency of management.","Highlighting the various sources of revenue receivable and expenditure incurred.","Identifying the sources of funding for capital projects.","Evaluating the economy, efficiency and effectiveness with which public sector organisations pursue their goals and objectives.","Ensuring that costs are matched by at least equivalent benefits accruing therefrom.","Providing the details of outstanding long-term commitments and financial obligations.","Providing the means by which actual performance may be compared with the set target.","Proffering solutions to the various bottlenecks and/or problems identified.","Providing the basis for decision-making."]},{"key":"The four italicised words in objective (v) above — **economy, efficiency and effectiveness** — are the \"three Es\" of value-for-money, and they resurface throughout this paper (and in Chapter 15 of Financial Accounting's interpretation work) as the standard framework for judging whether public money was well spent, not merely lawfully spent."},{"h3":"Characteristics of public sector accounting"},{"p":"Three characteristics distinguish public sector accounting from an ordinary business's accounting:"},{"ol":["There are **distinct aspects of accounting information, classification and procedures**, which apply only to transactions made by the government. Examples are the budgeting system and applicable procedures, fiscal policy, accounting methods and sources of revenue. The peculiar nature of government transactions makes it desirable — and indeed mandatory — to treat them in accordance with specific, but cohesive and standardised, measurement approaches and rules.","In view of the requirement to obtain **legislative approval** for government revenue and expenditure, budgeting largely determines the structure of Public Sector Accounting. The government sometimes finds it necessary to segregate its resources into specific or special-purpose compartments — a set-up of receipts and expenditure known as **\"Funds.\"** The method of accounting adopted in recording and measuring the Funds is referred to as **\"fund accounting.\"**","Another peculiarity of government operations is that the accounting system is traditionally maintained on a **\"cash basis.\"** Only transactions involving the movement of cash come into reckoning. Although the approach has its inadequacies, the general practice has been to adopt the \"cash-basis\" of accounting. All assets are written off as they are regarded as consumed at the point they are paid for. Accordingly, government's statement of assets and liabilities does not contain information on tangible assets, and neither is depreciation charged in the revenue and expenditure accounts."]},{"note":"This is your first sight of \"fund accounting\" and of the Consolidated Revenue Fund family of concepts — the study text only names them here in passing, as an example of characteristic (ii). The full treatment of the Constitution's specific funds — the Consolidated Revenue Fund, the Development Fund, the Contingencies Fund and the Federation Account — belongs to Chapter 2, on the constitutional and regulatory framework. Because this chapter's own quiz already draws on that material as essential context, a compact version is given at the end of this chapter (§1.8) so you are not left without it until Chapter 2 is reached."}]},{"n":"1.3","t":"Users of public sector accounting information","b":[{"p":"There are two groups of users of Public Sector Accounting information: **\"Internal\"** and **\"External\"** users, whose peculiarities and areas of interest are as follows."},{"h3":"Internal users and their interest areas"},{"ol":["**The Labour Union** in the public service, which will press for improved conditions of employment and security of tenure for its members;","**Members of the Executive Arm of Government**, such as the President, Ministers and Governors, Commissioners and Chairmen of Local Governments. Their interest areas are to ensure probity and accountability through record-keeping and performance control, which are achieved through accounting information; and","**Top management members**, such as Permanent Secretaries of various Ministries and Chief Executives of Parastatals. They are the conduit of accounting information generation and transmission, and serve as liaison officers between Government, employees and the public."]},{"h3":"External users and their areas of interest"},{"ol":["**Members of the Legislature**, at National, State and Local Government levels. Information in the accounts of Governments is the major medium through which politicians render stewardship to their constituencies and apprise them of the endeavours of governance;","**Members of the public**, to demonstrate accountability and assist the people to appreciate — or otherwise — the efforts of Governments;","**Researchers and financial journalists.** Researchers are expected to develop new and better ideas of governance; financial journalists cherish accounting information to advise existing and potential investors;","**Financial institutions**, such as Commercial Banks, the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund (IMF). Accounting information assists them to evaluate the credit rating of a borrowing Nation;","**Governments, apart from the one reporting.** Governments collaborate on ideas of investment and research; they require accounting information on the well-being, or otherwise, of each other;","**Suppliers and contractors**, who are eager to ascertain the ability of a government to pay for goods and services delivered — only accounting information can be revealing on this point;","**Political Parties, Trade Unions and Civil Liberty Organisations**; and","**Foreign countries and foreign financial institutions**, such as the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank. Like other external users, they require accounting information to ascertain the financial viability of the public sector organisations and the efficiency and effectiveness of management — and to know whether the accounting information enhances the quality, consistency and transparency of public sector financial reporting."]},{"note":"A question asking for \"users and their information needs\" wants both halves of each pair — the user, and specifically what that user is looking for. Listing users without saying what each wants typically scores only about half the available marks. Notice also that \"financial institutions\" and \"foreign financial institutions\" appear as two separate, if overlapping, entries in the source's own list (iv) and (viii) — the second emphasises the *foreign* and *comparative* angle (comparing this government's standing against others') rather than simply repeating the first."}]},{"n":"1.4","t":"Concepts and principles applicable to public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"**Concepts** have been defined as broad basic assumptions underlying the preparation of financial statements of an enterprise. Public sector accounting is an integral but separate branch of financial accounting, sharing in common many concepts and principles applicable in the private sector. The study text names six such concepts — materiality, periodicity, duality, entity, historical cost and going concern — without itself re-defining them, on the assumption you already met them in Financial Accounting Chapter 1. Since they are examinable here too, and it costs nothing to be certain, each is restated below in the specifically public-sector reading:"},{"ul":["**Materiality** — information is material if its omission or misstatement could influence the decisions users take on the basis of the financial statements. In a public sector context, materiality is judged against the size of the vote or the fund concerned, not against the size of government as a whole — a ₦50 million discrepancy is immaterial to the Federation Account, but may be highly material to a single Local Government's budget.","**Periodicity** — the endless life of government is divided into reporting periods (financial years) so that performance and position can be reported at regular intervals, rather than only once, at the end of time. Nigeria's public sector financial year runs from 1 January to 31 December.","**Duality** — every transaction has two effects, exactly as in double-entry bookkeeping. This underlies the double-entry cash books and ledgers kept in government accounting units, even where the reporting basis itself is cash rather than accrual.","**Entity** — the government unit or agency is accounted for as a unit separate and distinct from the individuals — ministers, civil servants, contractors — who transact with it. A Permanent Secretary's personal bank account is never part of the Ministry's accounts, for exactly the same reason a sole trader's personal spending is never part of the business's accounts.","**Historical cost** — assets and transactions are recorded at the amount of cash, or cash equivalent, actually paid or received. Under a cash-basis system this concept does unusually heavy lifting, since — as characteristic (iii) in §1.2 above already noted — no later remeasurement (depreciation, revaluation, impairment) is applied at all: the historical cost figure is also, in effect, the only figure that ever exists for the asset.","**Going concern** — the reporting government entity is assumed to continue operating for the foreseeable future. Unlike a company, which can be wound up, a government is, for accounting purposes, presumed perpetual — this is part of why public sector accounting has historically felt less urgency about asset valuation than the private sector: there is no liquidation scenario to prepare the statements for."]},{"key":"Learn these by the same discipline used throughout Financial Accounting: you are far more often asked which concept a described situation illustrates or breaches than asked to define one cold. A ministry that fails to record a transaction because \"it was paid from a special account outside the normal ledger\" has breached the **entity** concept, not merely made a bookkeeping slip."}]},{"n":"1.5","t":"Bases of public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"The following are the bases under which the financial statements of a public sector enterprise are compiled: **cash basis; accrual basis; commitment basis; modified cash basis; and modified accrual basis.**"},{"h3":"Cash basis"},{"p":"It is the basis of accounting under which revenue is recorded only when cash is received, and expenditure recognised only when cash is paid, irrespective of the fact that the transactions might have occurred in the previous accounting period. This is a simple method of recording financial transactions in the public sector."},{"h4":"Advantages of the cash basis"},{"ol":["It is simple to understand;","It eliminates the existence of debtors and creditors;","It permits easy identification of those who authorise payments and collect revenue;","It allows for comparison between the amount provided in the budget and that actually spent;","It saves time and is easy to operate;","It permits the delegation of work in certain circumstances; and","The cost of tangible assets is written off in the year of purchase, resulting in fewer accounting entries."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages of the cash basis"},{"p":"It takes an unrealistic view of financial transactions, as only the settlement of liabilities is recognised. There are, in fact, **five stages** through which a spending decision passes:"},{"ol":["Management decision to spend money;","Issue of order or contract for the supply of goods or services;","Supply of goods or services — acknowledgment of liability;","Settlement of the amount of the good or service received; and","Consumption of value."]},{"key":"The **cash basis** records only stage (v). The **accrual basis** takes care of stages (iii), (iv) and (v). The **commitment basis** records stages (ii) through (v). This one diagram is the fastest way to answer almost any exam question that asks you to compare two of these bases — work out which stage each one starts recording from."},{"ul":["It does not provide for depreciation, since assets are written off in the year of purchase.","It does not convey an accurate picture of the financial affairs at the end of the year.","The cash basis cannot be used for economic decisions, as it tends to hide basic information, such as information relating to tangible assets, debtors and creditors.","It does not accord with the **matching concept**."]},{"h3":"Modified cash basis"},{"p":"Under this basis, the books of accounts are left open for a maximum of three months after the end of the year, so as to capture a substantial amount of income or expenses relating to the year just ended."},{"h3":"Accrual basis"},{"p":"Under this basis, revenue is recorded when earned, and expenditure acknowledged as a liability when known or when the benefit is received — notwithstanding the fact that the receipts or payments of cash have taken place wholly or partly in other accounting periods. The accrual basis is practised in the private sector and in all parastatals, such as the Central Bank of Nigeria and the Federal Inland Revenue Service. The reason for this is that private-sector concerns are profit-oriented — it is therefore necessary to estimate how much profit has been earned in each period, with a view to keeping invested assets intact and making periodic distributions to shareholders by way of dividends. However, the main consideration in the public sector is the **improvement in the standard of living of the citizens.**"},{"h4":"Advantages of the accrual basis"},{"ol":["It takes a realistic view of financial transactions;","It reveals an accurate picture of the state of financial affairs at the end of the period;","It could be used for both economic and investment decision-making, as all parameters for performance appraisal are available;","It aligns with the \"matching concept\"; and","It makes allowance for the diminution in the value of assets used to generate the revenue of the enterprise."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages of the accrual basis"},{"ol":["It is very difficult to understand, especially by non-accountants.","It does not permit easy delegation of work in certain circumstances."]},{"h3":"Modified accrual basis"},{"p":"This is the basis under which revenue is recorded when received (not earned), while expenditure is recorded once its liability is incurred. It means that the cash basis is used for recording revenue while the accrual basis is used for expenditure. The modified accrual basis operates as follows. **Revenue** is recorded when cash is received, except for: (a) revenue which is susceptible to accrual; and (b) revenue of a material amount which has not been received at the time it should have been. **Expenditure** is recorded on the accrual basis, except in the cases of: (a) disbursements for inventory items, which may be considered as expenditure at the time the items are utilised; and (b) interest on long-term debt, commonly accounted for in debt service funds and recorded as expenditure on its due date."},{"h3":"Commitment basis"},{"p":"It is a basis that records **anticipated expenditure** evidenced by a contract or a purchase order. In public sector financing, budgetary and accounting systems are closely related to the commitment basis."},{"warn":"The study text's own list of \"advantages of the commitment basis\" (and the model answer it gives to the matching end-of-chapter question) simply **repeats, word for word, four of the five listed advantages of the accrual basis** above — \"it reveals an accurate picture...\", \"it could be used for economic and investment decision-making...\", \"it aligns with the matching concept,\" and \"it makes allowance for the diminution in the value of assets.\" This cannot be right as written: the commitment basis exists specifically to record **anticipated** expenditure at the point an order or contract is raised, for budgetary control purposes — it says nothing about matching revenue to expenses, and nothing at all about asset valuation or depreciation, which is precisely the sort of remeasurement a commitment-basis (order-stage) system has not yet reached. This is a copy-paste artefact in the source, not a real property of the commitment basis. Only the genuinely distinct advantages below are commitment-basis advantages; treat the other four as belonging to the accrual basis alone, above, and not as also true here."},{"h4":"The genuine advantages of the commitment basis (kept on a memorandum vote-book basis)"},{"ol":["A separate payment tabulation is available when required.","Adjustments occurring when actual expenditure has been obtained do not affect the final accounts.","It is an aid to financial control — a commitment is regarded as a charge which has been made on a budget provision, so it prevents a spending unit from over-committing an appropriation before the cash has even moved."]},{"h4":"Disadvantages of the commitment basis"},{"ol":["The system involves extra work: actual figures have to be substituted for the commitment provisions to finally determine the running balances under the sub-heads of expenditure.","There is a risk of over-expenditure under the commitment basis, in the expectation that government may finally release funds to settle the legal obligations.","At the year end, all commitments that are the subject of unfulfilled orders will have to be written back to reflect the exact picture of the transactions which took place during the year.","Balances which ought to have lapsed in the Vote Book at the end of the year may instead be spent by issuing local purchase orders, purely to exhaust the votes before they lapse."]}]},{"n":"1.6","t":"Non-profit-oriented organisations compared with profit-oriented organisations","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis","Non-profit oriented","Profit oriented"],"rows":[["Profit","The main objective is to provide adequate welfare to the people at reasonable costs.","The main objective is to maximise profit."],["Revenue","Revenue is derived from the public in the form of taxation, fines, fees, etc.","Obtain their income principally from sales of goods and services."],["Legal formation","They are created by Acts of the Legislature, i.e. National/State Assemblies.","Registered with the Corporate Affairs Commission."],["Budget","The public is more interested in the annual budget than the annual report.","The public is more interested in the annual reports than the annual budgets."],["Budgetary approach","Public entities are rigid on the budgetary approach.","Budgets are internal affairs of these organisations."],["Accounting","They strictly operate on International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSAS), cash or accrual. They operate substantially on fund accounting.","They strictly operate on International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS). The proprietary approach is adopted for their funding."],["Annual general meeting","There is no annual general meeting, but government can hold public briefing on specific issues.","There is an annual general meeting of the shareholders to discuss the performance of these organisations."],["Dissolution","On dissolution, any income raised or asset acquired cannot be distributed to, or benefit, members or directors or officers of the organisation, because all the proceeds must revert to the state.","Income realised on dissolution can be distributed for the benefit of members, directors or officers of the organisation."]]}},{"note":"This table is the study text's own way of drawing the public/private distinction — notice it uses the labels \"non-profit oriented\" and \"profit oriented\" rather than \"public sector\" and \"private sector\" outright, because a handful of public sector bodies (Government Business Enterprises, met later in the syllabus) are run on proprietary, profit-oriented lines despite being government-owned. The table's **dissolution** row is the one examiners most often lift into a short-answer question on its own."}]},{"n":"1.7","t":"Professional pronouncements on public sector accounting","b":[{"p":"The **International Accounting Standards Committee (IASC)**, formed in 1973, was the first international standards-setting body. In 2001, IASC was re-organised to become the **International Accounting Standards Board (IASB)** — an independent international standard-setter. Professional pronouncements on public sector accounting have three objectives:"},{"ol":["To develop and harmonise public sector financial reporting, accounting and auditing practices;","To put into practice the same accounting standards throughout the world, in order to make comparisons possible and meaningful;","To make guidelines available for practitioners, in order to maintain high reporting standards."]},{"h3":"International Federation of Accountants (IFAC)"},{"p":"IFAC is the global organisation for the accountancy profession, dedicated to serving the public interest by strengthening the profession and contributing to the development of strong international economies. Founded in **1977**, IFAC comprises 173 members and associates in 129 countries and jurisdictions, including Nigeria. The mission of IFAC is to serve the public interest by: contributing to the development, adoption and implementation of high-quality international standards and guidance; contributing to the development of strong professional accountants; promoting the value of professional accountants worldwide; and speaking out on accounting public interest issues."},{"p":"In spite of the importance of public sector accounting in the economic development process, significant attention was not always given to it. The development of the private sector depends largely on activities in the public sector, and in developing countries such as Nigeria, the public sector is not only the biggest actor but also the hub of economic activities — moreover, government has the responsibility for the efficient management of the environment of commercial transactions, through the maintenance of law and order and the enactment of legislation, and political stability is essential for the growth of the economy. It is in the realisation of this, and to bridge the gap between the public and private sectors, that many international pronouncements have been made:"},{"ol":["A **United Nations survey** was conducted, and recommendations made for improvements in the government accounting systems of \"third world\" countries, especially in budgeting practices, training, data classifications, methods and accounting procedures.","In the **United States of America**, the National Committee on Government Accounting issued a manual titled *\"Government Accounting, Auditing and Financial Reporting (GAAFR)\"* — generally referred to as **\"The Blue Book.\"** The Blue Book and other pronouncements of the committee set forth basic accounting and reporting principles covering: basis of accounting; legality; funds and fund accounting; budgetary, planning and control; tangible assets and depreciation; terminologies and accounting/classifications; and financial reporting. The National Committee on Government Accounting recommended the **accrual basis** of accounting for public enterprises, capital projects and trust funds; the **modified accrual basis** for special revenue and debt service funds; and that depreciation is not chargeable on tangible assets."]},{"p":"**Other pronouncements:**"},{"ol":["The **International Federation of Accountants (IFAC)** issued International Public Sector Accounting Standards, effective from the year 2003.","The **Nigerian Public Sector Auditing Standards**, effective from December 1997, were issued by the Auditor-General for the Federation and the Auditors-General for the States.","The **American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA)** recommended that the financial statements of each governmental unit should be prepared in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, while supplementary schedules should accord with legal compliance.","The **International Organisation of Supreme Audit Institutions (INTOSAI)**, the association of all Auditors-General in the world, meets annually and draws up resolutions on accounting and audit practices and procedures to be followed by member countries."]},{"h3":"The International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board (IPSASB)"},{"p":"The IPSASB (formerly the Public Sector Committee, PSC) is a Board of IFAC formed to develop and issue, under its own authority, International Public Sector Accounting Standards (IPSASs). IPSASs are high-quality global financial reporting standards for application by public sector entities **other than** Government Business Enterprises (GBEs)."},{"p":"The main objective of the IPSASB is to serve the public interest by developing high-quality public sector financial reporting standards. IPSASs are the authoritative requirements established by the authoritative publications, including studies, research reports and occasional papers, that deal with particular public sector financial reporting issues."},{"h4":"Membership of the IPSASB"},{"p":"The IPSASB consists of **18 members**, of which 15 are drawn from IFAC member bodies, and the remaining three are public members with expertise in public sector financial reporting. The IFAC Board, on the recommendation of the IFAC Nominating Committee, appoints all members of the IPSASB, including the chair and deputy chair."},{"h4":"Due process"},{"p":"The IPSASB adopts a due process for the development of IPSASs that provides the opportunity for comments by interested parties, including IFAC member bodies, auditors, preparers (including finance ministries), standard-setters and individuals. IPSASB's due process for projects normally, but not necessarily, takes the following stages:"},{"ol":["Study of national accounting requirements and practice, and exchange of views about the issues with national standard-setters;","Consideration of pronouncements issued by: (a) the International Accounting Standards Board (IASB); (b) national standard-setters, regulatory authorities and other authoritative bodies; (c) professional accounting bodies; and (d) other organisations interested in financial reporting in the public sector;","Formation of Steering Committees (SC), Project Advisory Panels (PAPs) or subcommittees to provide input to the IPSASB on a project;","Publication of an exposure draft for public comment, usually for at least four (4) months. This provides an opportunity for those affected by IPSASB's pronouncements to comment before they are finalised and approved as an IPSAS — the exposure draft will include a basis for conclusions;","Consideration of all comments received within the comment period on discussion documents and exposure drafts, and modification of the proposed standard as considered appropriate in light of them;","Publication of an IPSAS, which includes a basis for conclusions that explains the steps in IPSASB's due process and how the Board reached its conclusions."]},{"p":"The adoption of IPSASs by government will improve both the quality and the comparability of financial information reported by public sector entities around the world. The IPSASB recognises the right of governments and national standard-setters to establish accounting standards and guidelines for financial reporting in their jurisdictions, and encourages the adoption of IPSASs and the harmonisation of national requirements with them. Financial statements should be described as complying with IPSASs only if they comply with **all** requirements of each applicable IPSAS."},{"h3":"Pan African Federation of Accountants (PAFA)"},{"p":"PAFA is the continental body representing Africa's professional accountants. Established in **May 2011**, PAFA aims to accelerate the development of the accountancy profession in Africa and strengthen the voice of the accountancy profession within Africa and worldwide. PAFA's strategic objectives, and their economic and societal benefits, are aimed at creating long-term value and contributing to the realisation of the African Union's Agenda 2063 and the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs)."},{"h4":"The Seven Aspirations of Agenda 2063"},{"ol":["A prosperous Africa based on inclusive growth and sustainable development.","An integrated continent that is politically united, based on the ideals of Pan-Africanism and the vision of the African Renaissance.","An Africa of good governance, democracy, and respect for human rights, justice and the rule of law.","A peaceful and secure Africa.","An Africa with a strong cultural identity, common heritage, values and ethics.","An Africa whose development is people-driven, relying on the potential offered by the African people, especially its women and youth, and caring for children.","An Africa that is strong, united, resilient and an influential global player and partner."]},{"h3":"African Professionalization Initiatives (API) in the public sector"},{"p":"The African Professionalization Initiative (API) is a partnership between Accountants-General, Professional Accountancy Organisations and Supreme Audit Institutions, whose core objective is to professionalise accounting and auditing in the public sector in Africa."},{"h3":"The Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria (FRCN)"},{"p":"There is established the **Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria**. The Council shall be: (i) a body corporate with perpetual succession and a common seal; and (ii) may sue and be sued in its corporate name. The Council may acquire, hold or dispose of any property, movable or immovable, for the purpose of carrying out its functions."},{"h4":"Composition"},{"p":"There is established for the Council a **Board**, which has overall control of the Council. The Board shall consist of:"},{"ol":["A Chairman who shall be a professional accountant with considerable professional experience in accounting practices;","Two representatives from the Association of National Accountants of Nigeria;","Two representatives from the Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria;","One representative from each of: the Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation; the Office of the Auditor-General for the Federation; the Central Bank of Nigeria; the Chartered Institute of Stockbrokers; the Chartered Institute of Taxation of Nigeria; the Corporate Affairs Commission; the Nigeria Revenue Service; the Federal Ministry of Commerce; the Federal Ministry of Finance; the Nigerian Accounting Association; the Nigerian Association of Chambers of Commerce, Industries, Mines and Agriculture; the Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation; the Nigerian Institute of Estate Surveyors and Valuers; the Securities and Exchange Commission; the National Insurance Commission; the Nigerian Stock Exchange; and the National Pension Commission; and","The Executive Secretary of the Council."]},{"p":"The President, on the recommendation of the Minister, shall appoint the Chairman, and the members shall be recommended by their various professional or statutory bodies to the Minister for appointment by the President. The Chairman and other members of the Board shall each hold office for a term of 4 years in the first instance, and may be re-appointed for a further term of 4 years and no more."},{"h4":"Cessation and disqualification from membership"},{"p":"A person shall not be appointed or continue to hold office as a member of the Board if: he becomes bankrupt, suspends payment or compounds with his creditors; he is convicted of a felony or any offence involving dishonesty or fraud; he becomes of unsound mind, or incapable of carrying out his duties; he is guilty of serious misconduct in relation to his duties; or, being a person who possessed professional qualifications, he is disqualified or suspended (other than at his own request) from practising his profession anywhere in the world by order of a competent authority."},{"p":"The seat of a member becomes vacant if he resigns; he becomes disqualified from membership under section 4 of the Act; he no longer holds the office by virtue of which he became a member; or he has been absent from three consecutive meetings, or three-quarters of the meetings of the Board during a financial year, without leave. A vacancy so created must be filled not later than 30 days from the date it occurs, and the replacement holds office only for the remainder of the term of the vacating member."},{"h4":"Powers of the Council"},{"p":"The Council shall have power to: enforce and approve enforcement of compliance with accounting, auditing, corporate governance and financial reporting standards in Nigeria; enter into such contracts as may be necessary or expedient for discharging its functions; borrow such sums of money, or raise such loans, as it may require to discharge its functions; co-operate with, or become a member or affiliate of, any similar international body; exercise such powers as are necessary or expedient to give effect to the provisions of the Act; require management assessment of internal controls, including information systems controls, with independent attestation; require a code of ethics for financial officers and certification of financial statements by the Chief Executive Officer and Chief Financial Officer; require entities to provide real-time disclosures on material changes in financial condition or operations; and pronounce forfeiture, by Chief Executive Officers and Chief Financial Officers, of certain bonuses received and profits realised from the sale of company shares owned by them, where the company is required to prepare an accounting restatement."},{"h4":"Functions of the Council"},{"p":"Among a longer list, the Council shall: develop and publish accounting and financial reporting standards to be observed in the preparation of financial statements of public interest entities; review, promote and enforce compliance with the standards it adopts; receive notices of non-compliance from preparers, users, other third parties or auditors; receive copies of annual reports and financial statements of public interest entities within 60 days of the Board's approval; advise the Federal Government on matters relating to accounting and financial reporting standards; maintain a register of professional accountants and other professionals engaged in the financial reporting process; monitor compliance with the reporting requirements of the adopted code of corporate governance; promote compliance with standards issued by IFAC and the IASB; monitor and promote education, research and training in accounting, auditing, financial reporting and corporate governance; conduct practice reviews of registered professionals; review financial statements and reports of public interest entities; enforce compliance with the Act and the Council's rules; establish such systems, schemes or activities, alone or with any other organisation, as needed to discharge its functions; receive copies of all qualified audit reports together with detailed explanations within 30 days of the qualification (such reports are not announced to the public until all accounting issues relating to them are resolved by the Council); adopt and keep up to date accounting and financial reporting standards, ensuring consistency with IFRS; specify, in those standards, the minimum requirements for recognition, measurement, presentation and disclosure that every public interest entity must comply with; develop or adopt and keep up-to-date auditing standards, ensuring consistency with the pronouncements of the International Auditing and Assurance Standards Board; and perform such other functions as, in the Board's opinion, are necessary or expedient for the efficient performance of the Council's functions."},{"h4":"Functions of the Board"},{"p":"The Board shall: determine broad strategies and priorities; set out the budget, secure the necessary funding and monitor expenditure; appoint the directors and other senior management staff; oversee the delivery by each directorate of its functions, through regular reports from the directorates' coordinating directors; oversee the performance of the executive through regular reports from the Chief Executive Officer; ensure that the Council and its directorates achieve high levels of accountability and transparency; undertake an annual assessment of the risks to the success of the Council's operations and oversee the necessary risk-mitigation plan; and undertake an annual evaluation of its own performance, and that of its committees and operating bodies, against its objectives, including a review of the schedule of matters reserved to the Board."},{"h4":"Objectives of the Council"},{"p":"The objectives of the Council are to: protect investors' and other stakeholders' interests; give guidance on issues relating to financial reporting and corporate governance to the bodies it regulates; ensure good corporate governance practices in the public and private sectors of the Nigerian economy; ensure the accuracy and reliability of financial reports and corporate disclosures, pursuant to the various laws and regulations currently in existence; and harmonise the activities of relevant professional and regulatory bodies as they relate to corporate governance and financial reporting."},{"key":"Do not try to memorise the FRCN material as one undifferentiated block. Organise it into the same five buckets the source itself uses — **establishment, composition, powers, functions of the Council, functions of the Board, and objectives** — and learn which bucket a given fact belongs to. An exam question naming \"objectives\" wants the five-item list above, not the much longer \"functions\" list, even though both talk about broadly similar things."}]},{"n":"1.8","t":"Fund accounting — a compact preview ahead of Chapter 2","b":[{"p":"As flagged in §1.2, this study text's own Chapter 1 does not itself define the constitutional funds — that material belongs to Chapter 2, on the constitutional and regulatory framework, where it will be treated in full alongside the sections of the 1999 Constitution that establish each one. This section exists only so that the concepts this chapter's quiz assumes are not left completely undefined until then."},{"def":{"t":"Fund","d":"a self-balancing set of accounts recording cash and other resources, together with the related liabilities and balances, segregated for the purpose of carrying out a specific activity or attaining certain objectives in accordance with special regulations or limitations."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Fund","What it is"],"rows":[["**Consolidated Revenue Fund (CRF)**","Established by section 80(1) of the 1999 Constitution. All revenues of the Federation are paid into it, except revenues payable into another fund established for a specific purpose. Withdrawals require appropriation by the National Assembly."],["**Development Fund**","Established by section 81(2). Receives transfers from the CRF and finances capital expenditure."],["**Contingencies Fund**","Established by section 83. Meets urgent and unforeseen expenditure for which no other provision exists; the amount advanced is later replaced by a supplementary appropriation."],["**Federation Account**","Established by section 162(1). All revenue collected by the Government of the Federation is paid into it, and distributed among the Federal, State and Local Governments on the basis prescribed by the National Assembly."]]}},{"warn":"The **Federation Account** and the **Consolidated Revenue Fund** are different things and are very easy to confuse. The Federation Account is the pool from which all three tiers of government are paid; the CRF is the Federal Government's **own** account, and it receives the Federal Government's share out of the Federation Account. Section 162 governs the first; section 80 governs the second."}]},{"n":"1.9","t":"Further reading","b":[{"note":"[IFAC](https://www.ifac.org) and its board the [IPSASB](https://www.ipsasb.org) publish the full text and handbook of every IPSAS, free to read, along with the IPSASB's current work plan. The [Pan African Federation of Accountants](https://pafa-af.org) has more on Agenda 2063 and its own initiatives across the continent. For Nigeria specifically, the [Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria](https://www.frcnigeria.gov.ng) is the primary source for the FRCN's current Board composition, standards and enforcement activity, and the [Institute of Chartered Accountants of Nigeria](https://icanig.org) publishes guidance and past questions relevant to this paper. If any of these links have moved, search the organisation's name directly — these sites reorganise from time to time."}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Two or three Section A marks every diet on definitions, objectives, the five bases and the professional bodies, and a Section B question in most diets — usually \"distinguish the bases of accounting\", \"state the users and their information needs\", or \"state the objectives of public sector accounting\". These are recall questions and should be scored close to in full. The five-stages-of-a-spending-decision diagram in §1.5 is the fastest way to answer almost any question comparing two bases of accounting — learn it as a diagram, not as prose.","errors":["Confusing the Federation Account (section 162) with the Consolidated Revenue Fund (section 80).","Listing users without stating what each of them needs from the report.","Describing the commitment basis as recording the same things as the accrual basis — it records orders and contracts placed (stage ii onward), not liabilities incurred (stage iii onward).","Giving the accrual basis's advantages (matching, asset valuation) as advantages of the commitment basis — the source itself makes this exact error, and it is worth being able to say why it is wrong.","Stating that the public sector has no objective, rather than that its objectives centre on accountability and service delivery rather than profit.","Giving advantages of the accrual basis when a question asks for advantages of the cash basis, or vice versa."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under section 80(1) of the 1999 Constitution, all revenues of the Federation are paid into the","o":["Federation Account","Consolidated Revenue Fund","Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Excess Crude Account"],"a":1,"w":"Section 80(1) establishes the Consolidated Revenue Fund. The Federation Account is established separately by section 162(1) and is the pool shared among the three tiers.","src":"Chapter 1.8"},{"q":"Under which basis of accounting is expenditure recognised when an order is placed, before any invoice is received?","o":["Cash basis","Commitment basis","Accrual basis","Modified cash basis","Modified accrual basis"],"a":1,"w":"The commitment basis records the obligation at the point of order so that the vote is not over-committed. It is a budgetary control device.","src":"Chapter 1.5"},{"q":"The primary objective of public sector accounting is to","o":["maximise government revenue","demonstrate accountability for public resources","compute the profit of government","value government assets","reduce the cost of governance"],"a":1,"w":"Because public money is raised compulsorily, accountability rather than profit measurement is the central purpose.","src":"Chapter 1.2"},{"q":"Which fund meets urgent and unforeseen expenditure for which no other provision exists?","o":["Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Consolidated Revenue Fund","Federation Account","Ecological Fund"],"a":1,"w":"Section 83 of the Constitution establishes the Contingencies Fund; the advance is subsequently replaced by a supplementary appropriation.","src":"Chapter 1.8"},{"q":"A disadvantage of the cash basis of accounting is that it","o":["is difficult to audit","ignores assets and liabilities","requires extensive professional judgement","is not comparable with the budget","is expensive to operate"],"a":1,"w":"Recording only receipts and payments means receivables, payables and non-current assets never appear, so the financial position is not disclosed. The other four options are in fact advantages of the cash basis.","src":"Chapter 1.5"},{"q":"Nigeria adopted the accrual basis IPSAS with effect from","o":["January 2012","January 2016","January 2014","January 2020","January 2010"],"a":1,"w":"Cash basis IPSAS was adopted from January 2014 and accrual basis IPSAS from January 2016, subject to the transitional provisions of IPSAS 33.","src":"Chapter 1.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"Distinguish between public sector accounting and private sector accounting, and explain the users of public sector financial reports and their information needs.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"The distinction"},{"p":"Public sector accounting records and reports the receipt, transfer and disposition of government funds and property. Private sector accounting reports the financial performance and position of a business. They differ in the following respects."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Basis of comparison","Public sector","Private sector"],"rows":[["Objective","Delivery of services for the welfare of citizens","Maximisation of profit and of shareholder wealth"],["Ownership","Vested in the citizens collectively; no transferable interest","Shareholders holding transferable shares"],["Funding","Taxation, statutory allocation, levies, grants and borrowing","Share capital, retained earnings and borrowing"],["Nature of contribution","Compulsory, and unrelated to the benefit an individual receives","Voluntary, and made in expectation of a return"],["Regulation","The 1999 Constitution, the Finance (Control and Management) Act, the Fiscal Responsibility Act, Financial Regulations and Treasury Circulars","CAMA 2020 and the company's own constitution"],["Reporting framework","IPSAS","IFRS"],["Accounting basis","Historically cash; now accrual under IPSAS","Accrual"],["Performance measurement","Value for money — economy, efficiency and effectiveness — which resist quantification","Profit, earnings per share, return on capital employed"],["Budget","A legal authority to spend; exceeding it is unlawful","A management plan with no legal force"],["Audit","Auditor-General for the Federation, reporting to the National Assembly","External auditors, reporting to the members"],["Continuity","Government is presumed to continue indefinitely","The entity may be wound up"]]}},{"p":"The differences all flow from the first. Because there is no profit to measure and no shareholder to satisfy, the public sector substitutes **accountability and budgetary compliance** for profitability as the test of stewardship."},{"h4":"Users and their information needs"},{"ol":["**The general public and taxpayers** — whether the taxes and levies they pay are properly collected, applied to lawful purposes and translated into services.","**The legislature** — whether the executive spent only on the purposes appropriated and within the sums appropriated; the National Assembly needs this to discharge its oversight function and to consider the following year's estimates.","**The executive and public sector managers** — cost and revenue information for planning, budgetary control, resource allocation and day-to-day operating decisions.","**Creditors, contractors and lenders** — the government's capacity to pay for goods and services supplied and to service and repay its debt.","**Investors in government securities** — the security of their investment and the certainty of interest and redemption.","**International agencies and donors** (the World Bank, the IMF, bilateral donors) — whether grants and concessionary loans were applied to the agreed purposes, and whether the overall fiscal position justifies further support.","**Employees and their unions** — the capacity of government to meet salaries, allowances, pensions and gratuities, which informs negotiation.","**Other tiers of government** — State and Local Governments need information on the Federation Account and on the basis of the statutory allocations they receive.","**Researchers, analysts and the press** — data for economic analysis, policy evaluation and public debate.","**The Auditor-General** — evidence on which to base the audit opinion and the report submitted to the legislature."]},{"note":"The distinguishing feature of the public sector user group is that most of its members cannot obtain the information any other way. A shareholder dissatisfied with a company's disclosure can sell the shares; a citizen cannot resign from the state. That asymmetry is the reason public sector reporting obligations are imposed by statute rather than left to the entity."}],"src":"Chapter 1.2–1.3"}]}},{"n":2,"t":"Regulatory and Legal Framework","brief":"The Constitution, the principal Acts, the Financial Regulations and Treasury Circulars that govern the receipt and expenditure of public money in Nigeria, and the standard-setting framework under IPSAS.","outcomes":["Cite the provisions of the 1999 Constitution governing public finance","State the purpose of the Finance (Control and Management) Act and the Audit Act","Explain the Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 and its principal requirements","Describe the status of the Financial Regulations and Treasury Circulars","Explain the role of IPSAS and of the Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria"],"secs":[{"n":"2.1","t":"The Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria 1999","b":[{"p":"The Constitution is the supreme law: any other law inconsistent with it is void to the extent of the inconsistency. Part XII of Chapter V (sections 80–89) governs federal public revenue and expenditure; sections 120–129 make parallel provision for the States; sections 162–168 govern revenue allocation."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Section","Provision"],"rows":[["**80(1)**","Establishes the **Consolidated Revenue Fund**. All revenues of the Federation are paid into it, except revenue payable into some other fund established for a specific purpose"],["**80(2)**","No money may be withdrawn from the CRF except to meet expenditure charged on the Fund by the Constitution, or where authorised by an Appropriation Act, Supplementary Appropriation Act or an Act made under section 81"],["**80(4)**","No money may be withdrawn from the CRF or any other public fund except in the manner prescribed by the National Assembly"],["**81(1)**","The President must lay the **estimates of revenue and expenditure** before the National Assembly before the start of each financial year"],["**81(2)**","Establishes the **Development Fund**, into which sums are paid for capital expenditure"],["**81(3)**","Expenditure charged directly on the CRF (statutory transfers) — including the salaries of judicial officers, the Auditor-General and members of certain commissions — does not require annual appropriation"],["**82**","Where the Appropriation Act has not been passed at the start of the year, the President may authorise withdrawals for up to **six months**, or until the Act is passed, whichever is earlier"],["**83**","Establishes the **Contingencies Fund** for urgent and unforeseen expenditure for which no other provision exists; a supplementary estimate must follow"],["**84**","Salaries of the President, Vice-President, judicial officers, the Auditor-General and the chairmen and members of specified bodies are charged on the CRF and may not be altered to their disadvantage"],["**85**","Establishes the office of **Auditor-General for the Federation** and his duty to audit the public accounts of the Federation"],["**86**","Appointment of the Auditor-General on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission, subject to Senate confirmation"],["**87**","Tenure of the Auditor-General: he may be removed only by the President acting on an address supported by two-thirds of the Senate"],["**88–89**","Powers of the National Assembly to conduct investigations and to summon persons and require evidence"],["**162(1)**","Establishes the **Federation Account**, into which all revenue collected by the Government of the Federation is paid"],["**162(3)**","The Federation Account is distributed among the Federal, State and Local Governments on terms prescribed by the National Assembly"],["**162(5)–(6)**","The State Joint Local Government Account: allocations to local government councils are paid to the States for the benefit of their councils"]]}},{"warn":"Sections 80 and 162 are the two most frequently examined and the two most frequently confused. **80 = Consolidated Revenue Fund** (the Federal Government's own fund); **162 = Federation Account** (the pool shared among the three tiers). Learn the numbers, not just the names."}]},{"n":"2.2","t":"Principal Acts","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Legislation","Year","Principal provisions"],"rows":[["**Finance (Control and Management) Act**, Cap F26 LFN 2004","1958","Regulates the management and operation of government finances. Vests supervision of the Federation's finances in the Minister of Finance, empowers him to issue instructions, and provides for the keeping of accounts and the issue of warrants"],["**Audit Act**, Cap A5 LFN 2004","1956","Governs the audit of federal public accounts by the Auditor-General; his access to books, powers of examination and duty to report to the National Assembly"],["**Fiscal Responsibility Act**","2007","Requires a Medium Term Expenditure Framework, prudent fiscal management, limits on deficits and borrowing, and the remittance of operating surpluses by scheduled corporations. Establishes the Fiscal Responsibility Commission"],["**Public Procurement Act**","2007","Establishes the Bureau of Public Procurement and the National Council on Public Procurement; prescribes open competitive bidding as the default method and sets approval thresholds"],["**Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria Act**","2011","Establishes the FRC as the national standard setter and regulator of financial reporting for public interest entities, including public sector entities"],["**Appropriation Act**","annual","Gives legal authority to spend the sums voted for the year on the purposes stated. Expenditure outside it is unlawful"],["**Allocation of Revenue (Federation Account, etc.) Act**","as amended","Prescribes the vertical and horizontal formulae for sharing the Federation Account"],["**Pension Reform Act**","2014","Establishes the Contributory Pension Scheme and regulates its administration (Chapter 4)"],["**Treasury Single Account** (Presidential directive and Financial Regulations)","2015","Consolidates all government receipts into a single account at the Central Bank, ending the proliferation of ministry accounts in commercial banks"]]}},{"h4":"The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 in more detail"},{"ul":["The Federal Government must prepare a **Medium Term Expenditure Framework (MTEF)** covering three financial years, approved by the Federal Executive Council and endorsed by the National Assembly before the estimates are laid.","The MTEF contains a macroeconomic framework, a fiscal strategy paper, an expenditure and revenue framework, and a consolidated debt statement.","The **annual budget must be derived from the MTEF**; an estimate inconsistent with it is invalid.","Aggregate expenditure may not exceed estimated aggregate revenue plus a deficit of no more than **3% of GDP**, save in exceptional circumstances.","Borrowing must be for capital expenditure and human development only, at concessional terms, and subject to a debt limit set by the National Assembly.","Scheduled corporations must prepare and submit annual budgets and remit **80% of their operating surplus** to the Consolidated Revenue Fund.","The **Fiscal Responsibility Commission** monitors and enforces compliance."]}]},{"n":"2.3","t":"Subsidiary instruments","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Instrument","Nature and force"],"rows":[["**Financial Regulations**","Issued by the Minister of Finance under the Finance (Control and Management) Act. A comprehensive code of accounting and financial procedure binding on all federal officers. The current edition is the Revised Financial Regulations 2009"],["**Treasury Circulars**","Issued by the Accountant-General or the Minister to amend, clarify or supplement the Financial Regulations, or to give effect to a policy decision. They have the force of the Regulations they amend and take effect from the date stated"],["**Financial Memoranda**","The equivalent code for Local Government, issued by the States. Commonly called the Model Financial Memoranda"],["**Establishment Circulars**","Issued by the Head of the Civil Service on personnel and emolument matters"],["**Store Regulations**","Govern the receipt, custody, issue and disposal of government stores"]]}},{"key":"The hierarchy of authority runs: **Constitution → Acts of the National Assembly → Financial Regulations → Treasury Circulars → internal instructions**. An instrument lower in the hierarchy cannot override one above it, and an officer who obeys a circular that conflicts with an Act is not thereby protected."}]},{"n":"2.4","t":"The standard-setting framework","b":[{"p":"**IPSAS** — International Public Sector Accounting Standards — are issued by the International Public Sector Accounting Standards Board (IPSASB), a board of the International Federation of Accountants (IFAC). They exist in two series: a single cash basis standard, and a suite of accrual basis standards largely converged with IFRS but adapted for the public sector."},{"ul":["Nigeria adopted **cash basis IPSAS from 1 January 2014** and **accrual basis IPSAS from 1 January 2016**, on the recommendation of the Federation Account Allocation Committee and by decision of the National Executive Council.","The **Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria**, established by the FRC Act 2011, is the national standard setter and adopts IPSAS for the public sector.","The **Federal Account Allocation Committee (FAAC)** established a Sub-Committee on roadmap implementation, which produced the National Chart of Accounts and the General Purpose Financial Statements templates (Chapter 3).","IPSAS are **not law in themselves**: they acquire force through adoption by the FRC and through Treasury Circulars directing their use."]},{"h4":"Benefits of adopting IPSAS"},{"ul":["Comparability of government financial statements across states and internationally.","Improved transparency and accountability, and hence a stronger basis for the fight against corruption.","Better information for decision-making, since assets and liabilities are recognised.","Enhanced credibility with international lenders and donor agencies.","A uniform basis of consolidation across ministries, departments and agencies."]},{"h4":"Challenges of implementation"},{"ul":["The cost and difficulty of identifying and valuing legacy assets, many of which have no reliable records.","Shortage of trained accountants familiar with accrual concepts.","Weak information systems in many entities, and the cost of upgrading them.","Resistance to change and the loss of discretion that greater transparency entails.","The need to reconcile accrual reporting with a budget still prepared and appropriated on a cash basis."]}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"One of the most heavily examined chapters in the paper. Expect two or three Section A marks on section numbers and Act titles, and a Section B question in most diets on the Fiscal Responsibility Act, the regulatory framework generally, or the benefits and challenges of IPSAS adoption. Learn the section numbers: an answer that says \"section 80 of the 1999 Constitution\" scores where \"the Constitution\" does not.","errors":["Attributing the Federation Account to section 80 or the Consolidated Revenue Fund to section 162.","Stating that IPSAS are law; they take effect through adoption by the FRC and by circular.","Confusing the Financial Regulations (federal) with the Financial Memoranda (local government).","Giving the deficit limit under the Fiscal Responsibility Act as a naira figure; it is 3% of GDP.","Saying the Auditor-General audits all government entities; parastatals are audited by external auditors appointed from a list he approves, and he receives their reports."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The Consolidated Revenue Fund is established by which section of the 1999 Constitution?","o":["Section 162(1)","Section 80(1)","Section 81(2)","Section 83(1)","Section 85(1)"],"a":1,"w":"Section 80(1) establishes the CRF. Section 162(1) establishes the Federation Account, section 81(2) the Development Fund and section 83 the Contingencies Fund.","src":"Chapter 2.1"},{"q":"Under the Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007, the aggregate expenditure deficit may not exceed","o":["1% of GDP","3% of GDP","5% of GDP","3% of aggregate revenue","10% of GDP"],"a":1,"w":"Section 12(1) caps the deficit at 3% of estimated GDP, save in exceptional circumstances.","src":"Chapter 2.2"},{"q":"Where the Appropriation Act has not been passed at the beginning of a financial year, the President may authorise withdrawals from the Consolidated Revenue Fund for a maximum period of","o":["three months","six months","one month","twelve months","nine months"],"a":1,"w":"Section 82 permits withdrawals for up to six months or until the Appropriation Act is passed, whichever is earlier.","src":"Chapter 2.1"},{"q":"Scheduled corporations are required by the Fiscal Responsibility Act to remit to the Consolidated Revenue Fund","o":["50% of operating surplus","80% of operating surplus","100% of operating surplus","25% of gross revenue","80% of gross revenue"],"a":1,"w":"The Act requires 80% of the operating surplus, computed in accordance with the Commission's template, to be paid over.","src":"Chapter 2.2"},{"q":"Financial Regulations derive their authority from the","o":["1999 Constitution directly","Finance (Control and Management) Act","Audit Act","Public Procurement Act","Financial Reporting Council Act"],"a":1,"w":"The Minister of Finance issues them under the Finance (Control and Management) Act 1958, which vests in him the supervision of the finances of the Federation.","src":"Chapter 2.3"},{"q":"Nigeria adopted the accrual basis IPSAS with effect from","o":["1 January 2014","1 January 2016","1 January 2012","1 January 2018","1 January 2020"],"a":1,"w":"Cash basis IPSAS took effect from 1 January 2014 and accrual basis IPSAS from 1 January 2016.","src":"Chapter 2.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 was enacted to promote prudent management of the nation's resources. Explain the principal provisions of the Act and assess the extent to which it has strengthened fiscal discipline in Nigeria.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"Purpose of the Act"},{"p":"The Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007 provides for prudent management of the nation's resources, ensures long-term macroeconomic stability, secures greater accountability and transparency in fiscal operations within a medium-term fiscal framework, and establishes the Fiscal Responsibility Commission to enforce these objectives."},{"h4":"Principal provisions"},{"ol":["**The Medium Term Expenditure Framework.** The Federal Government must prepare an MTEF covering the next three financial years, containing a macroeconomic framework, a fiscal strategy paper, an expenditure and revenue framework, and a consolidated debt statement. It must be approved by the Federal Executive Council and laid before the National Assembly not later than four months before the end of the financial year.","**The annual budget must derive from the MTEF.** The estimates of revenue and expenditure laid before the National Assembly must be consistent with the approved framework, and any estimate inconsistent with it is invalid. This converts the budget from an annual improvisation into the first year of a rolling medium-term plan.","**A limit on the deficit.** Aggregate expenditure may not exceed estimated aggregate revenue by more than 3% of estimated Gross Domestic Product, or such other percentage as the National Assembly may approve in exceptional circumstances such as a threat to national security.","**Control of borrowing.** Government at all tiers may borrow only for capital expenditure and human development, only on concessional terms with low interest and a reasonably long amortisation period, and only within the debt limit set by the National Assembly. A cost-benefit analysis of the project to be financed must precede the borrowing.","**Remittance of operating surpluses.** Corporations listed in the Schedule must prepare annual budgets and estimates, submit them to the Minister, and pay 80% of their operating surplus into the Consolidated Revenue Fund. This addressed the long-standing practice of agencies retaining substantial internally generated revenue.","**Budget execution and cash management.** Ministries must prepare disbursement schedules, and a cash plan is drawn up so that releases follow revenue rather than creating arrears.","**Savings from excess revenue.** Revenue in excess of the budget benchmark is to be saved rather than spent, a provision that gave rise to the Excess Crude Account and later the Sovereign Wealth Fund.","**Transparency and public access.** The Act requires publication of the budget, the MTEF and audited accounts, and gives any person the right to enforce its provisions in court after giving written notice to the Commission.","**The Fiscal Responsibility Commission.** An independent body to monitor and enforce compliance, to compel remittance of surpluses, and to investigate breaches."]},{"h4":"Assessment"},{"p":"**Where it has strengthened discipline:**"},{"ul":["Budgeting is now framed within a three-year horizon linked to explicit oil price and production benchmarks, which has reduced the purely incremental budgeting of earlier years.","The requirement to remit operating surpluses has recovered substantial sums from agencies that previously retained them, and the Commission publishes the amounts due and paid.","Borrowing is now formally tied to capital projects and to a debt limit, and the debt position is published.","Publication requirements have improved the availability of fiscal information to the legislature, analysts and the public."]},{"p":"**Where it has fallen short:**"},{"ul":["**Weak enforcement powers.** The Commission may investigate and report but has limited power to sanction. Breaches by ministries have generally attracted no penalty.","**Persistent breach of the deficit rule.** The 3% limit has been exceeded in several years, using the \"exceptional circumstances\" exemption widely.","**Incomplete adoption by the States.** The Act binds the Federal Government; States were expected to enact their own fiscal responsibility laws, and adoption has been uneven, so the framework does not cover the whole of general government.","**Late budgets.** The requirement that the MTEF precede the estimates has often been observed late, compressing legislative scrutiny.","**Incomplete remittance.** Many agencies remain outside the Schedule or dispute the computation of operating surplus, and arrears persist."]},{"p":"**Conclusion.** The Act has materially improved the *framework* of fiscal management — medium-term planning, published rules, an identified enforcement body. Its weakness is in enforcement rather than design: rules that carry no effective sanction and that admit a broad exemption are followed only when it is convenient. Strengthening the Commission's sanctioning powers, extending the Act to all tiers, and narrowing the exceptional-circumstances exemption would do more than further legislation."}],"src":"Chapter 2.2"}]}},{"n":3,"t":"Standardisation of Reporting Format","brief":"The National Chart of Accounts and its segments, the GIFMIS platform, the Treasury Single Account, and the standard templates for the General Purpose Financial Statements.","outcomes":["Explain why a uniform chart of accounts is necessary in the public sector","Describe the segments of the National Chart of Accounts","Distinguish administrative, economic, functional, programme, fund, geographical and financing segments","Explain GIFMIS and the Treasury Single Account","List the General Purpose Financial Statements required under IPSAS"],"secs":[{"n":"3.1","t":"The need for standardisation","b":[{"p":"Before the adoption of IPSAS, each ministry, state and local government maintained its own classification of receipts and payments. The consequences were that consolidation was laborious and unreliable, comparison between states was impossible, national statistics were incompatible with international standards, and the same item of expenditure carried different codes in different entities."},{"h4":"Objectives of a national chart of accounts"},{"ul":["To provide a **uniform basis of classification** for all receipts and payments across all tiers of government.","To enable **consolidation** of the accounts of the Federal, State and Local Governments into government-wide statements.","To align government reporting with the **IMF Government Finance Statistics (GFS) Manual** and the United Nations Classification of the Functions of Government (COFOG), so that national statistics are internationally comparable.","To support **budget preparation, execution and monitoring** through the same codes used for accounting, so that budget and actual can be compared directly.","To make **IPSAS-compliant financial statements** possible, since accrual accounting requires asset, liability and equity codes that a cash-based classification did not have.","To provide the coding structure required by an integrated financial management information system."]}]},{"n":"3.2","t":"The National Chart of Accounts","b":[{"p":"The National Chart of Accounts (NCOA) was developed by the Sub-Committee on the roadmap for IPSAS implementation under the Federation Account Allocation Committee. It is a multi-dimensional coding structure in which every transaction is classified simultaneously along several independent **segments**, so that the same transaction can be reported by ministry, by economic nature, by function, by programme and by location without being recorded more than once."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Segment","Question it answers","Illustration"],"rows":[["**Administrative**","Who spent it? Which ministry, department or agency?","Federal Ministry of Health → Nigeria Centre for Disease Control"],["**Economic**","What was it spent on, by nature?","Personnel cost → salaries; Other service-wide votes → electricity"],["**Functional (COFOG)**","For what purpose of government?","Health; Education; Defence; Public order and safety"],["**Programme**","Under which policy programme or project?","Universal Basic Education; Immunisation programme"],["**Fund**","From which fund?","Consolidated Revenue Fund; Development Fund; a donor fund"],["**Geographical**","Where was it spent?","State, senatorial district, local government"],["**Financing / donor**","Who financed it?","Government of Nigeria; World Bank credit; a bilateral donor"]]}},{"h4":"The economic segment"},{"p":"This is the segment used most in accounting, and it follows the structure of the financial statements:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Code series","Class"],"rows":[["**1**","Assets — cash, receivables, inventory, investments, property, plant and equipment"],["**2**","Liabilities — payables, borrowings, provisions, employee benefit obligations"],["**3**","Net assets / equity — accumulated surplus or deficit, reserves"],["**4**","Revenue — tax revenue, statutory allocation, aid and grants, other revenue"],["**5**","Expenditure — personnel cost, overhead cost, consolidated revenue fund charges, capital expenditure"]]}},{"key":"The multi-segment design is what allows one posting to serve every report. A salary payment at a rural clinic is at once **administrative** (Ministry of Health), **economic** (personnel cost), **functional** (health), **programme** (primary health care), **fund** (CRF) and **geographical** (that local government). No re-analysis is needed to produce any of those reports."}]},{"n":"3.3","t":"GIFMIS and the Treasury Single Account","b":[{"def":{"t":"GIFMIS","d":"The Government Integrated Financial Management Information System — an integrated computerised platform covering budget preparation, budget execution, commitment control, payments, accounting, cash management, payroll (through IPPIS) and reporting, built on the National Chart of Accounts."}},{"h4":"Benefits of GIFMIS"},{"ul":["A single, real-time record of government financial transactions, eliminating reconciliation between disconnected ledgers.","Automatic **commitment control**: an order cannot be raised where the vote is exhausted, which prevents the accumulation of unfunded arrears.","Faster and more reliable production of the monthly transcript and the annual accounts.","A stronger audit trail, since every transaction is time-stamped and attributed to a user.","Better cash management, because balances across government are visible.","Reduction of ghost workers when combined with the **IPPIS** payroll platform."]},{"def":{"t":"Treasury Single Account (TSA)","d":"A unified structure of government bank accounts, operated through the Central Bank of Nigeria, which gives a consolidated view of government cash resources. Implemented fully in Nigeria from September 2015, with revenue collected through the Remita platform sweeping into the TSA daily."}},{"h4":"Benefits of the TSA"},{"ul":["Consolidation of idle balances that were previously spread across thousands of accounts in commercial banks, reducing the need to borrow while holding cash.","Elimination of the interest earned privately on public funds and of the associated incentives.","A complete daily picture of government cash, which makes cash planning possible.","Blocking of unauthorised accounts and of revenue diversion.","Reduced cost of debt service, since borrowing no longer coexists with idle balances."]},{"note":"The examinable criticism of the TSA is the strain it placed on deposit money banks, which lost substantial public sector deposits, and the administrative difficulty faced by agencies with genuine operational needs for local payments. A balanced answer states both sides."}]},{"n":"3.4","t":"The General Purpose Financial Statements","b":[{"p":"The standardised templates issued with the National Chart of Accounts prescribe the form of the General Purpose Financial Statements. Under **cash basis IPSAS** the required statements are fewer than under **accrual basis IPSAS**."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cash basis IPSAS","Accrual basis IPSAS"],"rows":[["Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","Statement of Financial Performance"],["Statement of Assets and Liabilities","Statement of Financial Position"],["Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","Statement of Changes in Net Assets / Equity"],["Notes to the financial statements, including a statement of accounting policies","Statement of Cash Flows"],["—","Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts"],["—","Notes, including a statement of accounting policies"]]}},{"p":"Each statement is examined in detail later: the cash basis statements in Chapter 14 and the accrual basis statements in Chapter 16."},{"warn":"The **Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts** is required under both bases and is peculiar to the public sector. Because the budget is a legal authority rather than a management plan, comparison with it is a reporting obligation, not a management convenience. Candidates who omit it from a list of required statements lose an easy mark."}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"One or two Section A marks on the segments of the chart of accounts or on the meaning of GIFMIS, IPPIS and TSA. In Section B it appears as a discussion question — the benefits of a uniform chart of accounts, or the advantages and problems of the Treasury Single Account. Both are recall questions, and the segments should be learnt as a list of seven with an example each.","errors":["Listing only the administrative and economic segments; the chart has seven.","Confusing GIFMIS (the financial management system) with IPPIS (the payroll platform) or with the TSA (the banking arrangement).","Omitting the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts from the required statements.","Describing the functional segment as the ministry; the ministry is the administrative segment, the function is health, education, defence and so on."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"In the National Chart of Accounts, classification by ministry, department or agency is the","o":["economic segment","administrative segment","functional segment","programme segment","fund segment"],"a":1,"w":"The administrative segment answers \"who spent it\". The economic segment answers \"on what, by nature\" and the functional segment \"for what purpose of government\".","src":"Chapter 3.2"},{"q":"GIFMIS stands for","o":["Government Integrated Fiscal Management Information Service","Government Integrated Financial Management Information System","General Integrated Financial Monitoring Information System","Government Internal Financial Management and Internal Systems","Government Institutional Financial Management Information Service"],"a":1,"w":"It is the integrated platform covering budgeting, commitment control, payments, accounting and reporting, built on the National Chart of Accounts.","src":"Chapter 3.3"},{"q":"The Treasury Single Account was fully implemented in Nigeria in","o":["2012","2015","2014","2016","2011"],"a":1,"w":"Full implementation followed the presidential directive of 2015, with collections sweeping into the CBN through Remita from September of that year.","src":"Chapter 3.3"},{"q":"In the economic segment of the National Chart of Accounts, code series 4 represents","o":["Assets","Revenue","Liabilities","Expenditure","Net assets"],"a":1,"w":"The series run 1 assets, 2 liabilities, 3 net assets/equity, 4 revenue, 5 expenditure.","src":"Chapter 3.2"},{"q":"Which financial statement is required under BOTH cash basis and accrual basis IPSAS?","o":["Statement of Financial Performance","Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","Statement of Cash Flows","Statement of Changes in Net Assets","Statement of Financial Position"],"a":1,"w":"Comparison with the budget is required under both bases, because the budget is a legal authority to spend rather than an internal plan.","src":"Chapter 3.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the objectives of the National Chart of Accounts, describe its segments, and discuss the benefits which the Treasury Single Account has brought to public financial management in Nigeria, together with the problems it has created.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"Objectives of the National Chart of Accounts"},{"ol":["To provide a **uniform classification** of all government receipts and payments across the Federal, State and Local Governments, in place of the incompatible classifications each previously operated.","To make **consolidation** of the accounts of all tiers into government-wide financial statements possible.","To align government reporting with the **IMF Government Finance Statistics Manual** and the UN Classification of the Functions of Government, so that Nigerian fiscal statistics are internationally comparable.","To integrate the **budget with the accounts**, since the same codes are used for appropriation, for commitment and for recording actual expenditure, permitting direct comparison of budget and actual.","To provide the asset, liability and net-asset codes necessary for **IPSAS-compliant accrual reporting**, which a purely cash-based classification did not contain.","To supply the coding structure required by **GIFMIS**."]},{"h4":"The segments"},{"p":"The chart is multi-dimensional: each transaction carries a code in every segment simultaneously, so one posting supports every dimension of reporting."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Segment","Classifies by","Example"],"rows":[["Administrative","Ministry, department or agency incurring the transaction","Federal Ministry of Education"],["Economic","The nature of the receipt or payment","Personnel cost; overhead; capital expenditure"],["Functional","The purpose of government served","Education; health; defence"],["Programme","The policy programme or project","Universal Basic Education"],["Fund","The fund from which the money comes","Consolidated Revenue Fund; Development Fund"],["Geographical","The location of the activity","State; local government area"],["Financing / donor","The source of finance","Government of Nigeria; World Bank; bilateral donor"]]}},{"h4":"Benefits of the Treasury Single Account"},{"ol":["**Consolidation of cash.** Balances formerly held in many thousands of accounts across commercial banks are now visible in one structure at the Central Bank, so that government knows daily what cash it actually has.","**Reduced cost of borrowing.** Government previously borrowed at commercial rates while simultaneously holding large idle deposits. Netting the two has reduced debt service costs materially.","**Elimination of leakage.** Revenue collected by agencies now sweeps automatically to the TSA rather than resting in accounts under agency control, which removed both the private interest earned on public money and much of the scope for diversion.","**Better cash planning and budget execution.** A complete daily cash position permits realistic disbursement schedules rather than releases made in ignorance of the aggregate position.","**Improved accountability and audit.** Every receipt and payment passes through a traceable channel, and unauthorised accounts were closed on implementation.","**Support for the wider reform.** The TSA supplies the transaction data on which GIFMIS and IPSAS reporting depend."]},{"h4":"Problems created"},{"ol":["**Liquidity shock to the banking system.** Deposit money banks lost very large public sector deposits over a short period, which tightened liquidity, raised interbank rates and contributed to job losses in the sector.","**Operational difficulty for agencies.** Institutions with genuine day-to-day payment needs — teaching hospitals, universities, embassies, agencies operating in remote areas — found centralised payment processing slow, and delays in releases disrupted operations.","**Dependence on a single platform.** Reliance on one payment gateway and on the Central Bank's systems creates a single point of failure; downtime halts government payments nationally.","**Transaction charges and disputes** over the fees payable on collections through the platform.","**Incomplete coverage.** Some funds and some tiers of government remained outside the arrangement for a considerable period, limiting the completeness of the consolidated view."]},{"p":"**Conclusion.** The Treasury Single Account has been among the most effective of the public financial management reforms, chiefly because it removed opportunity rather than relying on exhortation: money that sweeps automatically cannot be diverted by an official who intends to divert it. The costs it imposed on the banking sector and on agency operations were real but largely transitional, and are outweighed by the improvement in control and in the cost of debt."}],"src":"Chapter 3.1–3.3"}]}},{"n":4,"t":"Pension and Gratuity","brief":"The Contributory Pension Scheme under the Pension Reform Act 2014: the rates of contribution, the roles of PenCom, the Pension Fund Administrators and Custodians, the Retirement Savings Account, and the modes of withdrawal at retirement.","outcomes":["Distinguish a defined benefit from a defined contribution scheme","State the rates and basis of contribution under the Pension Reform Act 2014","Compute an employer and employee contribution from an officer's emoluments","Describe the roles of PenCom, PFAs and PFCs","State the modes of withdrawal from a Retirement Savings Account","Identify the categories of employee exempt from the scheme"],"secs":[{"n":"4.1","t":"The two types of scheme","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Defined benefit","Defined contribution"],"rows":[["Promise made","A specified pension, usually a fraction of final salary per year of service","Whatever the accumulated contributions and investment returns will buy"],["Contributions","Whatever is needed to fund the promise; usually the employer alone","A fixed percentage of emoluments from both employer and employee"],["Investment risk","Borne by the **employer**","Borne by the **employee**"],["Longevity risk","Borne by the employer","Borne by the employee"],["Funding","Often unfunded, paid from current revenue (pay-as-you-go)","Fully funded, in an individual account"],["Portability","Poor; the benefit is tied to service with one employer","Full; the account follows the employee"],["Accounting","Requires actuarial valuation; complex","Simple — the expense is the contribution payable"]]}},{"p":"Nigeria operated a **defined benefit** scheme before 2004. It failed because it was unfunded: pensions were paid out of current budget provision, arrears accumulated into the hundreds of billions of naira, the pensioner records were unreliable and heavily populated with ghost pensioners, and there was no fund from which any claim could be met. The Pension Reform Act 2004, re-enacted with amendments as the **Pension Reform Act 2014**, replaced it with a mandatory, fully funded **defined contribution** scheme."}]},{"n":"4.2","t":"The Contributory Pension Scheme","b":[{"h4":"Scope"},{"ul":["Applies to all employees in the **public service of the Federation**, the Federal Capital Territory, the States and Local Governments, and to the **private sector** where the organisation has **15 or more employees**.","Organisations with fewer than three employees, and self-employed persons, may participate under guidelines issued by the Commission (the Micro Pension Plan)."]},{"fbox":{"h":"Rates of contribution (section 4, PRA 2014)","rows":[{"lb":"Employer, minimum","tex":"10\\% \\text{ of monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Employee, minimum","tex":"8\\% \\text{ of monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Combined minimum","tex":"18\\% \\text{ of monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Employer alone, if it so elects","tex":"\\ge 20\\% \\text{ (employee then contributes nothing)}"}]}},{"def":{"t":"Monthly emolument","d":"The total of **basic salary, housing allowance and transport allowance** — not gross pay. Other allowances are excluded unless the employer elects to include them."}},{"ul":["The employer must **deduct the employee's contribution at source** and remit both contributions to the Pension Fund Custodian within **seven working days** of paying the salary.","Late remittance attracts a penalty of not less than **2% of the unremitted amount per month**, payable to the employee's Retirement Savings Account.","The employer must in addition maintain a **group life insurance policy** for each employee of not less than **three times the annual total emolument**.","Employees may make **voluntary contributions** in addition to the statutory minimum."]},{"eg":{"t":"Computing the contributions","q":[{"p":"An officer's annual emoluments are: basic salary ₦2,400,000; housing allowance ₦600,000; transport allowance ₦300,000; utility allowance ₦180,000; meal subsidy ₦120,000. Compute the monthly employer and employee pension contributions and the total annual contribution to the officer's Retirement Savings Account."}],"a":[{"p":"Only basic salary, housing and transport enter monthly emolument. The utility allowance and meal subsidy are excluded."},{"stmt":{"t":"Annual pensionable emolument","rows":[["Basic salary",2400000],["Housing allowance",600000],["Transport allowance",300000],["Total annual emolument@tot",3300000]]}},{"tex":"\\text{Monthly emolument} = \\frac{3{,}300{,}000}{12} = ₦275{,}000"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Monthly (₦)","Annual (₦)"],"rows":[["Employer contribution at 10%","27,500","330,000"],["Employee contribution at 8%","22,000","264,000"],["**Total credited to the RSA**","**49,500**","**594,000**"]]}},{"p":"The employer's ₦330,000 is a charge in the statement of financial performance for the year; the employee's ₦264,000 is deducted from salary and is not an additional cost to the employer. The employer must also carry group life cover of at least $3 \\times ₦3{,}600{,}000 = ₦10{,}800{,}000$, taking the total annual emolument including the utility and meal allowances."},{"warn":"Applying the percentages to **gross pay** rather than to basic, housing and transport is the standard error, and it inflates the answer. Here gross pay is ₦3,600,000 and would give an employer contribution of ₦360,000 instead of ₦330,000."}]}}]},{"n":"4.3","t":"The institutional structure","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Body","Role"],"rows":[["**National Pension Commission (PenCom)**","Regulates, supervises and ensures effective administration of all pension matters. Licenses and monitors PFAs and PFCs, issues guidelines, approves investment categories, maintains a national databank of pension contributors, and receives and investigates complaints"],["**Pension Fund Administrator (PFA)**","Opens and maintains the Retirement Savings Account, **invests and manages** the pension fund, maintains books of account, provides statements to contributors at least annually, and pays retirement benefits. Licensed by PenCom with minimum share capital prescribed by the Commission"],["**Pension Fund Custodian (PFC)**","Receives contributions from employers on behalf of the PFA and **holds the pension assets** in trust. Settles transactions on the instruction of the PFA. The custodian never manages the fund and the administrator never holds the assets"],["**Employer**","Deducts and remits contributions within seven working days, maintains group life insurance, and provides records to the PFA"],["**Employee**","Opens an RSA with a PFA of his own choice, notifies the employer of the PFA and account number, and may transfer between PFAs once a year"]]}},{"key":"The **separation of the administrator from the custodian** is the central control of the scheme. The PFA decides how the money is invested but never touches it; the PFC holds the money but has no discretion over it. Neither can misapply the fund without the other, and PenCom supervises both. Explaining this separation is worth several marks in any question on the structure of the scheme."}]},{"n":"4.4","t":"Retirement and withdrawal","b":[{"h4":"When benefits become payable"},{"ul":["On attaining the age of **50 years**, or on retirement in accordance with the terms of employment, whichever is later. The general retirement age in the public service is **60 years or 35 years of service**, whichever comes first.","Judicial officers and certain other categories have longer statutory ages (65 or 70), and academic staff of universities may serve to 70.","On **medical grounds**, certified by a suitably qualified physician or a properly constituted medical board.","Where an employee **disengages before 50** and remains unemployed for four months, he may withdraw not more than **25% of the RSA balance**.","On the **death** of the employee, the balance is paid to the named beneficiaries under a will or letters of administration, together with the group life insurance proceeds."]},{"h4":"Modes of withdrawal at retirement"},{"ol":["**Programmed monthly or quarterly withdrawal**, computed by the PFA on the basis of the retiree's expected life span.","**Annuity for life** purchased from a life insurance company licensed by NAICOM, under guidelines jointly issued with PenCom, with monthly or quarterly payments.","**A lump sum** from the balance, provided that the amount left is sufficient to procure a programmed withdrawal or annuity of not less than **50% of the retiree's annual remuneration as at the date of retirement**."]},{"h4":"Exemptions from the scheme"},{"ul":["Members of the **Armed Forces**, the **Department of State Services**, the **National Intelligence Agency** and the **Nigerian Financial Intelligence Unit**.","Employees who, as at the commencement of the 2004 Act, had **three years or less to retire**.","Categories of persons covered by section 291 of the Constitution (judicial officers) in respect of the pension provided there."]},{"note":"The **minimum pension guarantee** provided by section 84 is a further feature: PenCom may, subject to guidelines, guarantee a minimum pension to a retiree who has contributed for a specified number of years. A **Pension Protection Fund** is established for the purpose, funded by an annual subvention, an annual pension protection levy on the Commission and licensed operators, and income from the fund's investments."}]},{"n":"4.5","t":"Gratuity","b":[{"p":"Under the old defined benefit arrangement, **gratuity** was a lump sum paid on retirement in addition to a monthly pension, both computed from final salary and length of service. The Contributory Pension Scheme does not provide a separate gratuity: the lump sum a retiree may take is drawn from the balance in his own Retirement Savings Account, subject to the 50% test above."},{"p":"An employer may, however, operate a **separate gratuity scheme** in addition to the statutory pension contribution, either as a contractual benefit or under a collective agreement. Where it does, the gratuity is an employee benefit accounted for under IPSAS 39 (Chapter 5), and if it is a defined benefit promise it requires actuarial valuation."},{"warn":"A question asking you to \"compute the gratuity\" is almost always set under the old defined benefit rules or under a stated scheme rule, and will give you the formula — typically a percentage of final annual salary for each year of pensionable service. Read the rule given in the question; do not import one."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Monthly emolument","tex":"\\text{Basic} + \\text{Housing} + \\text{Transport}"},{"lb":"Employer contribution","tex":"10\\% \\times \\text{Monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Employee contribution","tex":"8\\% \\times \\text{Monthly emolument}"},{"lb":"Group life cover","tex":"3 \\times \\text{Annual total emolument}"},{"lb":"Lump sum constraint","tex":"\\text{Residual balance} \\ge \\text{fund a pension of } 50\\% \\text{ of final annual remuneration}"}],"focus":"Reliably examined. One or two Section A marks on the contribution rates, the meaning of monthly emolument, or the roles of the PFA and PFC. Section B commonly asks for the features of the Contributory Pension Scheme, the reasons the old defined benefit scheme failed, or a computation of contributions from a salary schedule. The computation is easy marks provided the pensionable emolument is identified correctly.","errors":["Applying the contribution percentages to gross pay instead of to basic, housing and transport.","Giving the employer rate as 7.5% and the employee rate as 7.5%; those were the rates under the 2004 Act, superseded in 2014 by 10% and 8%.","Saying the PFA holds the pension assets; the PFC does.","Stating the remittance period as seven calendar days rather than seven working days.","Treating the group life insurance premium as part of the 18% contribution; it is separate and additional.","Forgetting that the lump sum is limited by the requirement to leave enough to fund 50% of final annual remuneration."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the Pension Reform Act 2014, the minimum contribution rates are","o":["7.5% employer and 7.5% employee","10% employer and 8% employee","8% employer and 10% employee","15% employer and 5% employee","10% employer and 10% employee"],"a":1,"w":"The 2014 Act raised the combined rate to 18%, split 10% employer and 8% employee. 7.5% each were the rates under the repealed 2004 Act.","src":"Chapter 4.2"},{"q":"For pension purposes, \"monthly emolument\" means","o":["gross monthly salary","basic salary plus housing and transport allowances","basic salary only","basic salary plus all allowances","net salary after tax and deductions"],"a":1,"w":"The Act defines it as basic salary, housing allowance and transport allowance. Other allowances are excluded unless the employer elects to include them.","src":"Chapter 4.2"},{"q":"Pension contributions must be remitted to the Pension Fund Custodian within","o":["seven calendar days of payment of salary","seven working days of payment of salary","thirty days of the end of the month","fourteen working days","the end of the following month"],"a":1,"w":"Section 11(3)(b) requires remittance within seven working days of paying the salary, with a penalty of not less than 2% per month on late remittance.","src":"Chapter 4.2"},{"q":"The body that holds pension fund assets in trust is the","o":["Pension Fund Administrator","Pension Fund Custodian","National Pension Commission","Central Bank of Nigeria","employer"],"a":1,"w":"The PFC holds the assets; the PFA manages and invests them but never holds them. That separation is the central control of the scheme.","src":"Chapter 4.3"},{"q":"An employer must maintain a group life insurance policy for each employee of not less than","o":["twice the annual total emolument","three times the annual total emolument","five times the annual basic salary","the annual total emolument","ten times the monthly emolument"],"a":1,"w":"Section 4(5) requires cover of a minimum of three times the annual total emolument, in addition to the pension contributions.","src":"Chapter 4.2"},{"q":"An employee with an annual basic salary of ₦1,800,000, housing allowance ₦450,000 and transport allowance ₦150,000 will have an annual employee pension contribution of","o":["₦144,000","₦192,000","₦240,000","₦180,000","₦216,000"],"a":1,"w":"Pensionable emolument is $1{,}800{,}000 + 450{,}000 + 150{,}000 = ₦2{,}400{,}000$; the employee contributes 8% of it.","calc":"8\\% \\times 2{,}400{,}000 = ₦192{,}000","src":"Chapter 4.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"The Pension Reform Act 2014 established a Contributory Pension Scheme in place of the defined benefit arrangement previously operated in the public service. (a) Distinguish between a defined benefit and a defined contribution pension scheme. (b) Explain why the previous defined benefit scheme failed. (c) Describe the roles of the National Pension Commission, the Pension Fund Administrator and the Pension Fund Custodian. (d) State the modes by which a retiree may withdraw benefits from a Retirement Savings Account.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) The two types of scheme"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Feature","Defined benefit","Defined contribution"],"rows":[["The promise","A stated pension, typically a fraction of final salary for each year of service","No stated pension; the benefit is whatever the accumulated fund will provide"],["Contributions","Set at whatever level is needed to fund the promise; usually the employer alone","A fixed percentage of emoluments by both employer and employee"],["Who bears investment risk","The employer, who must make good any shortfall","The employee, whose pension rises or falls with investment returns"],["Who bears longevity risk","The employer, who must pay for as long as the pensioner lives","The employee, unless an annuity is purchased"],["Funding","Frequently unfunded and paid from current revenue","Fully funded in an individual account before benefits fall due"],["Portability","Poor — the benefit is tied to service with the one employer","Complete — the Retirement Savings Account follows the employee"],["Accounting complexity","High; requires actuarial valuation and recognition of a net liability","Low; the expense is simply the contribution payable for the period"]]}},{"h4":"(b) Why the previous scheme failed"},{"ol":["**It was unfunded.** Pensions were paid out of the annual budget as they fell due, with no fund set aside. When revenue was short, pensions were simply not paid, and arrears accumulated into hundreds of billions of naira.","**The liability was never measured.** Because no actuarial valuation was performed, government did not know the size of the obligation it had incurred, and successive budgets under-provided for it.","**Records were unreliable.** There was no reliable database of pensioners. Verification exercises repeatedly disclosed large numbers of ghost pensioners, and genuine pensioners were excluded through poor record-keeping.","**Administration was weak and corrupt.** The pension administration was fragmented across ministries and agencies; several large-scale frauds were subsequently prosecuted.","**Benefits were not portable.** An employee who moved between the public and private sectors, or between agencies, frequently lost accrued entitlement.","**Payment was humiliating and unreliable.** Elderly pensioners were required to attend periodic verification exercises in person, sometimes waiting for days, and payments were months or years in arrears.","**Coverage was narrow.** Most private sector employees had no pension arrangement at all."]},{"h4":"(c) The institutional roles"},{"p":"**National Pension Commission (PenCom)** — the regulator. It licenses and supervises Pension Fund Administrators and Custodians; issues guidelines, rules and standards for the administration and investment of pension funds; approves the categories of investment and the limits on each; maintains a national databank of contributors; receives and resolves complaints; carries out routine and special examinations of operators; and imposes sanctions for breach. It does not manage or hold any pension money."},{"p":"**Pension Fund Administrator (PFA)** — the manager. It opens and maintains the Retirement Savings Account for each contributor; invests and manages the pension fund within the categories PenCom permits; maintains books of account on all transactions; provides each contributor with a statement of account at least once a year; computes and pays retirement benefits; and reports to the Commission. It gives instructions to the custodian but never holds the assets itself."},{"p":"**Pension Fund Custodian (PFC)** — the trustee of the assets. It receives contributions remitted by employers on behalf of the PFA and notifies the PFA within 24 hours; holds the pension fund assets in trust for the contributors; settles transactions and undertakes activities relating to the assets only on the instruction of the PFA; and reports to the Commission. It has no discretion over the investment of the fund."},{"p":"The separation of the three functions is deliberate and is the principal safeguard in the scheme. The administrator decides but cannot touch the money; the custodian holds the money but cannot decide; and the Commission supervises both. No single party can misapply the fund without the concurrence of another."},{"h4":"(d) Modes of withdrawal"},{"ol":["**Programmed withdrawal** — monthly or quarterly payments computed by the PFA by reference to the retiree's expected life span, drawn from the balance in the account, which continues to earn investment income.","**Annuity for life** — purchased from a life insurance company licensed by NAICOM under guidelines jointly issued by NAICOM and PenCom, providing monthly or quarterly payments for life.","**A lump sum from the balance** — provided that the amount remaining after the withdrawal is sufficient to fund a programmed withdrawal or an annuity of not less than 50% of the retiree's annual remuneration as at the date of retirement."]},{"note":"A retiree who disengages before the age of 50 and remains unemployed for four months may withdraw not more than 25% of the balance, and on the death of a contributor the balance is paid to the beneficiaries named under a will or letters of administration, together with the proceeds of the group life policy."}],"src":"Chapter 4.1–4.4"}]}},{"n":5,"t":"IPSAS 39 — Employee Benefits","brief":"The four categories of employee benefit, recognition and measurement of short-term benefits, the distinction between defined contribution and defined benefit post-employment plans, and the accounting entries for each.","outcomes":["State the objective and scope of IPSAS 39","Classify a benefit into one of the four categories","Account for short-term benefits including compensated absences and bonuses","Account for a defined contribution plan","Explain the components of defined benefit cost and where each is recognised","State the disclosures required"],"secs":[{"n":"5.1","t":"Objective and scope","b":[{"p":"**IPSAS 39, Employee Benefits**, replaced IPSAS 25 for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2018. Its objective is to prescribe the accounting and disclosure for employee benefits, requiring an entity to recognise:"},{"ul":["a **liability** when an employee has rendered service in exchange for benefits to be paid in the future; and","an **expense** when the entity consumes the economic benefit or service potential arising from the service rendered."]},{"def":{"t":"Employee benefits","d":"All forms of consideration given by an entity in exchange for service rendered by employees or for the termination of employment. They include benefits provided to employees or their dependants, and may be settled by payment in cash or in goods and services, and directly to employees, to their spouses, children or other dependants, or to others such as insurance companies."}},{"p":"The standard applies to all employee benefits **except** share-based transactions (dealt with by the relevant standard) and benefits arising from an employer's reporting on retirement benefit plans, which is a separate matter."}]},{"n":"5.2","t":"The four categories","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Category","Definition","Examples"],"rows":[["**Short-term employee benefits**","Benefits (other than termination benefits) expected to be settled wholly **within twelve months** after the end of the annual reporting period in which the service was rendered","Wages, salaries and social security contributions; paid annual leave and paid sick leave; profit-sharing and bonuses payable within twelve months; non-monetary benefits such as medical care, housing, cars and subsidised goods"],["**Post-employment benefits**","Benefits (other than termination benefits) payable **after the completion of employment**","Retirement benefits such as pensions and lump-sum payments; post-employment life insurance and medical care"],["**Other long-term employee benefits**","Benefits **not expected to be settled wholly within twelve months** after the end of the period in which the service was rendered, and which are not post-employment or termination benefits","Long-service leave or sabbatical leave; jubilee or other long-service benefits; long-term disability benefits; deferred bonuses"],["**Termination benefits**","Benefits payable as a result of either the entity's decision to terminate employment before normal retirement date, or the employee's decision to accept voluntary redundancy in exchange for those benefits","Severance pay; redundancy payments; enhanced pension on early retirement"]]}},{"key":"Classification turns on **when the benefit is expected to be settled**, not on how it is described. A bonus payable fifteen months after the year in which it was earned is an *other long-term* benefit, not a short-term one, and must be discounted."}]},{"n":"5.3","t":"Short-term employee benefits","b":[{"p":"These are the simplest: no actuarial assumptions are required, the obligation is **not discounted**, and there are no remeasurement gains or losses. The entity recognises the undiscounted amount as a liability (accrued expense) after deducting any amount already paid, and as an expense, unless another standard requires or permits it to be included in the cost of an asset such as inventory or self-constructed property."},{"h4":"Compensated absences"},{"ul":["**Accumulating** absences are carried forward and can be used in future periods if not used in full in the current period. The expected cost is recognised **as the employees render the service that increases their entitlement**.","  — *Vesting*: the employee is entitled to a cash payment for unused entitlement on leaving. The whole accumulated entitlement is provided for.","  — *Non-vesting*: no payment on leaving. Provide only for the amount expected to be used, taking account of the possibility that employees may leave.","**Non-accumulating** absences (such as sick leave that lapses, or maternity leave) do not carry forward. The cost is recognised **when the absence occurs**, not before."]},{"h4":"Bonus and profit-sharing plans"},{"p":"The expected cost is recognised when, and only when, the entity has a **present legal or constructive obligation** to make the payment as a result of past events, and a **reliable estimate** of the obligation can be made. A constructive obligation exists where past practice has created a valid expectation on the part of employees."},{"eg":{"t":"Accumulating vesting leave","q":[{"p":"A government agency employs 120 staff, each entitled to 20 working days of paid annual leave. Unused leave may be carried forward for one year, and any balance unused on leaving is paid in cash. At 31 December 2025 the average unused entitlement is 4 days per employee, and the average daily rate of pay is ₦12,500. Determine the amount to be recognised."}],"a":[{"p":"The entitlement is **accumulating and vesting**, so the full accumulated balance must be provided for regardless of whether the employees are expected to use it: it will be paid out in cash if it is not taken as leave."},{"tex":"\\text{Liability} = 120 \\text{ employees} \\times 4 \\text{ days} \\times ₦12{,}500 = ₦6{,}000{,}000"},{"p":"The entries at 31 December 2025 are:"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Employee benefits expense","6,000,000",""],["Accrued leave liability","","6,000,000"]]}},{"note":"Had the leave been **non-vesting** — lapsing unpaid on departure — the agency would provide only for the days expected actually to be taken. If, say, 15% of accumulated days were historically forfeited, the liability would be $₦6{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.85 = ₦5{,}100{,}000$."}]}}]},{"n":"5.4","t":"Post-employment benefits","b":[{"p":"The accounting depends entirely on the classification of the plan, and the classification depends on **where the risk lies**."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Defined contribution plan","Defined benefit plan"],"rows":[["Obligation of the entity","Limited to the agreed contributions. Once paid, the entity has no further obligation","To provide the agreed benefits, whatever the fund earns"],["Actuarial and investment risk","Falls on the **employee**","Falls on the **entity**"],["Measurement","Undiscounted contributions payable (discounted if not due within twelve months)","Present value of the defined benefit obligation, less the fair value of plan assets"],["Actuary required?","No","Yes — the projected unit credit method"],["Nigerian example","The Contributory Pension Scheme under the Pension Reform Act 2014","The pre-2004 public service pension scheme; some gratuity schemes"]]}},{"h4":"Defined contribution plans"},{"p":"Recognise the contribution payable in exchange for service rendered during the period as a liability, after deducting any contribution already paid, and as an expense. If the contribution already paid exceeds the amount due, recognise the excess as an asset (a prepayment) to the extent that it will lead to a refund or a reduction in future payments."},{"eg":{"t":"Defined contribution entries","q":[{"p":"A ministry's pensionable emoluments for the year are ₦480,000,000. The employer contributes 10% and the employee 8%. By the year end ₦44,000,000 of the employer contribution had been remitted. Show the amounts to be recognised."}],"a":[{"tex":"\\text{Employer contribution} = 10\\% \\times 480{,}000{,}000 = ₦48{,}000{,}000"},{"tex":"\\text{Employee contribution} = 8\\% \\times 480{,}000{,}000 = ₦38{,}400{,}000"},{"p":"The employer contribution is an expense of the period; the employee contribution is a deduction from gross pay and is not an additional expense — it is already inside the ₦480,000,000 salary cost."},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Dr (₦)","Cr (₦)"],"rows":[["Pension contribution expense","48,000,000",""],["Cash (remitted)","","44,000,000"],["Accrued pension contribution (liability)","","4,000,000"]]}},{"p":"The unremitted ₦4,000,000 is a liability at the reporting date, and if the seven-working-day limit has passed it also attracts the statutory penalty of not less than 2% per month, which is an additional expense."}]}},{"h4":"Defined benefit plans"},{"p":"These require the **projected unit credit method**: each period of service gives rise to an additional unit of benefit entitlement, and each unit is measured separately and discounted to build up the final obligation. The net liability recognised is:"},{"tex":"\\text{Net defined benefit liability} = \\text{PV of the defined benefit obligation} - \\text{Fair value of plan assets}"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Component of defined benefit cost","What it is","Recognised in"],"rows":[["**Current service cost**","The increase in the obligation from employee service in the current period","Surplus or deficit"],["**Past service cost**","The change in the obligation from a plan amendment or curtailment relating to prior service","Surplus or deficit, immediately"],["**Net interest** on the net defined benefit liability or asset","The discount rate applied to the opening net liability","Surplus or deficit"],["**Remeasurements**","Actuarial gains and losses; the return on plan assets excluding net interest; changes in the effect of the asset ceiling","**Net assets / equity** — not surplus or deficit, and never reclassified"]]}},{"warn":"The single most examinable point on defined benefit plans is that **remeasurements bypass surplus or deficit entirely** and are taken to net assets/equity, where they remain permanently. Service cost and net interest go through surplus or deficit. Getting these two the wrong way round loses the whole of the marks for that part."}]},{"n":"5.5","t":"Other long-term and termination benefits","b":[{"h4":"Other long-term employee benefits"},{"p":"Measured on the same basis as a defined benefit obligation, but with one important simplification: **all** of the cost, including remeasurements, is recognised in **surplus or deficit**. There is no separate treatment of actuarial gains and losses, because the uncertainty and the amounts involved are generally smaller."},{"h4":"Termination benefits"},{"p":"Recognised at the **earlier** of:"},{"ul":["when the entity can **no longer withdraw** the offer of the benefits; and","when the entity recognises **costs for a restructuring** that involves the payment of termination benefits."]},{"p":"Termination benefits arise from the **termination** of employment rather than from service rendered, which is why they are recognised at the point of the decision rather than spread over the service period. If they are not expected to be settled wholly within twelve months, they are discounted."},{"h4":"Principal disclosures"},{"ul":["The accounting policy for recognising actuarial gains and losses.","A general description of the type of plan.","A reconciliation of the opening and closing balances of the defined benefit obligation and of the plan assets.","The principal actuarial assumptions used, including the discount rate, expected salary increases and mortality.","A sensitivity analysis for each significant actuarial assumption.","The amount recognised as an expense for defined contribution plans.","The expected contributions to the plan for the next reporting period."]}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Net defined benefit liability","tex":"PV(\\text{obligation}) - FV(\\text{plan assets})"},{"lb":"Accumulating vesting leave","tex":"N \\times \\text{days} \\times \\text{daily rate}"},{"lb":"Defined contribution expense","tex":"\\text{Contribution rate} \\times \\text{pensionable emolument}"},{"lb":"Net interest","tex":"\\text{Discount rate} \\times \\text{opening net liability}"}],"focus":"Usually one or two Section A marks on the four categories or on which plan carries the investment risk. In Section B it appears as a short discussion of defined contribution against defined benefit, or a computation of a leave accrual or a contribution expense. The full projected unit credit computation is beyond Part II; what is examined is the principle and where each component of cost is recognised.","errors":["Classifying a benefit by its name rather than by when it is expected to be settled.","Discounting short-term benefits; they are measured at their undiscounted amount.","Providing for non-accumulating absences before they occur.","Recognising remeasurements of a defined benefit plan in surplus or deficit instead of in net assets/equity.","Treating the employee's own pension contribution as an additional expense of the employer; it is already within the salary cost.","Recognising termination benefits over the remaining service period rather than at the point the offer can no longer be withdrawn."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under IPSAS 39, benefits expected to be settled wholly within twelve months after the end of the reporting period in which the service was rendered are classified as","o":["post-employment benefits","short-term employee benefits","other long-term employee benefits","termination benefits","defined benefit obligations"],"a":1,"w":"The twelve-month test is the dividing line between short-term and other long-term benefits.","src":"Chapter 5.2"},{"q":"In a defined contribution plan, actuarial and investment risk falls on the","o":["employer","employee","pension fund custodian","government","actuary"],"a":1,"w":"The employer's obligation ends when the contribution is paid, so any shortfall in investment returns reduces the employee's eventual benefit.","src":"Chapter 5.4"},{"q":"Remeasurements of a net defined benefit liability are recognised in","o":["surplus or deficit","net assets / equity","the statement of cash flows","the notes only","surplus or deficit over the remaining service life"],"a":1,"w":"Remeasurements — actuarial gains and losses and the return on plan assets excluding net interest — go to net assets/equity and are never reclassified to surplus or deficit.","src":"Chapter 5.4"},{"q":"A ministry has 200 staff each with 5 days of accumulating vesting leave unused at the year end. The average daily rate is ₦9,000. The liability to be recognised is","o":["₦1,800,000","₦9,000,000","₦45,000","₦900,000","₦4,500,000"],"a":1,"w":"Vesting entitlement is paid in cash if not taken, so the whole accumulated balance is provided for.","calc":"200 \\times 5 \\times 9{,}000 = ₦9{,}000{,}000","src":"Chapter 5.3"},{"q":"Termination benefits are recognised at the earlier of the date the entity can no longer withdraw the offer and the date","o":["the employee accepts the offer","the entity recognises costs for a related restructuring","the payment is made","the employee leaves employment","the reporting period ends"],"a":1,"w":"IPSAS 39 sets these two dates as the recognition trigger, because the obligation arises from the decision to terminate rather than from service rendered.","src":"Chapter 5.5"},{"q":"Which of the following is an \"other long-term employee benefit\"?","o":["Paid annual leave taken within the year","Long-service leave after ten years","A pension payable on retirement","Redundancy pay on restructuring","Monthly salary"],"a":1,"w":"Long-service leave is not expected to be settled within twelve months, is not payable after employment ends, and does not arise from termination — so it falls into the residual \"other long-term\" category.","src":"Chapter 5.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the four categories of employee benefit recognised by IPSAS 39, and distinguish between the accounting treatment of a defined contribution plan and that of a defined benefit plan.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"The objective of IPSAS 39"},{"p":"IPSAS 39 requires an entity to recognise a **liability** when an employee has rendered service in exchange for benefits to be paid in the future, and an **expense** when the entity consumes the economic benefit or service potential arising from that service. The classification of the benefit determines how the liability is measured."},{"h4":"The four categories"},{"ol":["**Short-term employee benefits** — benefits, other than termination benefits, expected to be settled wholly within twelve months after the end of the reporting period in which the employees rendered the related service. They include wages and salaries, social security contributions, paid annual and sick leave, profit-sharing and bonuses payable within twelve months, and non-monetary benefits such as medical care, housing and motor vehicles. They are measured at their **undiscounted** amount, require no actuarial assumptions, and give rise to no remeasurement.","**Post-employment benefits** — benefits, other than termination benefits, payable after the completion of employment. They include pensions, retirement lump sums, post-employment life insurance and post-employment medical care. They are accounted for as either defined contribution or defined benefit plans, discussed below.","**Other long-term employee benefits** — all employee benefits other than short-term, post-employment and termination benefits. They include long-service and sabbatical leave, jubilee awards, long-term disability benefits and deferred bonuses. They are measured in the same way as a defined benefit obligation, but with the important simplification that **all** the cost, including remeasurements, is recognised in surplus or deficit.","**Termination benefits** — benefits payable as a result of either the entity's decision to terminate employment before the normal retirement date, or an employee's decision to accept voluntary redundancy in exchange for those benefits. Because they arise from termination rather than from service, they are recognised at the earlier of the date the entity can no longer withdraw the offer and the date it recognises costs for a related restructuring."]},{"h4":"Defined contribution against defined benefit"},{"p":"The distinction rests on **where the risk lies**. Under a defined contribution plan the entity pays fixed contributions into a separate fund and has **no legal or constructive obligation** to pay further amounts if the fund proves insufficient. Under a defined benefit plan the entity has undertaken to provide agreed benefits, and must make good any shortfall."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Defined contribution","Defined benefit"],"rows":[["Nature of the obligation","To pay the agreed contribution and nothing more","To provide the agreed benefit, whatever it costs"],["Actuarial and investment risk","Borne by the employee","Borne by the entity"],["Actuarial valuation","Not required","Required, using the projected unit credit method"],["Amount recognised as a liability","The contribution payable for the period, less amounts already paid","Present value of the defined benefit obligation less the fair value of plan assets"],["Expense in surplus or deficit","The contribution payable for the period","Current service cost, past service cost and net interest"],["Remeasurements","None arise","Actuarial gains and losses and the return on plan assets other than net interest, recognised in net assets/equity and never reclassified"],["Disclosure burden","Light — the expense for the period","Extensive — reconciliations, actuarial assumptions and sensitivity analysis"]]}},{"p":"**Defined contribution accounting** is straightforward. The contribution payable in exchange for service in the period is recognised as an expense and as a liability, less any amount already paid. Where the amount paid exceeds the amount due, the excess is recognised as an asset to the extent that it will give rise to a refund or a reduction in future contributions. Contributions not due within twelve months are discounted."},{"p":"**Defined benefit accounting** requires four steps: an actuary estimates the benefit attributable to current and prior periods using the projected unit credit method; that benefit is discounted to a present value; the fair value of the plan assets is deducted to give the net liability; and the total defined benefit cost is determined and split between the components recognised in surplus or deficit (current service cost, past service cost, net interest) and those recognised in net assets/equity (remeasurements)."},{"note":"In the Nigerian public service the Contributory Pension Scheme under the Pension Reform Act 2014 is a **defined contribution** plan, which is precisely why it removed the unfunded liability that destroyed the previous arrangement. The obligation of government now ends when the 10% is remitted."}],"src":"Chapter 5.2, 5.4"}]}},{"n":6,"t":"Government Budgeting","brief":"The purposes of a government budget, the budgeting techniques from incremental to zero-based, the budget cycle in Nigeria, the warrants that release money, virement, and the vote book.","outcomes":["State the purposes and functions of a government budget","Distinguish recurrent from capital expenditure, and balanced from deficit budgets","Compare incremental, zero-based, performance and planning-programming budgeting","Describe the stages of the budget cycle in Nigeria","Identify the warrants used to release funds and the purpose of each","Explain virement and the operation of the vote book"],"secs":[{"n":"6.1","t":"Nature and purposes","b":[{"def":{"t":"Government budget","d":"A financial plan of the estimated revenue and proposed expenditure of government for a stated period, usually one financial year, which on enactment by the legislature becomes the **legal authority** for the executive to collect the revenue and incur the expenditure."}},{"key":"The words **legal authority** are what distinguish a government budget from a company budget. A company that exceeds its budget has a management problem; a ministry that exceeds its appropriation has acted unlawfully, and the accounting officer is personally liable."},{"h4":"Purposes and functions"},{"ol":["**Legal authorisation.** The Appropriation Act gives the executive lawful authority to withdraw money from the Consolidated Revenue Fund and spend it on the stated purposes.","**Planning.** It translates policy into money — how much will be spent, on what, and by whom.","**Control.** Actual expenditure is compared with the appropriation, and no vote may be exceeded without further authority.","**Accountability.** It is the yardstick against which the legislature and the public judge the executive's stewardship.","**Resource allocation.** It settles the competing claims of ministries and sectors on a limited pool of revenue.","**Economic management.** Through the level and composition of spending, taxation and the deficit, government influences employment, inflation, growth and income distribution.","**Co-ordination.** It reconciles the plans of the several arms and tiers of government.","**Communication and motivation.** It informs officers of what is expected of them and the resources they will have."]}]},{"n":"6.2","t":"Classification of budgets","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Classification","Description"],"rows":[["**Recurrent budget**","Expenditure that recurs annually and does not create an asset — personnel cost (salaries, allowances, pension contributions) and overhead cost (utilities, travel, maintenance, stationery). Financed from the Consolidated Revenue Fund"],["**Capital budget**","Expenditure creating or acquiring a non-current asset — roads, buildings, plant, vehicles. Financed through the Development Fund"],["**Balanced budget**","Estimated revenue equals proposed expenditure"],["**Surplus budget**","Estimated revenue exceeds proposed expenditure. Used to restrain inflation"],["**Deficit budget**","Proposed expenditure exceeds estimated revenue; the gap is financed by borrowing. Used to stimulate a depressed economy, but limited by the Fiscal Responsibility Act to 3% of GDP"],["**Supplementary budget**","An additional appropriation during the year for expenditure not provided for, or inadequately provided for, in the main estimates"]]}},{"note":"Nigeria has run a deficit budget in most years. The Fiscal Responsibility Act requires that borrowing to finance it be applied to capital expenditure and human development, not to recurrent spending — a rule frequently cited in examination questions on the quality of the budget."}]},{"n":"6.3","t":"Budgeting techniques","b":[{"h4":"Incremental (line-item) budgeting"},{"p":"The previous year's figures are taken as the base and adjusted for inflation, price changes and known policy decisions. Only the **increment** is examined."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Simple, quick and cheap to prepare","Perpetuates existing inefficiency and waste, since the base is never questioned"],["Requires little training or data","Encourages the year-end spending rush to protect next year's base"],["Produces stability and continuity","Does not relate spending to output or to policy priorities"],["Avoids conflict, because departments are treated evenhandedly","Cannot accommodate a fundamental change of direction"]]}},{"h4":"Zero-based budgeting (ZBB)"},{"p":"Every activity starts from a base of **zero** and must be justified afresh each year as though it were being proposed for the first time. The steps are:"},{"steps":["Identify **decision units** — the lowest level at which an activity can be separately evaluated.","Prepare **decision packages** for each unit, setting out the activity, its objective, the cost, the benefits, alternative ways of performing it, and the consequences of not performing it at all.","Prepare incremental packages showing what different levels of funding would achieve — typically a minimum, a current and an enhanced level.","**Rank** all the packages across the organisation in order of priority.","Allocate the available resources down the ranking until they are exhausted; packages below the cut-off are not funded."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Advantages","Disadvantages"],"rows":[["Eliminates obsolete and wasteful activities, because nothing is assumed","Very time-consuming and expensive to operate"],["Allocates resources by priority rather than by history","Generates a large volume of paperwork"],["Forces managers to state objectives and consider alternatives","Ranking is subjective, and cross-departmental comparison is difficult"],["Improves communication and involves managers at every level","Threatening to managers, who may inflate packages defensively"],["Identifies the minimum level at which a service can be delivered","Difficult to quantify the benefits of many public services"]]}},{"h4":"Planning, Programming and Budgeting System (PPBS)"},{"p":"Resources are allocated to **programmes and objectives** rather than to departments and line items. Its steps are to define the objectives of government, identify the programmes that will achieve them, evaluate alternative programmes by cost-benefit analysis, allocate resources to the programmes selected, and monitor achievement against objectives. It looks beyond one year and cuts across departmental boundaries — a \"road safety\" programme may draw on works, police and health."},{"ul":["*Advantages:* links spending to objectives; permits comparison of alternative means to the same end; takes a multi-year view; exposes overlap between agencies.","*Disadvantages:* difficult to define public objectives in measurable terms; programmes cut across the departmental structure through which money is actually appropriated and controlled; heavy data requirements."]},{"h4":"Performance budgeting"},{"p":"Funds are allocated by reference to the **output** or level of service to be delivered — cost per kilometre of road maintained, per pupil taught, per patient treated — rather than by input. It makes efficiency visible and supports value-for-money assessment, but depends on output measures that many public services resist."},{"h4":"Rolling budgets and the MTEF"},{"p":"A **rolling budget** is continuously updated: as one period expires another is added, so a full planning horizon is always maintained. Nigeria's **Medium Term Expenditure Framework**, required by the Fiscal Responsibility Act 2007, is a three-year rolling framework from which the annual budget must be derived, supported by sector-level **Medium Term Sector Strategies (MTSS)**."}]},{"n":"6.4","t":"The budget cycle in Nigeria","b":[{"steps":["**Macroeconomic framework and MTEF.** The Budget Office, with the Ministry of Finance and the National Planning Commission, prepares the MTEF and Fiscal Strategy Paper, setting the oil price benchmark, production estimate, exchange rate and aggregate expenditure ceiling. The Federal Executive Council approves it and the National Assembly endorses it.","**Budget Call Circular.** The Budget Office issues the call circular to all ministries, departments and agencies, conveying the expenditure ceiling (\"envelope\") for each and the format, assumptions and timetable for submissions.","**MDA submissions.** Each MDA prepares its estimates within its envelope, supported by its Medium Term Sector Strategy, and submits them to the Budget Office.","**Bilateral discussions.** The Budget Office examines and defends the submissions with each MDA, and consolidates them into a draft budget.","**Executive approval.** The draft is considered and approved by the Federal Executive Council.","**Presentation to the legislature.** The President lays the estimates before a joint session of the National Assembly, under section 81(1) of the Constitution, before the start of the financial year.","**Legislative consideration.** The Appropriation Bill is read, referred to the Committees on Appropriation and to sectoral committees, which conduct budget defence sessions with the MDAs. The committees report, the bill is considered clause by clause and passed by both chambers, and any differences are harmonised by a conference committee.","**Presidential assent.** The bill becomes the **Appropriation Act**.","**Implementation.** The Minister of Finance issues warrants; the Accountant-General releases funds; MDAs incur commitments and expenditure within their votes, recording both in the vote book.","**Monitoring, evaluation and reporting.** The Budget Office monitors implementation and reports to the National Assembly; the Accountant-General prepares the annual financial statements.","**Audit and legislative review.** The Auditor-General audits the accounts and reports to the National Assembly, whose Public Accounts Committees examine the report and take evidence."]}]},{"n":"6.5","t":"Warrants","b":[{"def":{"t":"Warrant","d":"The instrument, issued by the Minister of Finance to the Accountant-General, authorising the release of funds from the Consolidated Revenue Fund or the Development Fund for expenditure already appropriated. The Appropriation Act authorises the spending; the warrant releases the cash."}},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Warrant","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Annual General Warrant (AGW)**","Issued after the Appropriation Act, authorising the Accountant-General to release funds for **recurrent** expenditure and accounting officers to incur it"],["**Provisional General Warrant (PGW)**","Issued **before** the Appropriation Act is passed, under section 82 of the Constitution, to keep essential services running. Based on the previous year's provision and limited to six months"],["**Supplementary General Warrant (Recurrent)**","Releases funds under a supplementary appropriation for recurrent expenditure"],["**Development Fund General Warrant (DFGW)**","Authorises the release of funds for **capital** expenditure out of the Development Fund"],["**Development Fund Supplementary General Warrant**","Releases additional capital funds under a supplementary appropriation"],["**Reserve Expenditure Warrant**","Releases funds that were appropriated but held in reserve pending some condition being met"],["**Virement Warrant**","Authorises the transfer of savings from one **subhead** to another within the same **head**, without changing the total appropriated"],["**Supplementary Contingencies Warrant**","Authorises expenditure from the **Contingencies Fund** for urgent and unforeseen needs; a supplementary estimate must follow"],["**Supplementary (Statutory) Expenditure Warrant**","Covers expenditure charged directly on the Consolidated Revenue Fund by the Constitution, which does not require annual appropriation"]]}},{"def":{"t":"Virement","d":"The transfer of savings from one subhead of a vote to another subhead within the same head, authorised by a virement warrant. It does not increase the total sum appropriated. Virement is **not permitted** from a capital head to a recurrent head, nor from personnel cost to any other subhead, nor to create a new subhead."}},{"warn":"The distinction examiners test is between **virement** (moving money *within* an existing appropriation, by warrant) and a **supplementary appropriation** (increasing the total, by a further Act of the National Assembly). Money cannot be moved between heads, or the total increased, by warrant alone."}]},{"n":"6.6","t":"The vote book","b":[{"p":"The **Departmental Vote Expenditure Allocation (DVEA) book**, commonly the vote book, is the record in which an accounting officer controls his appropriation. It is kept on the **commitment basis**: an amount is charged to the vote when the order is placed, not when payment is made, so that the officer always knows the uncommitted balance available."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Column","Content"],"rows":[["Date","Date of the transaction"],["Particulars / payee","Description of the goods or service and the supplier"],["Reference","Local purchase order or payment voucher number"],["Commitment (liability)","Amount of the order placed"],["Actual expenditure","Amount of the payment voucher when it is raised"],["Total charge","Cumulative commitments and expenditure to date"],["**Uncommitted balance**","Appropriation less total charge — the amount still available"]]}},{"key":"The uncommitted balance column is the whole point of the vote book. An accounting officer who places an order exceeding it commits an **over-expenditure**, for which he is personally liable to surcharge under the Financial Regulations. Recording commitments rather than payments is what prevents the accumulation of unfunded arrears."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Budget balance","tex":"\\text{Estimated revenue} - \\text{Proposed expenditure}"},{"lb":"Deficit limit (FRA 2007)","tex":"\\text{Deficit} \\le 3\\% \\times GDP"},{"lb":"Uncommitted balance","tex":"\\text{Appropriation} - (\\text{Commitments} + \\text{Actual expenditure})"},{"lb":"Budget variance","tex":"\\text{Actual} - \\text{Budget}"}],"focus":"The single most heavily examined chapter in the paper. A Section B question appears in almost every diet: the budget cycle, a comparison of incremental and zero-based budgeting, the types of warrant, or the purposes of a government budget. Section A regularly tests the names of the warrants and the meaning of virement. Learn zero-based budgeting well enough to give the five steps and four advantages and disadvantages — it is asked more often than any other single topic.","errors":["Describing virement as increasing the total appropriation; it only moves money within it.","Confusing the Provisional General Warrant (before the Act, section 82) with the Annual General Warrant (after it).","Listing zero-based budgeting steps without the ranking stage, which is where the technique actually allocates resources.","Saying the vote book is kept on the cash basis; it is kept on the commitment basis.","Omitting the MTEF stage from the budget cycle, which since 2007 begins the process.","Treating capital expenditure as payable from the Consolidated Revenue Fund; it comes from the Development Fund."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The warrant issued before the Appropriation Act has been passed, to enable essential services to continue, is the","o":["Annual General Warrant","Provisional General Warrant","Virement Warrant","Reserve Expenditure Warrant","Development Fund General Warrant"],"a":1,"w":"The PGW is issued under section 82 of the Constitution and is limited to six months or until the Appropriation Act is passed, whichever is earlier.","src":"Chapter 6.5"},{"q":"Virement is the transfer of funds","o":["from one head to another","from one subhead to another within the same head","from the Development Fund to the Consolidated Revenue Fund","from one financial year to the next","from the Contingencies Fund to a ministry"],"a":1,"w":"Virement operates within a head and does not change the total appropriated. Moving money between heads requires a supplementary appropriation.","src":"Chapter 6.5"},{"q":"In zero-based budgeting, the lowest level at which an activity can be separately evaluated is called a","o":["decision package","decision unit","cost centre","programme","vote"],"a":1,"w":"Decision units are identified first; decision packages are then prepared for each unit and ranked.","src":"Chapter 6.3"},{"q":"The vote book (DVEA) is maintained on the","o":["cash basis","commitment basis","accrual basis","modified cash basis","modified accrual basis"],"a":1,"w":"Charging the vote when the order is placed, rather than when payment is made, is what prevents over-commitment of the appropriation.","src":"Chapter 6.6"},{"q":"Capital expenditure of the Federal Government is financed from the","o":["Consolidated Revenue Fund","Development Fund","Contingencies Fund","Federation Account","Excess Crude Account"],"a":1,"w":"Section 81(2) of the Constitution establishes the Development Fund for capital expenditure; the CRF meets recurrent expenditure.","src":"Chapter 6.2"},{"q":"A principal disadvantage of incremental budgeting is that it","o":["is expensive and time-consuming to operate","perpetuates existing inefficiencies because the base is never examined","requires extensive staff training","cannot be used where inflation is high","produces too much paperwork"],"a":1,"w":"Only the increment is scrutinised, so waste embedded in the base carries forward indefinitely. The other options describe zero-based budgeting.","src":"Chapter 6.3"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a purpose of a government budget?","o":["Legal authorisation of expenditure","Determination of the profit of government","Allocation of scarce resources","Control of public expenditure","Management of the economy"],"a":1,"w":"Government does not compute a profit; its objective is service delivery, and the budget serves authorisation, planning, control, allocation and economic management.","src":"Chapter 6.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Explain zero-based budgeting and outline the steps involved in its operation. (b) State four advantages and four disadvantages of zero-based budgeting compared with incremental budgeting. (c) Describe the stages of the budget cycle in Nigeria from the preparation of the Medium Term Expenditure Framework to the audit of the accounts.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Zero-based budgeting"},{"p":"Zero-based budgeting is a method of budget preparation in which every activity begins from a base of zero and must be justified in full each year as though it were being proposed for the first time. Nothing is carried forward simply because it existed last year: a manager must show that the activity should be undertaken at all, that it should be undertaken by his unit, and that the amount requested is the right amount. It stands in direct contrast to incremental budgeting, in which the previous year's figure is accepted as the base and only the increase is examined."},{"p":"**Steps in operation:**"},{"ol":["**Identify the decision units.** These are the lowest levels of the organisation at which an activity can be separately identified, costed and evaluated — typically a section, a programme or a service.","**Prepare decision packages.** For each decision unit a package sets out the purpose of the activity, its cost, the benefits expected, alternative ways of achieving the same objective, and the consequences of not performing the activity at all.","**Prepare incremental packages.** For each activity, packages are prepared at different levels of service — commonly a minimum level below which the activity is not worth performing, the current level, and one or more enhanced levels — each showing the additional cost and the additional output.","**Rank the packages.** All packages across the organisation are placed in a single order of priority, from the most essential downwards. Ranking begins at the decision unit and is consolidated upwards through each level of management.","**Allocate resources.** The available funds are applied down the ranked list until exhausted. Packages falling below the cut-off receive no funding, and the activities they represent are discontinued or curtailed.","**Monitor and review** performance against the outputs promised in the funded packages."]},{"h4":"(b) Advantages and disadvantages"},{"p":"**Advantages over incremental budgeting**"},{"ol":["**It eliminates obsolete and wasteful activities.** Because nothing is assumed, expenditure that has continued out of habit rather than need is exposed. Incremental budgeting can carry waste forward for decades.","**It allocates resources according to current priorities** rather than historical accident, which matters most where resources are tight and policy has changed.","**It forces managers to define objectives and to consider alternatives.** The requirement to state what would happen if the activity ceased is a discipline that incremental budgeting never imposes.","**It identifies the minimum viable level of a service**, which is valuable information when cuts must be made, and improves communication because managers at every level participate in ranking."]},{"p":"**Disadvantages**"},{"ol":["**It is expensive and time-consuming.** Preparing and ranking packages for every activity annually consumes far more management time than adjusting last year's figures, and generates a large volume of paperwork.","**Ranking is subjective.** Comparing a package for school inspection against one for road maintenance requires judgements that no technique can make objective, and the outcome may reflect the persuasiveness of managers rather than the merit of the activities.","**Benefits in the public sector are hard to quantify.** Where output cannot be measured, the cost-benefit comparison at the heart of a decision package is weak.","**It is threatening to managers**, whose activities may be discontinued, which invites defensive behaviour: inflated packages, overstated consequences of non-performance, and resistance to the exercise itself."]},{"p":"In practice many governments apply zero-based budgeting **selectively** — to a rotating group of programmes each year, or to discretionary overhead spending while treating statutory and personnel costs incrementally. This captures much of the benefit at a fraction of the cost."},{"h4":"(c) The budget cycle in Nigeria"},{"ol":["**Medium Term Expenditure Framework.** The Budget Office, with the Ministry of Finance and the National Planning Commission, prepares a three-year MTEF and Fiscal Strategy Paper containing the oil price benchmark, production estimate, exchange rate, revenue projection and aggregate expenditure ceiling. It is approved by the Federal Executive Council and endorsed by the National Assembly.","**Budget Call Circular.** The Budget Office issues the call circular to all MDAs, conveying each one's expenditure envelope together with the format, the underlying assumptions and the timetable.","**Preparation of estimates by MDAs**, within their envelopes and supported by their Medium Term Sector Strategies, and submission to the Budget Office.","**Bilateral discussions.** The Budget Office examines each submission with the MDA concerned and consolidates the agreed figures into a draft budget.","**Approval by the Federal Executive Council.**","**Presentation to the National Assembly.** The President lays the estimates before a joint session under section 81(1) of the Constitution before the start of the financial year.","**Legislative consideration.** The Appropriation Bill is read and referred to the Appropriation Committees and to sectoral committees, which hold budget defence sessions with the MDAs. The committees report; the bill is considered and passed by both chambers, with a conference committee harmonising any differences.","**Presidential assent**, at which point the bill becomes the Appropriation Act and the legal authority to spend.","**Release of funds.** The Minister of Finance issues the Annual General Warrant for recurrent expenditure and the Development Fund General Warrant for capital expenditure, on the strength of which the Accountant-General releases funds to MDAs.","**Implementation and control.** Accounting officers incur commitments and expenditure within their votes, recording both in the Departmental Vote Expenditure Allocation book, so that the uncommitted balance is known at all times. Virement warrants and supplementary appropriations are obtained where necessary.","**Monitoring and reporting.** The Budget Office monitors implementation and reports periodically; the Accountant-General prepares the annual financial statements within the statutory period after the year end.","**Audit and legislative review.** The Auditor-General for the Federation audits the accounts and submits his report to the National Assembly, whose Public Accounts Committees examine it, take evidence from accounting officers and report their findings — closing the cycle of accountability that the Appropriation Act opened."]},{"note":"The cycle is a loop, not a line. The findings of the Public Accounts Committee on one year's accounts should inform the ceilings and priorities in the call circular for a later year. Where that link is broken — as it frequently is when audit reports are considered years in arrears — the accountability chain fails even though every individual stage has been performed."}],"src":"Chapter 6.3–6.4"}]}},{"n":7,"t":"Authorities and Responsibilities in Government Accounting","brief":"The officers who control public money — the Minister of Finance, the Accountant-General, the Auditor-General, accounting officers and sub-accounting officers — and the duties, powers and personal liabilities attaching to each.","outcomes":["State the functions of the Minister of Finance in relation to public finance","Describe the duties of the Accountant-General of the Federation","Describe the duties and independence of the Auditor-General for the Federation","Explain the responsibilities of an accounting officer and the sanctions for breach","Distinguish accounting officers, sub-accounting officers, revenue collectors and self-accounting units"],"secs":[{"n":"7.1","t":"The Minister of Finance","b":[{"p":"The Finance (Control and Management) Act vests in the Minister of Finance the **supervision of the finances of the Federation**, to the end that a full account may be made to the National Assembly."},{"h4":"Principal functions"},{"ul":["Supervises the finances of the Federation and ensures full accountability to the National Assembly.","Issues the **Financial Regulations** and gives instructions on the form of accounts and the procedure for receipts, payments and custody of public money.","Signs and issues **warrants** authorising the Accountant-General to release funds.","Prepares the **annual estimates** through the Budget Office, and defends them before the National Assembly.","Manages the **public debt** — domestic and external borrowing, and debt service.","Advises the President on fiscal and monetary policy and negotiates with international financial institutions.","Approves the opening and closing of government bank accounts.","Authorises the **write-off** of losses of public funds and stores within prescribed limits, and refers larger amounts to the National Assembly."]}]},{"n":"7.2","t":"The Accountant-General of the Federation","b":[{"p":"The Accountant-General is the **chief accounting officer of receipts and payments of the Government of the Federation** and the head of the Office of the Accountant-General of the Federation (OAGF), which is also the head of the Federal Treasury."},{"h4":"Duties"},{"ol":["Supervises the accounts of federal ministries, extra-ministerial departments and agencies.","Collates, prepares and publishes the **statutory financial statements of the Federation** and any other statements required by the Minister or the Accountant-General himself.","Maintains and operates the **accounts of the Consolidated Revenue Fund, the Development Fund, the Contingencies Fund** and all other public funds.","Releases funds to MDAs on the authority of warrants issued by the Minister.","Maintains and operates the **Treasury Single Account** and other government bank accounts.","Manages federal government investments and the payment of dividends and interest.","Conducts **routine and in-depth inspections** of the books of MDAs to ensure compliance with the Financial Regulations and accounting rules.","Provides financial and accounting advice to government, and issues **Treasury Circulars**.","Investigates cases of fraud, loss of funds and other financial irregularities.","Supervises and controls the Federal Pay Offices in the states.","Serves as the accounting officer for federal government treasury operations and appoints and posts treasury officers."]}]},{"n":"7.3","t":"The Auditor-General for the Federation","b":[{"p":"The office is established by **section 85** of the 1999 Constitution. The Auditor-General audits the public accounts of the Federation and of all offices and courts of the Federation, and reports to the **National Assembly**, not to the executive."},{"h4":"Duties and powers"},{"ul":["Audits and reports on the public accounts of the Federation and of all its offices and courts.","Submits his report to the National Assembly within **90 days** of receiving the Accountant-General's financial statements. The National Assembly causes it to be considered by its Public Accounts Committee.","Has **unrestricted access** at all reasonable times to all books, records, returns, reports, vouchers, cash and other government property, and may require any explanation he considers necessary.","Provides lists of auditors from which government statutory corporations and agencies appoint their external auditors, and comments on their audited accounts.","Conducts **value-for-money (performance) audits** examining economy, efficiency and effectiveness, in addition to regularity and compliance audits.","May carry out special investigations at the request of the National Assembly."]},{"h4":"Safeguards of independence"},{"ol":["His **salary and allowances are charged directly on the Consolidated Revenue Fund** (section 84), so they are not subject to annual appropriation and cannot be reduced to his disadvantage.","He may be **removed only by the President acting on an address supported by two-thirds of the Senate**, on grounds of inability to discharge the functions of the office or misconduct (section 87).","He reports to the **legislature**, not to the executive whose accounts he audits.","He is **not subject to the direction or control** of any other authority in the discharge of his functions.","He is appointed on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission subject to Senate confirmation, and a person who is not an auditor may not be appointed."]},{"warn":"A common examination trap: the Auditor-General does **not** directly audit the accounts of government statutory corporations, commissions and agencies. He provides the list of auditors from which they must appoint, comments on their audited accounts, and may conduct periodic checks — but the statutory audit is performed by the firm appointed. This limitation is regularly criticised as a gap in accountability."}]},{"n":"7.4","t":"Accounting officers","b":[{"def":{"t":"Accounting officer","d":"The officer charged with the duty of accounting for the funds voted to a ministry or extra-ministerial department — in practice the **Permanent Secretary** of a ministry or the **Head or Director-General** of an extra-ministerial office. He is **personally accountable** to the National Assembly for the funds under his control."}},{"h4":"Responsibilities"},{"ul":["Ensures that the appropriation is applied only to the purposes for which it was voted, and that the vote is not exceeded.","Ensures that an efficient system of internal control operates and that the Financial Regulations are complied with.","Ensures that all revenue due is promptly collected, receipted and paid into the appropriate account.","Ensures that all payments are properly authorised, supported by vouchers and made to the right person.","Ensures that proper books of account and records are kept and that returns are rendered punctually.","Ensures the safe custody of public money, stores and other government property.","Appears before the **Public Accounts Committee** to answer queries arising from the Auditor-General's report.","Answers audit queries within the period stipulated in the Financial Regulations.","Ensures that losses are promptly reported and investigated."]},{"h4":"Sanctions for breach"},{"p":"The Financial Regulations provide for **surcharge**: an officer who by reason of negligence or misconduct causes a loss of public funds or stores may be required to make good the loss personally. Other sanctions are interdiction or suspension, dismissal, and criminal prosecution where the conduct amounts to an offence. An accounting officer who incurs expenditure without authority, or who exceeds a vote, commits a breach for which he is personally liable."},{"key":"Personal liability is the feature that most distinguishes public sector stewardship. A company director who authorises an unbudgeted payment may be in breach of internal policy; a permanent secretary who does so may be surcharged for the whole amount out of his own pocket."}]},{"n":"7.5","t":"Other officers and units","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Officer or unit","Role"],"rows":[["**Sub-accounting officer**","An officer who receives and disburses public money and renders a **transcript** of his cash book to the Accountant-General or to his accounting officer. Federal Pay Officers and the accountants of large outstations are examples"],["**Revenue collector**","An officer authorised to collect and receipt public revenue. He must issue an official receipt for every sum collected and pay the money over intact to the sub-accounting officer or bank without delay, and may not use collections to meet expenditure"],["**Imprest holder**","An officer entrusted with a fixed sum (the imprest) to meet minor payments, retired and reimbursed on presentation of vouchers. A **standing imprest** is replenished throughout the year; a **special imprest** is issued for a particular purpose and fully retired on completion"],["**Self-accounting unit**","A ministry or department authorised to maintain its own complete set of books, prepare its own final accounts and deal directly with the Accountant-General, rather than having its accounting done centrally by the Treasury"],["**Non-self-accounting unit**","A unit whose accounting is performed on its behalf by a sub-treasury or by the ministry to which it is attached"],["**Internal auditor**","Appointed by and reporting to the accounting officer, providing continuous appraisal of the internal control system; his reports are copied to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General (Chapter 18)"],["**Board of Survey**","A body appointed to verify cash and bank balances at the year end and to examine and report on unserviceable stores (Chapter 11)"]]}},{"note":"The **transcript** rendered by a sub-accounting officer is the mechanism by which transactions at outstations reach the central accounts. It is dealt with in detail in Chapter 13, and is regularly examined in Section B as a preparation question."}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Examined in almost every diet. Section A tests the section number establishing the Auditor-General, the reporting line, and who the accounting officer of a ministry is. Section B commonly asks for the duties of the Accountant-General, the duties of the Auditor-General and the safeguards of his independence, or the responsibilities of an accounting officer. These are recall marks and should be scored in full.","errors":["Saying the Auditor-General reports to the President or the Minister of Finance; he reports to the National Assembly.","Confusing the Accountant-General (prepares the accounts) with the Auditor-General (audits them).","Naming the Minister as the accounting officer of a ministry; it is the Permanent Secretary.","Stating that the Auditor-General audits parastatals directly; he supplies the list of auditors and comments on their reports.","Omitting the 90-day period for submission of the Auditor-General's report.","Confusing a standing imprest, replenished through the year, with a special imprest, fully retired on completion."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The Auditor-General for the Federation submits his report to the","o":["President","National Assembly","Minister of Finance","Accountant-General of the Federation","Federal Executive Council"],"a":1,"w":"Reporting to the legislature rather than to the executive he audits is the core of his independence.","src":"Chapter 7.3"},{"q":"The accounting officer of a federal ministry is the","o":["Minister","Permanent Secretary","Director of Finance and Accounts","Accountant-General","Internal Auditor"],"a":1,"w":"The Permanent Secretary is personally accountable to the National Assembly for the funds voted to the ministry.","src":"Chapter 7.4"},{"q":"The Auditor-General must submit his report within how many days of receiving the financial statements?","o":["60 days","90 days","30 days","180 days","120 days"],"a":1,"w":"Section 85(5) of the Constitution requires submission within 90 days of receipt of the Accountant-General's financial statements.","src":"Chapter 7.3"},{"q":"Requiring a public officer to make good a loss of public funds caused by his negligence is known as","o":["interdiction","surcharge","virement","retirement","suspension"],"a":1,"w":"Surcharge is the personal financial liability imposed under the Financial Regulations. Interdiction and suspension are disciplinary measures, not financial ones.","src":"Chapter 7.4"},{"q":"Preparation and publication of the statutory financial statements of the Federation is the duty of the","o":["Auditor-General","Accountant-General","Minister of Finance","Budget Office of the Federation","Fiscal Responsibility Commission"],"a":1,"w":"The Accountant-General prepares them; the Auditor-General audits them and reports to the National Assembly.","src":"Chapter 7.2"},{"q":"A fixed sum entrusted to an officer to meet minor payments, replenished on presentation of vouchers, is a","o":["warrant","imprest","transcript","vote","subvention"],"a":1,"w":"An imprest may be standing (replenished throughout the year) or special (issued for a particular purpose and fully retired on completion).","src":"Chapter 7.5"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State eight duties of the Accountant-General of the Federation. (b) State the duties of the Auditor-General for the Federation and explain the constitutional provisions that safeguard his independence. (c) Explain the responsibilities of an accounting officer and the sanctions to which he is exposed for breach of them.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Duties of the Accountant-General of the Federation"},{"p":"The Accountant-General is the chief accounting officer of receipts and payments of the Government of the Federation and head of the Federal Treasury. His duties include the following."},{"ol":["**Supervising the accounts** of all federal ministries, extra-ministerial departments and agencies, and ensuring that they conform to the prescribed form.","**Preparing and publishing the statutory financial statements** of the Federation and submitting them to the Auditor-General within the statutory period after the year end.","**Maintaining and operating the public funds** — the Consolidated Revenue Fund, the Development Fund, the Contingencies Fund and all other funds of the Federation.","**Releasing funds to MDAs** on the authority of warrants issued by the Minister of Finance, and maintaining the Treasury Single Account and other government bank accounts.","**Conducting routine and in-depth inspections** of the books and records of MDAs to ensure compliance with the Financial Regulations and with accounting instructions.","**Managing federal government investments**, including the receipt of dividends and interest and the servicing of investments.","**Issuing Treasury Circulars** and providing accounting and financial advice to government.","**Investigating cases of fraud, loss of funds and financial irregularity** and reporting to the Minister.","**Supervising and controlling the Federal Pay Offices** in the states and posting treasury officers to MDAs."]},{"h4":"(b) The Auditor-General for the Federation"},{"p":"**Duties**"},{"ol":["To audit and report on the **public accounts of the Federation** and of all offices and courts of the Federation.","To submit his reports to the **National Assembly** within 90 days of receiving the Accountant-General's financial statements, for consideration by the Public Accounts Committees.","To provide the **list of auditors** from which government statutory corporations, commissions and agencies appoint their external auditors, and to comment on their audited accounts.","To conduct **regularity and compliance audits**, verifying that expenditure was properly authorised and applied to the purposes appropriated.","To conduct **value-for-money audits** examining economy, efficiency and effectiveness in the use of public resources.","To carry out **special investigations** at the request of the National Assembly.","To have **unrestricted access** at all reasonable times to all books, records, returns, vouchers, cash and government property, and to require any explanation he considers necessary."]},{"p":"**Safeguards of independence**"},{"ol":["**Security of remuneration.** Under section 84 his salary and allowances are charged directly on the Consolidated Revenue Fund. They are therefore not subject to annual appropriation by the body he may have to criticise, and may not be altered to his disadvantage during his tenure.","**Security of tenure.** Under section 87 he may be removed only by the President acting on an address supported by two-thirds majority of the Senate, and only on the ground of inability to discharge the functions of the office or of misconduct. He cannot be dismissed by the executive for producing an unwelcome report.","**Reporting line.** He reports to the National Assembly, not to the executive whose accounts he audits — the separation on which the whole value of the audit depends.","**Freedom from direction.** In the exercise of his functions he is not subject to the direction or control of any other person or authority.","**Manner of appointment.** He is appointed by the President on the recommendation of the Federal Civil Service Commission, subject to confirmation by the Senate, and a person who is not an auditor may not be appointed.","**Unrestricted access to records**, which prevents the audited body from limiting the scope of the audit."]},{"p":"Two limitations are nevertheless worth noting in a full answer. He does not directly audit government corporations and agencies, which weakens accountability over a large part of public spending; and his office depends on the executive for its operational funding and staffing notwithstanding the protection of his own salary."},{"h4":"(c) Responsibilities and liability of an accounting officer"},{"p":"The accounting officer — in a ministry, the Permanent Secretary; in an extra-ministerial department, its head — is personally accountable to the National Assembly for the funds voted to his organisation. His responsibilities are:"},{"ul":["to ensure that expenditure is applied **only to the purposes appropriated** and that no vote is exceeded;","to ensure that an **effective system of internal control** operates and that the Financial Regulations are complied with throughout the organisation;","to ensure that **all revenue due is promptly collected**, receipted and paid into the appropriate account intact;","to ensure that **all payments are properly authorised**, supported by vouchers, and made to the person entitled;","to ensure that **proper books and records** are kept and that returns are rendered punctually;","to secure the **safe custody** of public money, stores and other government property;","to **answer audit queries** within the stipulated period and to appear before the Public Accounts Committee to explain matters raised in the Auditor-General's report;","to **report and investigate losses** promptly."]},{"p":"**Sanctions.** The distinguishing feature of public sector stewardship is personal liability. Where a loss of public funds or stores arises from an officer's negligence or misconduct, he may be **surcharged** — required to make good the loss out of his own resources. He may in addition be **interdicted or suspended** pending investigation, **dismissed** from the service, and **prosecuted** where the conduct amounts to a criminal offence. Incurring expenditure without authority or in excess of a vote is itself a breach for which the accounting officer is personally answerable, whether or not any money was lost."}],"src":"Chapter 7.2–7.4"}]}},{"n":8,"t":"Vouchers and Their Classification","brief":"The types of voucher used in government accounting, the contents of a payment voucher, the registers in which vouchers are recorded, the treatment of a lost voucher, and the rules on custody and retention.","outcomes":["Define a voucher and state its purpose","Classify vouchers into payment, receipt, adjustment and journal vouchers","List the contents of a properly completed payment voucher","Describe the flow of a payment voucher through the accounts department","State the registers in which vouchers are recorded","Explain the procedure where a voucher is lost"],"secs":[{"n":"8.1","t":"Nature and purpose","b":[{"def":{"t":"Voucher","d":"A document that evidences a transaction and authorises its entry in the books of account. Every payment out of public funds and every receipt into them must be supported by a voucher, and no entry may be made in the accounts without one."}},{"h4":"Purposes"},{"ul":["To **evidence** that a transaction actually took place and that value was received.","To **authorise** the payment or the accounting entry — the voucher carries the signature of the officer empowered to approve it.","To **classify** the transaction to the correct head, subhead and economic code, so that it is charged to the right vote.","To provide the **audit trail** connecting the ledger entry to the underlying documents.","To fix **responsibility**, since the officers who prepared, checked, authorised and paid are all identified on the face of it."]}]},{"n":"8.2","t":"Classification of vouchers","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Type","Purpose","Effect on the accounts"],"rows":[["**Payment voucher (PV)**","Authorises and records a payment out of public funds — salaries, contractors, suppliers, advances","Credit cash or bank; debit the relevant expenditure or asset head"],["**Receipt voucher (RV)**","Records money received into public funds where no treasury receipt is issued to a payer — for example a transfer from another government office, or a refund","Debit cash or bank; credit the relevant revenue head"],["**Adjustment voucher (AV)**","Corrects an error of classification — a payment charged to the wrong head or subhead","Debit the correct head; credit the head wrongly charged"],["**Journal voucher (JV)**","Records transactions that do not involve the movement of cash — depreciation, accruals, transfers between funds, write-off of losses","As dictated by the entry; no cash movement"]]}},{"p":"Vouchers are further described by the stage they have reached: an **unpaid voucher** awaits payment; a **paid voucher** has been settled and is filed as evidence; an **outstanding voucher** was raised but not paid by the year end; and a **duplicate voucher** is a certified copy raised where the original is lost."},{"warn":"An **adjustment voucher** corrects a classification error only; it does not create or extinguish a liability. Using an adjustment voucher to record a genuine new transaction, or to move an over-expenditure quietly to a head with a balance, is a financial irregularity and is the sort of thing an auditor looks for."}]},{"n":"8.3","t":"Contents of a payment voucher","b":[{"p":"The Financial Regulations prescribe the particulars a payment voucher must carry. A voucher missing any of them is not a valid authority to pay."},{"ol":["The **serial number** of the voucher and the date.","The **name of the ministry, department or agency** and the station.","The **head, subhead and economic code** to which the expenditure is chargeable.","The **name and address of the payee**, and the bank account details where payment is by transfer.","The **full particulars of the service or goods** — quantity, rate, description, period covered — sufficient for the charge to be checked without reference to other documents.","The **amount in figures and in words**.","The **supporting documents** attached: local purchase order, invoice, store receipt voucher, certificate of completion, contract agreement, nominal roll.","The **certification** by the officer who checked the arithmetic and the classification.","The **certificate of the accounting officer** or officer authorised on his behalf, that the service has been performed, the rates are correct and the charge is a proper charge on the vote.","The **authority for the payment** — the warrant, contract or approval relied on.","The **payee's receipt or acknowledgement**, and the officer's certificate of payment.","The **stamp \"PAID\"** with the date, applied immediately on payment."]},{"key":"The two certificates matter most. The **certifying officer** vouches that the service was performed and the rate correct; the **authorising officer** vouches that the charge is a proper charge against the vote and that funds remain. Separating those two functions from the officer who makes the payment is the basic internal control over expenditure."}]},{"n":"8.4","t":"The flow of a payment voucher","b":[{"steps":["The **user department** raises a requisition and, where goods or services are required, a local purchase order or contract is issued after the procurement process.","On delivery, the store receives the goods and issues a **store receipt voucher**; or the work is inspected and a **certificate of completion** issued.","The **payment voucher is prepared** by the accounts section, classified to the correct head and subhead, and the supporting documents attached.","The vote book is examined and the amount **charged to the vote**; if the uncommitted balance is insufficient the voucher cannot proceed.","The voucher is **pre-audited** internally: arithmetic, classification, supporting documents, and compliance with the Financial Regulations.","The voucher is **certified and authorised** by the officers empowered to do so.","Payment is made — by mandate to the bank under the Treasury Single Account arrangement — and the voucher is **stamped \"PAID\"** and dated.","The voucher is **entered in the cash book** and posted to the ledger, and recorded in the Register of Payment Vouchers.","The paid voucher is **filed in serial order** and retained for the prescribed period, available for audit."]}]},{"n":"8.5","t":"Registers","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Register","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Register of Payment Vouchers**","Records every payment voucher in serial order — number, date, payee, amount, head and subhead. Establishes completeness and locates any voucher"],["**Register of Receipts (Revenue Collectors' Register)**","Records receipt books issued to collectors and their return, so that every receipt form is accounted for"],["**Register of Bill / Cheque Payments (Payment Mandate Register)**","Records mandates issued to the bank, so that payments can be reconciled with the bank statement"],["**Register of Losses**","Records every loss of cash or stores, the officer responsible, the investigation and its outcome"],["**Register of Unclaimed / Outstanding Vouchers**","Records vouchers passed for payment but not yet settled"],["**Vote book (DVEA)**","Records commitments and expenditure against each subhead and shows the uncommitted balance"],["**Register of Advances and Deposits**","Records advances made to officers and deposits received, and their clearance"]]}}]},{"n":"8.6","t":"Loss of a voucher and custody","b":[{"h4":"Where a voucher is lost"},{"p":"Loss of a paid voucher is a serious matter, because the evidence supporting a payment out of public funds has disappeared. The Financial Regulations prescribe the following procedure."},{"ol":["The loss is **reported immediately** to the accounting officer, who reports it to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General.","A **thorough search** is made and the circumstances of the loss investigated.","A **duplicate copy** is obtained — from the payee, from the file copy, or reconstructed from the records — and marked clearly as a **certified true copy** of the original.","The duplicate is **certified by the accounting officer**, who states that the original has been lost, that the payment was properly made, that the service was performed, and that to the best of his knowledge no double payment has occurred or will occur.","A **certificate of non-payment** is obtained where appropriate, confirming that the payment has not been made twice.","The circumstances are **recorded in the Register of Losses**, and where negligence is established the officer responsible may be **surcharged** or otherwise disciplined."]},{"h4":"Custody and retention"},{"ul":["Paid vouchers are filed in **serial order** in a secure place under the control of a named officer.","They must be produced to the internal auditor and the Auditor-General on demand and may not be released to any other person without authority.","They are retained for the period prescribed in the Financial Regulations — generally not less than **five years**, and longer where litigation, an unresolved audit query or an investigation is outstanding.","Vouchers may not be destroyed without the **written approval of the Auditor-General**.","Unused voucher and receipt forms are treated as **security documents**, held under lock and issued against signature in a register."]},{"note":"The examinable principle is that a lost voucher is never simply replaced. The duplicate is certified, the loss is reported to both the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General, and it is entered in the Register of Losses — because the possibility of a fraudulent second payment must be positively excluded."}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"One or two Section A marks most diets on the types of voucher or the contents of a payment voucher. In Section B it appears as \"state the contents of a payment voucher and the procedure where one is lost\", which is straightforward recall. Learn the contents as a numbered list of about ten and the loss procedure as six steps.","errors":["Confusing an adjustment voucher (corrects a misclassification) with a journal voucher (records a non-cash transaction).","Omitting the head, subhead and economic code from the contents of a payment voucher — the classification is the point of the document.","Saying a lost voucher is simply replaced by a photocopy, without certification or report.","Failing to mention that destruction of vouchers requires the Auditor-General's written approval.","Listing only payment and receipt vouchers when four types are examinable."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"A voucher used to correct an expenditure charged to the wrong head or subhead is a","o":["payment voucher","adjustment voucher","receipt voucher","journal voucher","duplicate voucher"],"a":1,"w":"The adjustment voucher moves the charge from the head wrongly debited to the correct one. It does not create or discharge a liability.","src":"Chapter 8.2"},{"q":"Vouchers may not be destroyed without the written approval of the","o":["Accountant-General","Auditor-General","Minister of Finance","accounting officer","internal auditor"],"a":1,"w":"Destruction would remove audit evidence, so it requires the approval of the officer who must rely on it.","src":"Chapter 8.6"},{"q":"A voucher recording a transaction that does not involve any movement of cash — such as depreciation or a transfer between funds — is a","o":["receipt voucher","journal voucher","adjustment voucher","payment voucher","store receipt voucher"],"a":1,"w":"The journal voucher records non-cash entries; the adjustment voucher is the special case of correcting a misclassification.","src":"Chapter 8.2"},{"q":"Immediately a payment voucher is settled it should be","o":["filed unmarked","stamped \"PAID\" and dated","returned to the payee","entered in the Register of Losses","sent to the Auditor-General"],"a":1,"w":"Stamping prevents the same voucher being presented a second time for payment.","src":"Chapter 8.4"},{"q":"Where a paid voucher is lost, the duplicate obtained must be certified by the","o":["payee","accounting officer","internal auditor","Auditor-General","Accountant-General"],"a":1,"w":"The accounting officer certifies that the original is lost, that the payment was properly made and that no double payment has occurred, and reports the loss to both the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General.","src":"Chapter 8.6"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Define a voucher and state five purposes it serves in government accounting. (b) List ten items that a properly completed payment voucher should contain. (c) Describe the procedure to be followed where a paid voucher is lost.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Definition and purposes"},{"p":"A voucher is a document evidencing a transaction and authorising its entry in the books of account. Every payment out of public funds and every receipt into them must be supported by a voucher, and no entry may be made in the accounts without one."},{"p":"**Purposes**"},{"ol":["**Evidence.** It proves that the transaction took place and that value was received for the money paid.","**Authorisation.** It carries the signature of the officer empowered to approve the payment, so that no money leaves the treasury without proper authority.","**Classification.** It states the head, subhead and economic code to which the transaction is chargeable, ensuring the correct vote is charged and the accounts correctly analysed.","**Audit trail.** It links the entry in the cash book and ledger to the underlying invoice, order, store receipt and certificate of completion, so that an auditor can trace a figure to its source.","**Fixing responsibility.** The officers who prepared, checked, certified, authorised and paid are each identified on the face of the voucher, so that liability can be established if the payment proves improper.","**Budgetary control.** The voucher is the instrument by which the charge reaches the vote book, where it reduces the uncommitted balance."]},{"h4":"(b) Contents of a payment voucher"},{"ol":["Serial number and date of the voucher.","Name of the ministry, department or agency, and the station.","The head, subhead and economic code to which the expenditure is chargeable.","Name and address of the payee, with bank account details where payment is by transfer.","Full particulars of the goods or services — description, quantity, rate and period covered — sufficient for the charge to be verified without reference to other documents.","The amount, in both figures and words.","A list of the supporting documents attached: local purchase order, invoice, store receipt voucher, certificate of job completion, contract agreement or nominal roll.","The certificate of the checking officer as to arithmetical accuracy and correct classification.","The certificate of the accounting officer, or an officer authorised on his behalf, that the service has been performed, the rates are correct and the charge is a proper charge on the vote.","The authority for the payment — warrant, contract, approval or regulation relied upon.","The payee's receipt or acknowledgement of the money.","The \"PAID\" stamp with the date of payment."]},{"h4":"(c) Procedure on loss of a paid voucher"},{"p":"The loss of a paid voucher removes the evidence supporting a payment already made out of public funds, and creates the risk that the same claim may be presented again. The Financial Regulations therefore prescribe a formal procedure."},{"ol":["**Immediate report.** The officer discovering the loss reports it at once to the accounting officer, who in turn reports it to the Accountant-General of the Federation and to the Auditor-General for the Federation.","**Search and investigation.** A thorough search is conducted and the circumstances of the loss investigated, to establish whether it arose from negligence, from a defect in the filing system, or from an attempt to conceal an irregularity.","**Obtain a duplicate.** A duplicate is obtained from the payee, from the departmental file copy, or reconstructed from the cash book, vote book and supporting documents. It is marked clearly as a certified true copy of the original.","**Certification.** The accounting officer certifies on the duplicate that the original has been lost, that the payment was properly made and the service performed, that the charge was correctly classified, and that to the best of his knowledge and belief no double payment has been or will be made.","**Certificate of non-payment.** Where appropriate, confirmation is obtained from the payee and from the bank that the sum has been received once only.","**Record and sanction.** The loss is recorded in the Register of Losses with full particulars. Where negligence on the part of an officer is established, he may be surcharged with any resulting loss and is liable to disciplinary action; where fraud is suspected the matter is referred for investigation and possible prosecution."]},{"note":"The reason for this weight of procedure is that a missing voucher is the classic cover for a fraudulent duplicate payment. The requirement to report to the Auditor-General ensures the matter is examined by someone outside the department in which the loss occurred."}],"src":"Chapter 8.3, 8.6"}]}},{"n":9,"t":"Sources of Government Revenue","brief":"Tax and non-tax revenue, the Federation Account and the principles on which it is shared, the derivation principle, the distribution of value added tax, and internally generated revenue at state and local level.","outcomes":["Distinguish tax from non-tax revenue and give examples of each","Distinguish statutory allocation from internally generated revenue","Explain the operation of the Federation Account","State the vertical and horizontal principles of revenue allocation","Explain the 13% derivation principle and the distribution of VAT","Suggest ways of improving internally generated revenue"],"secs":[{"n":"9.1","t":"Classification of government revenue","b":[{"h4":"Tax revenue"},{"p":"A **tax** is a compulsory levy imposed by government on individuals and organisations, for which no direct or proportionate benefit is received in return."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Tax","Collected by","Basis"],"rows":[["**Petroleum Profits Tax / Hydrocarbon Tax**","FIRS","Profits of companies engaged in petroleum operations"],["**Companies Income Tax**","FIRS","Profits of registered companies"],["**Personal Income Tax**","State Boards of Internal Revenue (FIRS for the Armed Forces, police, Foreign Affairs and FCT residents)","Income of individuals, by residence"],["**Value Added Tax**","FIRS","Consumption of goods and services"],["**Customs and Excise Duties**","Nigeria Customs Service","Imports, exports and excisable goods manufactured locally"],["**Capital Gains Tax**","FIRS / State Boards","Gains on disposal of chargeable assets"],["**Stamp Duties**","FIRS / State Boards","Instruments and certain transactions"],["**Education Tax (TETFund levy)**","FIRS","Assessable profits of companies"]]}},{"h4":"Non-tax revenue"},{"ul":["**Royalties** on crude oil, gas and solid minerals.","**Licences and fees** — drivers' licences, business premises registration, radio and television licences, court fees.","**Fines and penalties** imposed by courts and regulators.","**Earnings and sales** — proceeds of government commercial activities, hospital and school fees, sale of government publications and scrap.","**Rent** on government property and land.","**Interest and repayments** on loans granted by government.","**Dividends and operating surpluses** from government investments and from scheduled corporations under the Fiscal Responsibility Act.","**Grants and aid** from foreign governments and international agencies.","**Borrowing** — internal (treasury bills, bonds) and external — which is a source of **financing** rather than revenue proper, since it must be repaid."]},{"warn":"Borrowing is often listed by candidates as a source of revenue. It is more precisely a source of **financing**: it creates a liability and does not increase net assets. Saying so explicitly is worth a mark in a question on classification."},{"h4":"By tier of government"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Nature"],"rows":[["**Statutory allocation**","The share of the Federation Account distributed to a tier of government under the Allocation of Revenue Act"],["**Internally generated revenue (IGR)**","Revenue a state or local government raises within its own jurisdiction — personal income tax, licences, fees, fines, rates, rents, earnings"],["**Value added tax allocation**","The share of the VAT pool distributed to the tier"],["**Grants**","From a higher tier or from a donor, often for a specified purpose (matching grants for capital projects)"],["**Excess crude / augmentation**","Distributions from savings of revenue above the budget benchmark"]]}}]},{"n":"9.2","t":"The Federation Account","b":[{"p":"Section 162(1) of the 1999 Constitution provides that the Federation shall maintain a special account called **the Federation Account** into which shall be paid all revenue collected by the Government of the Federation, with the exception of the proceeds of personal income tax of the personnel of the Armed Forces, the Nigeria Police Force, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and residents of the Federal Capital Territory."},{"h4":"Operation"},{"ol":["Revenue-collecting agencies — the Federal Inland Revenue Service, the Nigeria Customs Service, the Nigerian Upstream Petroleum Regulatory Commission and NNPC — pay collections into the Federation Account.","First-line charges are deducted: the cost of collection of the collecting agencies, and certain other statutory deductions.","The **Federation Account Allocation Committee (FAAC)** meets monthly, reviews the inflows, and determines the amount available for distribution.","The balance is distributed among the three tiers on the **vertical** formula, and among the states and among the local governments on the **horizontal** formula.","The Federal share is paid into the **Consolidated Revenue Fund**; each state's share into its own consolidated revenue fund; and the local government share into the **State Joint Local Government Account** for distribution to the councils."]},{"h4":"Vertical allocation (sharing between tiers)"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tier","Share of the Federation Account"],"rows":[["Federal Government","52.68%"],["State Governments","26.72%"],["Local Governments","20.60%"],["Total","100.00%"]]}},{"p":"These are the percentages in operation following the Supreme Court judgment of 2002 and the subsequent modification orders. The formula is subject to periodic review by the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC), whose recommendations require an Act of the National Assembly."},{"h4":"Horizontal allocation (sharing among states, and among local governments)"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Principle","Weight"],"rows":[["Equality of states","40%"],["Population","30%"],["Internal revenue generation effort","10%"],["Land mass and terrain","10%"],["Social development factor (education, health, water)","10%"],["Total","100%"]]}},{"key":"The **derivation principle** operates separately and before the horizontal formula. Section 162(2) requires that not less than **13% of the revenue accruing to the Federation Account directly from any natural resource** be paid to the state from which it was derived, as a first charge. Only the balance goes into the pool shared on the formula above."}]},{"n":"9.3","t":"Value added tax","b":[{"p":"VAT proceeds are not paid into the Federation Account but into a **separate VAT Pool Account**, and are distributed on a different formula."},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["Tier","Share of the VAT pool"],"rows":[["Federal Government","15%"],["State Governments","50%"],["Local Governments","35%"],["Total","100%"]]}},{"p":"The state and local government shares are then distributed among them on the basis of equality (50%), population (30%) and derivation (20%)."},{"note":"The distribution of VAT has been the subject of litigation between the Federal Government and several states, which have argued that consumption tax is a residual matter within the legislative competence of the states. Candidates need only know that the dispute exists and that the formula above is the one currently operated."}]},{"n":"9.4","t":"Internally generated revenue","b":[{"p":"Heavy dependence on statutory allocation, and therefore on the oil price, is the principal structural weakness of state and local government finance in Nigeria. Improving internally generated revenue is a standard Section B question."},{"h4":"Problems of internally generated revenue"},{"ul":["A **narrow revenue base**, with much economic activity in the informal sector and outside any register.","**Poor records** — no reliable database of taxpayers, properties, vehicles or businesses.","**Leakage and diversion**, where collections are received in cash by officials and never reach the treasury.","**Multiple taxation** by overlapping authorities, which discourages compliance and drives businesses out of the formal sector.","**Weak enforcement** and reluctance to prosecute defaulters, particularly where they are politically connected.","**Public resistance**, arising from the perception that taxes are not translated into services.","**Untrained and poorly motivated staff**, and inadequate revenue offices and equipment."]},{"h4":"Ways of improving internally generated revenue"},{"ol":["**Broaden the base** by identifying and registering informal-sector operators, presumptive taxation of small businesses, and enumeration of properties for tenement rating.","**Automate collection** — electronic payment platforms, a single revenue account, and the elimination of cash handling by collectors. This is the single most effective measure, because it removes the opportunity rather than relying on integrity.","**Build a taxpayer database** and integrate it with other registers — the corporate registry, land registry, vehicle registration and the national identity number.","**Harmonise levies** to eliminate multiple taxation, publish an approved list of taxes and levies, and prohibit collection outside it.","**Strengthen enforcement** with adequate legal powers, tax tribunals, and the publication of the names of persistent defaulters.","**Train and motivate revenue staff**, with performance-based incentives tied to collection rather than to assessment.","**Improve taxpayer education and service delivery**, since compliance rises when taxpayers can see what the money purchases.","**Reform the revenue authority** into an autonomous, professionally managed board, as the states with the highest IGR have done.","**Block leakages** through internal audit, revenue monitoring and reconciliation between assessments raised and amounts collected."]}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Derivation (first charge)","tex":"\\ge 13\\% \\times \\text{natural resource revenue}"},{"lb":"Amount distributable","tex":"\\text{Gross} - \\text{cost of collection} - \\text{derivation}"},{"lb":"A state's statutory allocation","tex":"\\text{Distributable} \\times 26.72\\% \\times \\text{state horizontal factor}"}],"focus":"Two or three Section A marks every diet on the vertical formula, the 13% derivation principle, the VAT distribution, or the classification of a particular receipt. Section B commonly asks for the sources of revenue of a state or local government, or for ways of improving internally generated revenue. Learn the three vertical percentages and the five horizontal principles with their weights; they are asked directly.","errors":["Saying all revenue of the Federation goes into the Consolidated Revenue Fund; revenue collected by the Government of the Federation goes into the Federation Account, and only the Federal share reaches the CRF.","Giving the derivation principle as 13% of the Federation Account; it is 13% of revenue from natural resources, and it is a first charge.","Applying the Federation Account vertical formula to VAT; VAT has its own pool and its own formula.","Listing borrowing as revenue without noting that it is financing and creates a liability.","Confusing statutory allocation (from the Federation Account) with internally generated revenue (raised by the tier itself)."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under section 162(2) of the 1999 Constitution, the derivation principle provides that not less than what percentage of revenue from natural resources goes to the state of derivation?","o":["10%","13%","20%","25%","50%"],"a":1,"w":"Not less than 13% of revenue accruing directly from any natural resource is paid to the state of derivation as a first charge, before the balance is shared.","src":"Chapter 9.2"},{"q":"The current vertical allocation of the Federation Account to the Federal Government is","o":["48.50%","52.68%","56.00%","26.72%","20.60%"],"a":1,"w":"The shares are Federal 52.68%, States 26.72% and Local Governments 20.60%.","src":"Chapter 9.2"},{"q":"Value added tax proceeds are shared among the tiers of government in the ratio","o":["Federal 52.68%, States 26.72%, Local 20.60%","Federal 15%, States 50%, Local 35%","Federal 50%, States 35%, Local 15%","Federal 35%, States 50%, Local 15%","Federal 20%, States 40%, Local 40%"],"a":1,"w":"VAT is not part of the Federation Account and has its own pool and formula: 15% Federal, 50% States, 35% Local Governments.","src":"Chapter 9.3"},{"q":"In the horizontal allocation formula for sharing among states, the principle carrying the greatest weight is","o":["population","equality of states","internal revenue effort","land mass","social development"],"a":1,"w":"Equality of states carries 40%, population 30%, and internal revenue effort, land mass and social development 10% each.","src":"Chapter 9.2"},{"q":"Which of the following is a non-tax revenue of government?","o":["Companies income tax","Royalties on crude oil","Value added tax","Customs duty","Education tax"],"a":1,"w":"Royalties are a payment for the extraction of a natural resource, not a compulsory levy on income or consumption.","src":"Chapter 9.1"},{"q":"Personal income tax of members of the Armed Forces and residents of the Federal Capital Territory is collected by","o":["the State Boards of Internal Revenue","the Federal Inland Revenue Service","the Nigeria Customs Service","the Accountant-General","the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission"],"a":1,"w":"Section 162(1) excepts this category from the Federation Account, and the FIRS collects it.","src":"Chapter 9.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"Many state and local governments in Nigeria depend heavily on statutory allocation from the Federation Account. (a) Distinguish between statutory allocation and internally generated revenue, and state five sources of internally generated revenue available to a state government. (b) Explain the problems that limit internally generated revenue in Nigeria. (c) Recommend measures by which a state government could improve its internally generated revenue.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) The distinction"},{"p":"**Statutory allocation** is the share of the Federation Account distributed to a tier of government under section 162 of the Constitution and the Allocation of Revenue Act. It is received rather than earned: the state exerts no effort to obtain it and has no control over its size, which depends on federally collected revenue and therefore largely on the price and volume of crude oil."},{"p":"**Internally generated revenue** is revenue a state raises within its own jurisdiction from its own tax and non-tax sources. It depends directly on the state's administrative effort, its economic base and the quality of its revenue machinery, and it is therefore both more stable and more within the state's control."},{"p":"**Sources of internally generated revenue available to a state:**"},{"ol":["**Personal income tax** on residents of the state, under the Pay As You Earn system and by direct assessment of the self-employed — normally the largest single source.","**Road taxes and vehicle-related charges** — vehicle licences, drivers' licences, number plates, roadworthiness certificates and haulage permits.","**Business premises registration and renewal fees**, and development levies.","**Fees and licences** — market and abattoir fees, liquor licences, court fees, registration of births, deaths and marriages, hospital and school fees.","**Earnings and sales** from state commercial undertakings, and dividends from state investments.","**Rent** on state-owned land and property, ground rent and certificate of occupancy charges.","**Fines and penalties** imposed by state courts and regulatory agencies.","**Capital gains tax and stamp duties** within the state's competence."]},{"h4":"(b) Problems limiting internally generated revenue"},{"ol":["**A narrow and largely informal revenue base.** A very large proportion of economic activity takes place outside any register — traders, artisans, transporters, small landlords — and is neither assessed nor collected from.","**Absence of reliable records.** Most states lack a complete database of taxpayers, properties, vehicles and businesses, so assessments cannot be raised systematically and defaulters cannot be identified.","**Leakage and diversion.** Where collections are received in cash by individual officers, a proportion never reaches the treasury. Fake receipts and unauthorised collection points are common.","**Multiple taxation.** Overlapping demands by state agencies, local governments and unauthorised bodies impose a heavy compliance burden, discourage formalisation and invite evasion.","**Weak enforcement.** Legal powers are underused, prosecution of defaulters is rare, and revenue agencies are reluctant to pursue politically connected taxpayers.","**Taxpayer resistance.** Where citizens see no relationship between the taxes they pay and the services they receive, compliance is treated as optional and evasion attracts no social disapproval.","**Institutional weakness.** Revenue staff are frequently untrained, poorly paid and poorly equipped, and revenue administration is fragmented across ministries rather than concentrated in a professional authority.","**Over-reliance on allocation.** Because the monthly allocation arrives whatever the state does, there has historically been little political incentive to undertake the unpopular work of taxing residents."]},{"h4":"(c) Recommended measures"},{"ol":["**Automate assessment and collection.** Move all collections onto electronic platforms paying into a single state revenue account, and abolish cash handling by collectors. This is the most effective single measure available, because it removes the opportunity for diversion rather than relying on the honesty of individual officers.","**Build and integrate a taxpayer database.** Enumerate residents, properties, vehicles and businesses, and link the register to the national identity number, the corporate registry, the land registry and utility records so that non-filers are identified automatically.","**Broaden the base.** Bring the informal sector in through simplified presumptive taxation at modest rates, which is administratively cheap and establishes a compliance habit; and complete the enumeration and valuation of properties for tenement rating, which is a large and largely untapped source.","**Harmonise and publish the approved list of taxes and levies**, prohibit collection outside it, and dismantle unauthorised roadblocks and collection points. Reducing multiple taxation raises revenue by encouraging formalisation.","**Establish an autonomous, professionally managed state internal revenue service**, with its own board, power to recruit and remunerate competitively, and a defined percentage of collections for its running costs. The states with the highest IGR are those that have done this.","**Strengthen enforcement.** Use the tax appeal machinery, prosecute persistent defaulters, publish their names, and apply the statutory sanctions consistently and without regard to political connection.","**Train and motivate revenue staff**, with performance measured on amounts actually collected and reconciled rather than on assessments raised.","**Improve taxpayer education and, above all, visible service delivery.** Compliance rises where citizens can see roads, schools and clinics attributable to the taxes they pay. Publishing what was collected and what it purchased is itself a revenue measure.","**Institute internal audit and revenue monitoring**, reconciling assessments raised with amounts collected and banked, to block leakage."]},{"note":"The underlying point, worth stating in a conclusion, is that internally generated revenue is not merely a fiscal matter. A government financed by its own citizens is accountable to them in a way that a government financed by an unearned monthly allocation is not. Raising IGR improves governance as well as cash flow."}],"src":"Chapter 9.1, 9.4"}]}},{"n":10,"t":"Public Procurement","brief":"The Public Procurement Act 2007: the institutions it creates, the fundamental principles of public procurement, the methods of procurement and when each may be used, approval thresholds, and the offences and penalties.","outcomes":["State the objectives of the Public Procurement Act 2007","Describe the roles of the National Council on Public Procurement and the Bureau of Public Procurement","State the fundamental principles of public procurement","Distinguish open competitive bidding from restricted tendering, request for quotations, direct procurement and emergency procurement","Explain the procurement planning and approval process, including the certificate of no objection","Identify offences under the Act and the penalties"],"secs":[{"n":"10.1","t":"Purpose and institutions","b":[{"p":"Before 2007, public procurement in Nigeria was governed only by the Financial Regulations and by circular. Contracts were routinely awarded without competition, at inflated prices, to companies connected with the awarding officers, and frequently for projects that were never completed. The **Public Procurement Act 2007** was enacted to establish a legal framework, following the \"due process\" reform begun in 2001."},{"h4":"Objectives of the Act"},{"ul":["To harmonise existing government policies and practices on public procurement.","To establish pricing standards and benchmarks.","To ensure **probity, accountability and transparency** in the procurement process.","To achieve **value for money** and fitness for purpose in public expenditure.","To promote competition, economy and efficiency.","To ensure that public procurement is applied as a tool of national development."]},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Institution","Role"],"rows":[["**National Council on Public Procurement (NCPP)**","The policy-making body. Chaired by the Minister of Finance, with the Attorney-General, the Secretary to the Government of the Federation, the Head of Service, the Economic Adviser and representatives of professional and private sector bodies. It considers and approves policies, approves the appointment of the Bureau's directors, receives the Bureau's audited accounts, and approves changes in procurement thresholds"],["**Bureau of Public Procurement (BPP)**","The regulatory and supervisory body, headed by a Director-General. It formulates policies for the Council, certifies federal procurement before award (the certificate of no objection), supervises implementation, publishes procurement details, maintains the National Database of contractors and of prices, monitors prices, conducts procurement audits and reviews complaints"],["**Accounting officer of the procuring entity**","Personally responsible for the entity's procurement: for planning, for constituting the tenders board, for ensuring compliance with the Act, and for the records"],["**Tenders Board / Procurement Planning Committee**","The Procurement Planning Committee prepares the plan; the Tenders Board conducts the process and makes the award within its threshold"]]}},{"note":"A recurring criticism, and a good point to make in a discussion question, is that the National Council on Public Procurement was not constituted for many years after the Act, its functions being exercised in practice by the Federal Executive Council. Since the Council approves thresholds and appointments, its absence weakened the independence the Act intended."}]},{"n":"10.2","t":"Fundamental principles","b":[{"ol":["**Open competitive bidding** is the default method for all procurement. Any other method requires justification and, above the threshold, the approval of the Bureau.","**Economy and efficiency** — the procurement must represent value for money, which is not necessarily the lowest price but the lowest **evaluated responsive** bid.","**Transparency** — bids are opened in public, immediately after the deadline, in the presence of bidders and observers from professional bodies and civil society.","**Equal opportunity and non-discrimination** — all interested suppliers must have the same information, the same deadline and the same criteria applied.","**Objective evaluation on criteria published in advance.** No criterion may be applied that was not in the bid documents.","**Procurement planning.** No procurement may proceed unless it is in the approved procurement plan and funds are available and appropriated.","**Accountability and record keeping** — a complete record of the process must be kept and is subject to procurement audit.","**Professionalism** — procurement must be conducted by trained officers of a procurement cadre."]},{"key":"The Act requires the award to go to the **lowest evaluated responsive bid**, not simply to the lowest bid. A bid that does not meet the specification, or comes from a bidder who fails the prequalification, is not responsive however cheap it is — and a bid that is abnormally low may be rejected as unrealistic."}]},{"n":"10.3","t":"Methods of procurement","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Method","When it may be used","Procedure"],"rows":[["**Open competitive bidding**","The default for all procurement above the threshold for quotations","Advertisement in at least two national newspapers, the procuring entity's website and the government tenders journal, allowing a minimum of six weeks; public bid opening; evaluation against published criteria; award to the lowest evaluated responsive bid"],["**Restricted (selective) tendering**","Where the goods or services are available only from a limited number of suppliers; where the cost of open bidding would be disproportionate to the value; or where the goods are of a specialised nature","Invitation of a shortlist of qualified suppliers, with the Bureau's approval, but otherwise following the open bidding procedure"],["**Request for quotations**","For procurement of readily available goods or small works below the prescribed threshold","Written quotations obtained from at least **three** unrelated suppliers; award to the lowest responsive quotation"],["**Direct (single-source) procurement**","Where the goods are available from only one supplier and no reasonable alternative exists; for additional supplies from the original supplier for compatibility reasons; or where there is an unforeseeable emergency","Negotiation with the single supplier, with full documentation of the justification and the approval of the Bureau"],["**Emergency procurement**","Where there is an imminent or actual threat to life, property or the environment, or where the country is seriously threatened by a disaster or war","Expedited procedure limited to what is strictly necessary; a report must be made to the Bureau within the prescribed period and the procurement ratified"],["**Two-stage tendering**","Where it is not feasible to formulate detailed specifications at the outset — complex works or information technology systems","First stage: technical proposals without prices, leading to a refined specification. Second stage: priced bids on the agreed specification"],["**Request for proposals**","Procurement of **consultancy and other services** where quality is paramount","Evaluation on quality and cost combined, in the weightings stated in the request"]]}}]},{"n":"10.4","t":"The procurement process","b":[{"steps":["**Procurement planning.** The Procurement Planning Committee prepares the annual procurement plan, based on the approved budget, and no procurement outside the plan may proceed.","**Confirmation of funds.** The accounting officer certifies that funds are appropriated and available. Commencing a procurement without funds is an offence.","**Advertisement and prequalification.** The invitation is published; where the works are complex, bidders are prequalified on experience, financial capacity, plant and personnel.","**Issue of bidding documents**, containing the specification, the conditions of contract, the evaluation criteria and the deadline.","**Receipt and public opening of bids.** Bids are opened immediately after the deadline, in public, in the presence of bidders and of observers from professional bodies and civil society organisations. Names and prices are read out and recorded.","**Evaluation.** A technical evaluation against the specification, then a financial evaluation of the technically responsive bids, applying only the published criteria.","**Recommendation and approval by the Tenders Board** within its threshold, or referral upward where the value exceeds it.","**Certificate of no objection.** Where the value exceeds the threshold for the entity, the Bureau reviews the process and issues a **Certificate of \"No Objection\" to Contract Award**. Without it the award is invalid.","**Award and contract execution.** The letter of award is issued, the contract signed, and performance bonds and advance payment guarantees obtained.","**Contract administration and payment**, against certificates of work done and after inspection.","**Record keeping and procurement audit.** The complete record is retained and is subject to review by the Bureau and by the Auditor-General."]},{"h4":"Approval thresholds"},{"p":"The Act provides for approving authorities by value, with the limits set in regulations and revised from time to time. The structure is:"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Value of the procurement","Approving authority"],"rows":[["Small value","Accounting officer of the procuring entity"],["Above that limit","Parastatals Tenders Board / Ministerial Tenders Board"],["Above the Bureau threshold","Federal Executive Council, on the certificate of no objection issued by the Bureau"]]}},{"warn":"Learn the **structure** of the thresholds rather than the naira figures, which are revised by circular and differ between goods, works and services. An answer that describes the tiers of approval and the certificate of no objection scores full marks; an answer quoting an out-of-date figure does not."}]},{"n":"10.5","t":"Offences and remedies","b":[{"h4":"Offences under the Act"},{"ul":["Entering or attempting to enter into a **collusive agreement** with bidders, or **bid-rigging**.","Directing the procuring entity to **split a contract** in order to bring it below a threshold and avoid the required approval.","Altering any procurement document with intent to influence the outcome.","Using a **fake document or encouraging its use**.","Wilful refusal to allow the Bureau access to any procurement record.","**Bid-rigging, giving or receiving bribes**, or influencing the process for personal gain.","Awarding a contract **without budgetary provision, approval or a cash-backed plan**.","Failing to obtain the certificate of no objection where required."]},{"h4":"Penalties"},{"ul":["For a **natural person**: imprisonment for a term of not less than five calendar years without the option of a fine.","For a **legal person** (a body corporate): a fine of not less than 25% of the value of the procurement, and the officers of the company are liable to prosecution; the company may be **debarred** from participating in public procurement for a stated period.","A public officer additionally faces **summary dismissal** from service.","Conviction may be accompanied by an order for **restitution** of any loss to government."]},{"h4":"Complaints and review"},{"p":"A bidder who considers that he has suffered loss through a breach of the Act must first complain in writing to the **accounting officer** of the procuring entity, who must respond within 15 working days. If dissatisfied, the bidder may complain to the **Bureau**, which must decide within 21 working days and may nullify the procurement, order a re-tender, or declare a rule or practice inconsistent with the Act. A bidder still dissatisfied may seek **judicial review** in the Federal High Court."}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"A Section B question in most diets, usually on the methods of procurement and when each may be used, the fundamental principles, or the roles of the Council and the Bureau. Section A tests the year of the Act, the minimum number of quotations, and the meaning of the certificate of no objection. Learn the seven methods with the circumstances for each — that table alone answers half the questions set on this chapter.","errors":["Saying the contract goes to the lowest bid; it goes to the lowest **evaluated responsive** bid.","Confusing the National Council on Public Procurement (policy) with the Bureau of Public Procurement (regulation and certification).","Stating that two quotations suffice for a request for quotations; the Act requires at least three from unrelated suppliers.","Describing restricted tendering as available at the entity's discretion; it requires justification and the Bureau's approval.","Omitting the certificate of no objection from the process, which is where the Bureau's control actually bites.","Giving the penalty for a body corporate as imprisonment; a company is fined not less than 25% of the value and may be debarred."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The default method of procurement under the Public Procurement Act 2007 is","o":["restricted tendering","open competitive bidding","request for quotations","direct procurement","two-stage tendering"],"a":1,"w":"Every other method requires justification, and above the threshold the approval of the Bureau of Public Procurement.","src":"Chapter 10.2"},{"q":"Under a request for quotations, quotations must be obtained from at least","o":["two unrelated suppliers","three unrelated suppliers","five suppliers","one supplier","seven suppliers"],"a":1,"w":"The Act requires a minimum of three quotations from unrelated contractors or suppliers.","src":"Chapter 10.3"},{"q":"The document issued by the Bureau of Public Procurement before a high-value contract may be awarded is the","o":["procurement plan","certificate of no objection","letter of award","performance bond","bid security"],"a":1,"w":"The Certificate of \"No Objection\" to Contract Award confirms that the Bureau has reviewed the process. Without it the award is invalid.","src":"Chapter 10.4"},{"q":"A contract under the Act should be awarded to the","o":["lowest bidder","lowest evaluated responsive bidder","bidder with the best technical proposal","most experienced bidder","bidder recommended by the Minister"],"a":1,"w":"The bid must first be responsive — meeting the specification and the qualification requirements — before price is compared. A cheap but non-responsive bid is rejected.","src":"Chapter 10.2"},{"q":"A body corporate convicted of an offence under the Public Procurement Act is liable to a fine of not less than","o":["10% of the procurement value","25% of the procurement value","50% of the procurement value","5% of the procurement value","₦1,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"The company is fined not less than 25% of the value of the procurement, its officers may be prosecuted, and it may be debarred from public procurement.","src":"Chapter 10.5"},{"q":"The policy-making body established by the Public Procurement Act 2007 is the","o":["Bureau of Public Procurement","National Council on Public Procurement","Federal Executive Council","Ministerial Tenders Board","Procurement Planning Committee"],"a":1,"w":"The Council makes policy and approves thresholds; the Bureau is the regulatory and supervisory body that certifies individual procurements.","src":"Chapter 10.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State six objectives of the Public Procurement Act 2007. (b) Describe five methods of procurement recognised by the Act, stating the circumstances in which each may be used. (c) Outline the stages of an open competitive bidding process from planning to award. (d) State four offences under the Act and the penalties on conviction.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Objectives of the Act"},{"ol":["To **harmonise** existing government policies and practices on public procurement, which before 2007 were scattered across the Financial Regulations and successive circulars.","To **establish pricing standards and benchmarks**, so that the reasonableness of a quoted price can be tested against a national database.","To ensure **probity, accountability and transparency** in the procurement process, by requiring advertisement, public bid opening and published evaluation criteria.","To achieve **value for money and fitness for purpose** in the application of public funds.","To promote **competition, economy and efficiency** among suppliers and contractors.","To ensure that public procurement is applied as a **tool of national development**, including support for local content where appropriate.","To **professionalise** procurement by establishing a trained procurement cadre."]},{"h4":"(b) Methods of procurement"},{"ol":["**Open competitive bidding.** The default method for all procurement above the quotation threshold. The invitation is advertised in at least two national newspapers, on the entity's website and in the tenders journal, with a minimum period for the preparation of bids. It applies unless the circumstances justify one of the alternatives below.","**Restricted (selective) tendering.** Permitted where the goods, works or services are available only from a limited number of suppliers; where the time and cost of the open procedure would be disproportionate to the value of the procurement; or where the subject matter is of a highly specialised nature. It requires the approval of the Bureau, and bids are invited from a shortlist of qualified suppliers.","**Request for quotations.** Used for readily available goods or minor works whose value falls below the prescribed threshold. Written quotations must be obtained from at least three unrelated suppliers, and the award goes to the lowest responsive quotation.","**Direct (single-source) procurement.** Permitted where the goods or services are available from only one supplier and no reasonable alternative exists; where additional supplies are required from the original supplier for reasons of compatibility or standardisation; or where an unforeseeable emergency makes competitive methods impracticable. Full documentation of the justification and the Bureau's approval are required.","**Emergency procurement.** Available where there is an imminent or actual threat to life, property or the environment, or where the country is seriously threatened by disaster or war. It is limited strictly to what is necessary to meet the emergency, and a report must be made to the Bureau, which ratifies or otherwise the action taken.","**Two-stage tendering.** Used where it is not feasible to formulate detailed specifications at the outset, as with complex works or information technology systems. Technical proposals without prices are invited first and used to settle the specification; priced bids on that specification follow.","**Request for proposals.** Used for consultancy and other services, where quality rather than price is the dominant consideration. Evaluation combines quality and cost in weightings stated in advance."]},{"h4":"(c) Stages of an open competitive bidding process"},{"ol":["**Procurement planning.** The Procurement Planning Committee prepares the annual procurement plan from the approved budget. Nothing outside the plan may be procured.","**Confirmation of funding.** The accounting officer certifies that the expenditure is appropriated and that funds are available. Commencing a procurement without funds is itself an offence.","**Advertisement.** The invitation to bid is published in at least two national newspapers, on the entity's website and in the government tenders journal, allowing the minimum period prescribed.","**Prequalification**, where the works are complex, assessing bidders on experience, financial capacity, plant and personnel.","**Issue of bidding documents** setting out the specification, conditions of contract, evaluation criteria and deadline. No criterion may later be applied that is not in these documents.","**Receipt and public opening of bids** immediately after the deadline, in the presence of bidders and of observers from professional bodies and civil society organisations, with names and prices read out and recorded.","**Technical evaluation** against the specification to determine responsiveness, followed by **financial evaluation** of the responsive bids.","**Recommendation and approval** by the Tenders Board within its threshold, or referral to the higher approving authority.","**Certificate of no objection** obtained from the Bureau of Public Procurement where the value exceeds the entity's threshold.","**Award and contract execution**, with performance bonds and advance payment guarantees where applicable.","**Contract administration**, payment against certified work done, and retention of the complete record for procurement audit."]},{"h4":"(d) Offences and penalties"},{"p":"**Offences** include entering into a collusive agreement with bidders or engaging in bid-rigging; splitting a contract to bring it below a threshold and avoid the required approval; altering a procurement document or using a fake document with intent to influence the outcome; giving or receiving a bribe or otherwise influencing the process for personal gain; wilfully refusing the Bureau access to procurement records; and awarding a contract without budgetary provision, without approval, or without the certificate of no objection."},{"p":"**Penalties.** A natural person convicted is liable to imprisonment for a term of not less than five calendar years without the option of a fine, and a public officer is additionally liable to summary dismissal from service. A body corporate is liable to a fine of not less than 25% of the value of the procurement, its directors and officers may themselves be prosecuted, and it may be debarred from participating in public procurement for a stated period. The court may in addition order restitution of any loss caused to government."},{"note":"The practical significance of the Act lies less in the penalties, which have rarely been applied, than in the procedural requirements — advertisement, public opening, published criteria and the certificate of no objection. These work by removing discretion and creating a record, which is a more reliable control than the threat of a prosecution that may never come."}],"src":"Chapter 10.1–10.5"}]}},{"n":11,"t":"Stores, Losses and the Board of Survey","brief":"The classification and control of government stores, the ledgers and cards used to record them, the treatment of losses of cash and stores, and the composition and duties of Boards of Survey and Boards of Enquiry.","outcomes":["Classify government stores as allocated, unallocated or expendable","Describe the documents used to control receipt and issue of stores","Explain stock verification and the treatment of surpluses and shortages","Describe the procedure on discovery of a loss of cash or stores","Distinguish a Board of Survey from a Board of Enquiry","Explain surcharge and the write-off of losses"],"secs":[{"n":"11.1","t":"Classification of stores","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Class","Description","Accounting treatment"],"rows":[["**Allocated stores**","Purchased for and charged directly to a particular vote or job at the time of purchase","Charged to the vote immediately; no store ledger value is carried"],["**Unallocated stores**","Purchased in bulk and held in store for future issue to various votes as required — stationery, fuel, spare parts, drugs","Held at cost in the store ledger; charged to the receiving vote on issue"],["**Expendable stores**","Consumed in use and not returnable — stationery, cleaning materials, drugs, fuel","Written off on issue"],["**Non-expendable stores**","Durable items returnable after use — tools, furniture, equipment, vehicles","Recorded on an inventory or asset register and accounted for until disposal"]]}},{"key":"The distinction between **allocated** and **unallocated** stores decides *when* the vote is charged. Allocated stores hit the vote on purchase; unallocated stores sit in a stores account and hit the vote on issue. Confusing the two produces a wrong charge to the vote and a wrong closing stores balance."}]},{"n":"11.2","t":"Records and documents","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Document","Purpose"],"rows":[["**Local Purchase Order (LPO)**","Authorises a supplier to deliver goods"],["**Store Receipt Voucher (SRV)**","Evidences receipt of goods into store after inspection; the authority for entry on the receipt side of the ledger"],["**Store Issue Voucher (SIV)**","Authorises and evidences the issue of stores from store to a using department; the authority for the issue side"],["**Bin card**","Kept by the storekeeper at the bin. Records **quantities only** — receipts, issues and balance — item by item"],["**Store ledger (stock card)**","Kept in the accounts section. Records **quantity and value**, and is the basis of the stores account"],["**Requisition**","The using department's request for stores"],["**Inventory / asset register**","Lists non-expendable items, their location and the officer responsible"],["**Store Adjustment Voucher**","Records approved adjustments — surpluses, shortages, revaluation, conversion"],["**Certificate of unserviceability / Board of Survey report**","Supports the write-off or disposal of stores no longer usable"]]}},{"note":"The **bin card is kept by the storekeeper and shows quantity only; the store ledger is kept in the accounts section and shows quantity and value.** The separation is deliberate: two independent records of the same movement, kept by different officers, which must agree. It is examined almost every diet."}]},{"n":"11.3","t":"Stock verification","b":[{"p":"Stock verification is the physical counting of stores and comparison with the records. It is carried out by **stock verifiers** independent of the storekeeper, periodically and without notice, and comprehensively at the year end."},{"h4":"Objectives"},{"ul":["To confirm the **existence and quantity** of stores shown in the records.","To detect **shortages, pilferage and fraud**.","To identify **obsolete, damaged or unserviceable** items for write-off or disposal.","To test the **accuracy of the ledgers and bin cards** and the adequacy of store procedures.","To confirm **safe custody and proper storage** conditions.","To provide assurance for the accounts and for the auditor."]},{"h4":"Treatment of differences"},{"ul":["**Surplus** — physical quantity exceeds the record. The cause is investigated; it usually reflects an unrecorded receipt or an issue recorded twice. Once explained, the ledger is adjusted by a Store Adjustment Voucher.","**Shortage** — record exceeds physical quantity. Investigated as a **loss**, following the procedure in §11.4. A shortage within a normal allowance for evaporation, breakage or wastage may be written off on the certificate of the accounting officer; anything beyond it requires a Board of Enquiry."]}]},{"n":"11.4","t":"Losses of cash and stores","b":[{"h4":"Causes of loss"},{"ul":["Theft, burglary, armed robbery or fraud.","Fire, flood or other accident.","Negligence in custody or in the keeping of records.","Deterioration, obsolescence or expiry.","Errors of accounting or overpayment not recovered.","Loss in transit or on delivery."]},{"h4":"Procedure on discovery of a loss"},{"ol":["The officer discovering the loss reports it **immediately in writing** to his supervising officer and to the **accounting officer**.","The accounting officer reports it at once to the **Accountant-General** and the **Auditor-General**, and to the **Police** where theft or fraud is suspected.","The loss is entered in the **Register of Losses** with full particulars.","The officer suspected of responsibility may be **interdicted** pending investigation.","A **Board of Enquiry** is constituted to establish the facts: the amount, the cause, whether the Financial Regulations were complied with, whether negligence contributed, and who is responsible.","The Board reports its findings and recommendations to the accounting officer.","Where **negligence or misconduct** is established, the officer is **surcharged** with the whole or part of the loss, and is liable to disciplinary action and, where the conduct is criminal, to prosecution.","The irrecoverable balance is **written off** by the authority competent to do so — the accounting officer, the Minister of Finance or the National Assembly, according to the amount involved.","The **internal control weakness** that permitted the loss is identified and remedied."]},{"def":{"t":"Surcharge","d":"The imposition on a public officer of personal liability to make good a loss of public funds or stores caused by his negligence, misconduct or failure to comply with the Financial Regulations. The amount may be recovered from his salary, allowances or terminal benefits."}},{"def":{"t":"Write-off","d":"The formal removal of an irrecoverable loss from the books. It requires the approval of the competent authority and does not absolve any officer from liability: an officer may be surcharged even though the loss itself has been written off."}},{"warn":"Write-off and surcharge are **different decisions**. Writing off clears the accounting record; surcharging fixes personal liability. A question that asks about the treatment of a loss expects both, and the point that one does not discharge the other."}]},{"n":"11.5","t":"Boards of Survey and Boards of Enquiry","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Board of Survey","Board of Enquiry"],"rows":[["Purpose","To verify cash, bank balances and stores, and to examine and report on unserviceable or obsolete stores","To investigate a **specific loss** of cash or stores and determine its cause and who is responsible"],["When constituted","Routinely — at the year end, and on the handing over of a cash office or store","On the occurrence of a loss"],["Composition","Officers appointed by the accounting officer, none of whom is connected with the custody of the items surveyed","Senior officers independent of the office where the loss occurred, sometimes with external or professional members"],["Output","A survey report certifying balances, and recommendations on disposal of unserviceable items","A report of findings on cause and responsibility, with recommendations on surcharge, discipline and prevention"]]}},{"h4":"Duties of a Board of Survey"},{"ol":["To count the **cash and stamps** on hand and agree them to the cash book balance.","To confirm **bank balances** by direct certificate from the bank and to examine the bank reconciliation.","To verify **stores** physically against the ledgers and bin cards.","To examine items reported **unserviceable, obsolete or surplus to requirements** and recommend their disposal by sale, transfer or destruction.","To confirm **safe custody arrangements** — keys, safes, security of the store.","To report **discrepancies** and comment on the adequacy of the records and procedures.","To sign and submit the report to the accounting officer, with copies to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General."]},{"p":"The **annual Board of Survey** is convened at the close of the financial year and its certificate supports the cash and stores figures in the financial statements. A **special Board of Survey** is convened on a change of custody — when a cashier or storekeeper hands over — so that the incoming officer takes over a verified balance and neither officer can be blamed for the other's shortfall."},{"key":"The single sentence that best distinguishes them: a **Board of Survey verifies what is there**; a **Board of Enquiry investigates what is missing**."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Closing stores balance","tex":"\\text{Opening} + \\text{Receipts} - \\text{Issues} \\pm \\text{Adjustments}"},{"lb":"Stock discrepancy","tex":"\\text{Physical count} - \\text{Ledger balance}"},{"lb":"Amount surchargeable","tex":"\\text{Loss} - \\text{amount recovered} - \\text{allowable wastage}"}],"focus":"A Section B question in perhaps half the diets, most often on the duties of a Board of Survey, the procedure on discovery of a loss, or the difference between a Board of Survey and a Board of Enquiry. Section A tests the bin card / store ledger distinction and the classification of stores. The procedure on a loss should be learnt as a numbered sequence of about eight steps.","errors":["Saying the bin card shows quantity and value; it shows quantity only, and is kept by the storekeeper.","Treating write-off as discharging the officer from liability; surcharge and write-off are separate decisions.","Confusing a Board of Survey (routine verification) with a Board of Enquiry (investigation of a specific loss).","Charging unallocated stores to the vote on purchase; they are charged on issue.","Omitting the report to the Auditor-General and the Police from the loss procedure.","Appointing to a Board of Survey an officer connected with the custody of the items being surveyed."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Stores purchased in bulk and held for issue to various votes as required are described as","o":["allocated stores","unallocated stores","expendable stores","non-expendable stores","obsolete stores"],"a":1,"w":"Unallocated stores are charged to the receiving vote on issue, not on purchase. Allocated stores are charged directly to a vote when bought.","src":"Chapter 11.1"},{"q":"The record kept by the storekeeper showing quantities only is the","o":["store ledger","bin card","store receipt voucher","inventory register","store issue voucher"],"a":1,"w":"The bin card records quantities at the bin. The store ledger, kept in the accounts section, records both quantity and value.","src":"Chapter 11.2"},{"q":"A body constituted to investigate a specific loss of cash or stores and determine responsibility for it is a","o":["Board of Survey","Board of Enquiry","Tenders Board","Procurement Planning Committee","Public Accounts Committee"],"a":1,"w":"A Board of Survey verifies what is present; a Board of Enquiry investigates what is missing.","src":"Chapter 11.5"},{"q":"Requiring a public officer to make good a loss caused by his negligence is known as","o":["write-off","surcharge","interdiction","virement","adjustment"],"a":1,"w":"Surcharge imposes personal financial liability. Write-off merely removes the irrecoverable amount from the books and does not absolve the officer.","src":"Chapter 11.4"},{"q":"A Board of Survey should be constituted","o":["only when a loss occurs","at the end of the financial year and on a change of custody of cash or stores","only when the Auditor-General so directs","every month","only when stores are declared unserviceable"],"a":1,"w":"The annual board supports the cash and stores figures in the financial statements; a special board on handing over protects both the outgoing and the incoming officer.","src":"Chapter 11.5"},{"q":"On discovery of a loss of public funds through suspected theft, the accounting officer must report to the Accountant-General, the Police and the","o":["Minister of Finance only","Auditor-General","National Assembly","Bureau of Public Procurement","Fiscal Responsibility Commission"],"a":1,"w":"The Auditor-General must be informed so that the loss is examined independently of the department in which it occurred.","src":"Chapter 11.4"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) Distinguish between a Board of Survey and a Board of Enquiry, stating the composition and duties of each. (b) Describe the procedure to be followed on the discovery of a loss of government stores. (c) Explain the terms \"surcharge\" and \"write-off\" and state the relationship between them.","marks":15,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Board of Survey and Board of Enquiry"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Board of Survey","Board of Enquiry"],"rows":[["Purpose","Routine verification of cash, bank balances and stores, and examination of items reported unserviceable or obsolete","Investigation of a particular loss of cash or stores, to establish its cause, extent and responsibility"],["Trigger","The end of the financial year, or a handing over of a cash office or store","The occurrence or discovery of a loss"],["Composition","Officers appointed by the accounting officer, none of whom has any connection with the custody of the items being surveyed","Senior officers independent of the office where the loss occurred, and where the matter is serious, officers from outside the entity"],["Nature","Verificatory and preventive","Investigative and, in effect, quasi-judicial"],["Output","A certificate of the balances found and recommendations on disposal","Findings on cause and responsibility, with recommendations on surcharge, discipline and prevention"]]}},{"p":"**Duties of a Board of Survey**"},{"ol":["Count the cash and postage stamps on hand and agree the total to the cash book balance.","Obtain bank certificates directly from the banks and examine the bank reconciliation statements.","Verify stores physically and compare the quantities found with the store ledgers and bin cards.","Examine items reported unserviceable, obsolete, damaged or surplus to requirements, and recommend disposal by public auction, sale by tender, transfer or destruction.","Satisfy itself as to the safe custody arrangements — safes, keys, security of the store and access controls.","Report any discrepancy found, with an explanation where one can be obtained, and comment on the adequacy of records and procedures.","Sign and submit the report to the accounting officer, with copies to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General."]},{"p":"**Duties of a Board of Enquiry**"},{"ol":["Establish the exact nature and amount of the loss.","Determine how and when it occurred, taking evidence from the officers concerned.","Establish whether the Financial Regulations and store procedures were complied with.","Determine whether negligence, misconduct or fraud on the part of any officer contributed to the loss, and identify that officer.","Assess whether any part of the loss is recoverable.","Recommend the amount, if any, to be surcharged, and any disciplinary or criminal action.","Identify the weakness in internal control that permitted the loss and recommend remedial measures."]},{"h4":"(b) Procedure on discovery of a loss of stores"},{"ol":["The officer discovering the loss reports it **immediately and in writing** to his supervising officer and to the accounting officer, stating the items and quantities involved and the circumstances.","The accounting officer reports the loss **at once to the Accountant-General and the Auditor-General**, and to the **Police** where theft, burglary or fraud is suspected. Reporting outside the department is essential: it prevents the matter being concealed by those responsible for it.","The loss is entered in the **Register of Losses**, with the date, description, quantity, value and circumstances.","The scene and the records are **preserved**, and the officer suspected of responsibility may be **interdicted** so that he does not remain in control of the items or records concerned.","A **Board of Enquiry** is constituted to investigate, taking evidence and examining the store ledgers, bin cards, receipt and issue vouchers and stock verification reports.","The Board **reports** its findings on the cause of the loss, the extent of any negligence and the officer responsible, with recommendations.","Where negligence or misconduct is established, the officer is **surcharged** with the whole or part of the loss and is subject to disciplinary action; where the conduct is criminal, the matter is referred for prosecution.","Any irrecoverable balance is **written off** by the authority competent to do so according to the amount — the accounting officer, the Minister of Finance, or the National Assembly.","The **control weakness** disclosed is remedied, and the stores records are adjusted by a Store Adjustment Voucher so that the ledger agrees with the physical position."]},{"h4":"(c) Surcharge and write-off"},{"p":"**Surcharge** is the imposition on a public officer of personal liability to make good a loss of public funds or stores which arose from his negligence, misconduct or failure to comply with the Financial Regulations. The amount may be recovered from his salary, his allowances or ultimately his terminal benefits. It is a measure directed at the **officer**."},{"p":"**Write-off** is the formal removal of an irrecoverable loss from the books, authorised by the officer or body competent to approve it for the amount involved. It is an accounting measure directed at the **records**, and is necessary so that the ledgers reflect the true position."},{"p":"**The relationship between them.** They are independent decisions and neither implies the other. A loss may be written off because it cannot be recovered from anyone — as with a fire — without any officer being surcharged. Conversely an officer may be surcharged and the loss still written off, because writing off clears the record while the surcharge pursues the officer. It is precisely because write-off does **not** discharge liability that the two are considered separately: if it did, an officer's easiest defence would be to procure a write-off."}],"src":"Chapter 11.4–11.5"}]}},{"n":12,"t":"Local Government Accounting","brief":"The constitutional position of local government, its functions and sources of revenue, the State Joint Local Government Account, the Model Financial Memoranda, the departmental structure and the officers responsible for its finances.","outcomes":["State the constitutional basis of local government and its functions","Identify the sources of local government revenue","Explain the State Joint Local Government Account and the Joint Account Allocation Committee","Describe the Model Financial Memoranda and their status","Describe the departmental structure of a local government and the duties of the Treasurer","Discuss the problems of local government finance"],"secs":[{"n":"12.1","t":"Constitutional position and functions","b":[{"p":"Section 7(1) of the 1999 Constitution guarantees a **system of local government by democratically elected local government councils**, and requires each State to ensure their existence, structure, composition, finance and functions by law. There are **774** local government areas listed in the First Schedule."},{"h4":"Main functions (Fourth Schedule, paragraph 1)"},{"ol":["Consideration and making of recommendations to the State commission on economic planning on the economic development of the State.","Collection of **rates** and of radio and television licences.","Establishment and maintenance of **cemeteries, burial grounds** and homes for the destitute or infirm.","Licensing of **bicycles, trucks** (other than mechanically propelled trucks), **canoes, wheelbarrows and carts**.","Establishment, maintenance and regulation of **slaughter houses and slabs, markets, motor parks and public conveniences**.","Construction and maintenance of **roads, streets, street lighting, drains, parks, gardens and open spaces**.","**Naming of roads and streets and numbering of houses**.","Provision and maintenance of **public conveniences, sewage and refuse disposal**.","**Registration of births, deaths and marriages**.","**Assessment of privately owned houses or tenements** for the purpose of levying rates.","Control and regulation of **out-door advertising and hoardings**, movement and keeping of pets, shops and kiosks, restaurants and other places for the sale of food, laundries, and the licensing and control of the sale of liquor."]},{"h4":"Participatory functions (Fourth Schedule, paragraph 2)"},{"p":"Performed jointly with the State Government: the provision and maintenance of **primary, adult and vocational education**; the development of **agriculture and natural resources** other than the exploitation of minerals; the provision and maintenance of **health services**; and such other functions as the State House of Assembly may confer."}]},{"n":"12.2","t":"Sources of revenue","b":[{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Source","Basis"],"rows":[["**Statutory allocation from the Federation Account**","20.60% of the distributable Federation Account, shared among the 774 councils on the horizontal formula. Normally the largest single source, often exceeding 80% of total revenue"],["**Share of value added tax**","35% of the VAT pool, shared among the councils"],["**Ten per cent of State internally generated revenue**","Section 162(7) of the Constitution requires each State to pay to its local government councils such proportion of its total revenue as may be prescribed; the figure commonly applied is 10% of the State's internally generated revenue"],["**Tenement rates**","Levied on privately owned houses and tenements assessed by the council. Potentially the largest local source and almost everywhere the most under-exploited"],["**Licences and fees**","Bicycle, truck, canoe, wheelbarrow and cart licences; liquor licences; shops and kiosks rates; slaughter slab fees; market and motor park fees; radio and television licences; registration of births, deaths and marriages; naming of streets; advertising and signboard permits; customary burial ground permits"],["**Earnings from commercial undertakings**","Rent from council property, income from transport services, guest houses and other ventures"],["**Grants**","From the Federal or State Government, often matching grants tied to a capital project"],["**Loans**","With the approval of the State Government, and subject to the Fiscal Responsibility framework"],["**Fines and penalties**","Imposed by customary courts and by council regulation"]]}},{"key":"The distinguishing feature of local government revenue is **dependence**. Where a council draws 80% or more of its income from a monthly allocation over which it has no influence, it cannot plan, its finances move with the oil price, and the political incentive to develop the tenement rate — the one substantial source genuinely within its control — is very weak."}]},{"n":"12.3","t":"The State Joint Local Government Account","b":[{"p":"Section 162(6) requires each State to maintain a special account called the **State Joint Local Government Account**, into which are paid all allocations to the local government councils of the State from the Federation Account and from the Government of the State."},{"ul":["Section 162(5): the amount standing to the credit of local government councils in the Federation Account is allocated to the States **for the benefit of** their local government councils.","Section 162(8): the amount standing to the credit of local government councils of a State is distributed among them **on such terms and in such manner as may be prescribed by the House of Assembly of the State**.","The **Joint Account Allocation Committee (JAAC)**, comprising representatives of the State and of the councils, meets monthly to determine and effect the distribution."]},{"warn":"The Joint Account is the most criticised provision in local government finance. Because the money passes through the State, deductions are frequently made before distribution — for primary school teachers' salaries, for state-wide projects, and in some cases for purposes never accounted for. Several councils have litigated the point. Candidates should know both the constitutional basis and the criticism, since discussion questions almost always ask for both."}]},{"n":"12.4","t":"Financial regulation and structure","b":[{"def":{"t":"Model Financial Memoranda","d":"The comprehensive code of financial and accounting procedure for local government, issued by the States and modelled on a national template. It performs for local government the function the Financial Regulations perform for the Federal Government, covering budgeting, revenue collection, expenditure control, stores, losses, the cash book, the treatment of vouchers and the preparation of the final accounts."}},{"h4":"Political and administrative structure"},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Organ or officer","Role"],"rows":[["**Chairman**","Chief executive and accounting officer of the council"],["**Vice-Chairman**","Deputises for the Chairman"],["**Supervisory Councillors**","Political heads of the departments, appointed by the Chairman"],["**Legislative Council (Councillors)**","Approves the annual estimates and the by-laws, and exercises oversight over the executive"],["**Secretary to the Local Government**","Co-ordinates the administration and the departments"],["**Treasurer / Director of Finance**","The professional head of the finance department; responsible for the accounts"],["**Head of Personnel Management**","Human resources and general administration"],["**Internal Auditor**","Reports to the Chairman; appraises the internal control system continuously"],["**Local Government Service Commission**","A State body responsible for appointment, promotion and discipline of local government staff"]]}},{"h4":"Departments of a local government"},{"ul":["**Administration and General Services** — personnel, records, legislative support.","**Finance and Supplies** — budget, accounts, revenue, stores.","**Works, Housing and Transport** — roads, drains, street lighting, buildings.","**Education, Social Welfare and Community Development**.","**Agriculture and Natural Resources**.","**Primary Health Care**."]},{"h4":"Duties of the Local Government Treasurer"},{"ol":["Maintains the books of account of the council and ensures they comply with the Model Financial Memoranda.","Prepares the annual **estimates** in consultation with the departments and the Chairman.","Prepares the **monthly and annual financial statements** and the transcripts required by the State.","Controls the **collection of revenue** and ensures it is banked promptly and intact.","Ensures all **payments are properly authorised**, vouched and within the vote.","Maintains the **vote book** and advises the Chairman when a vote is nearing exhaustion.","Ensures the **safe custody** of council funds and the security of receipt books and other accountable documents.","Advises the Chairman and the council on **all financial matters**.","Liaises with the **auditors** — the State Auditor-General for Local Governments — and responds to queries."]},{"note":"Local government accounts are audited by the **Auditor-General for Local Governments** of the State, an office distinct from the State Auditor-General, who reports to the State House of Assembly through the Local Government Service Commission or as the State law prescribes."}]},{"n":"12.5","t":"Problems of local government finance","b":[{"ol":["**Over-dependence on statutory allocation.** Most councils raise less than 10% of their income themselves, so their finances rise and fall with federally collected revenue and they cannot plan with confidence.","**Deductions from the Joint Account.** First-line charges for teachers' salaries and state projects frequently leave little of the allocation for the council's own functions, and the deductions are not always transparent.","**Weak internally generated revenue.** The tenement rate, the largest local source, is undeveloped almost everywhere because properties are not enumerated or valued.","**Shortage of qualified staff.** Councils, particularly rural ones, struggle to attract and retain accountants, engineers and revenue officers.","**Poor record keeping**, so that revenue due cannot be identified and expenditure cannot be properly accounted for.","**Political interference** in the appointment of officials and in the award of contracts, and in several States the replacement of elected councils with appointed caretaker committees, contrary to section 7(1).","**Recurrent expenditure dominance.** Personnel costs consume the greater part of most councils' revenue, leaving little for the capital projects that justify the tier's existence.","**Weak audit and oversight.** Audit reports are frequently in arrears and rarely acted on, and legislative councils often lack the capacity to scrutinise the accounts.","**Corruption and leakage**, particularly in revenue collected in cash at markets and motor parks."]},{"p":"**Remedies** commonly proposed are direct payment of the allocation to councils, abolishing or reforming the Joint Account; enumeration and valuation of properties for tenement rating; automation of revenue collection to eliminate cash handling; financial autonomy backed by the constitutional amendment guaranteeing tenure for elected councils; strengthening of the internal audit function; and capacity building for finance staff."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Local government share of Federation Account","tex":"20.60\\% \\times \\text{distributable pool}"},{"lb":"Local government share of VAT","tex":"35\\% \\times \\text{VAT pool}"},{"lb":"Share of State revenue (s.162(7))","tex":"10\\% \\times \\text{State internally generated revenue}"}],"focus":"A Section B question in perhaps a third of diets, usually on the sources of local government revenue, the functions under the Fourth Schedule, the duties of the Treasurer, or the problems of local government finance. Section A tests the number of local governments, the Joint Account, and which section of the Constitution guarantees elected councils. Learn the Fourth Schedule functions as a list of at least eight.","errors":["Saying local government allocations are paid directly to the councils; they pass through the State Joint Local Government Account.","Confusing the Financial Regulations (federal) with the Model Financial Memoranda (local government).","Naming the Secretary to the Local Government as the accounting officer; it is the Chairman.","Giving the local government share of VAT as 20.60%; that is the Federation Account share, while VAT is 35%.","Listing participatory functions (education, health, agriculture) as though they were exclusive functions of the council; they are shared with the State."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The number of local government areas listed in the First Schedule to the 1999 Constitution is","o":["768","774","776","750","780"],"a":1,"w":"There are 774 local government areas, together with the six area councils of the Federal Capital Territory.","src":"Chapter 12.1"},{"q":"Allocations to local government councils from the Federation Account are paid into the","o":["council's own account","State Joint Local Government Account","Consolidated Revenue Fund of the State","Development Fund","Contingencies Fund"],"a":1,"w":"Section 162(6) requires each State to maintain the Joint Account, from which the Joint Account Allocation Committee distributes to the councils.","src":"Chapter 12.3"},{"q":"The accounting officer of a local government council is the","o":["Treasurer","Chairman","Secretary to the Local Government","Head of Personnel Management","Supervisory Councillor for Finance"],"a":1,"w":"The Chairman is the chief executive and accounting officer; the Treasurer is the professional head of the finance department.","src":"Chapter 12.4"},{"q":"The code of financial and accounting procedure applicable to local government is the","o":["Financial Regulations","Model Financial Memoranda","Treasury Circulars","Store Regulations","Establishment Circulars"],"a":1,"w":"The Model Financial Memoranda perform for local government the function the Financial Regulations perform for the Federal Government.","src":"Chapter 12.4"},{"q":"Which of the following is an exclusive function of a local government council under the Fourth Schedule?","o":["Provision of secondary education","Naming of roads and numbering of houses","Provision of tertiary health care","Exploitation of minerals","Registration of companies"],"a":1,"w":"Naming of roads and numbering of houses is listed in paragraph 1. Primary education and health services are participatory functions shared with the State.","src":"Chapter 12.1"},{"q":"Section 7(1) of the 1999 Constitution guarantees","o":["the Joint Account Allocation Committee","a system of local government by democratically elected councils","the 20.60% share of the Federation Account","the Model Financial Memoranda","the office of the Treasurer"],"a":1,"w":"Section 7(1) guarantees democratically elected councils and requires each State to provide for their existence, structure, composition, finance and functions by law.","src":"Chapter 12.1"}],"theory":[{"q":"(a) State eight functions of a local government council under the Fourth Schedule to the 1999 Constitution. (b) Identify the sources of revenue available to a local government council. (c) State eight duties of the Local Government Treasurer. (d) Discuss the principal problems of local government finance in Nigeria and suggest remedies.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Functions under the Fourth Schedule"},{"ol":["Consideration and making of recommendations to the State commission on economic planning on the economic development of the State.","Collection of rates and of radio and television licences.","Establishment and maintenance of cemeteries, burial grounds and homes for the destitute or infirm.","Licensing of bicycles, trucks other than mechanically propelled trucks, canoes, wheelbarrows and carts.","Establishment, maintenance and regulation of slaughter houses and slabs, markets, motor parks and public conveniences.","Construction and maintenance of roads, streets, street lighting, drains, parks, gardens and open spaces.","Naming of roads and streets and numbering of houses.","Provision and maintenance of public conveniences, sewage and refuse disposal.","Registration of all births, deaths and marriages.","Assessment of privately owned houses or tenements for the purpose of levying rates.","Control and regulation of out-door advertising, shops and kiosks, restaurants and other places for the sale of food, laundries, and the sale of liquor."]},{"p":"In addition, councils perform **participatory functions** jointly with the State: primary, adult and vocational education; the development of agriculture and natural resources other than minerals; and the provision and maintenance of health services."},{"h4":"(b) Sources of revenue"},{"ol":["**Statutory allocation from the Federation Account** — 20.60% of the distributable pool, shared among the councils and normally the largest source by a wide margin.","**Share of value added tax** — 35% of the VAT pool.","**Share of State internally generated revenue** — under section 162(7), commonly 10% of the State's IGR.","**Tenement rates** on privately owned houses and tenements assessed by the council.","**Licences and fees** — bicycle, truck, canoe, wheelbarrow and cart licences; liquor licences; shops and kiosks rates; market and motor park fees; slaughter slab fees; registration of births, deaths and marriages; advertising permits.","**Earnings from commercial undertakings** — rent from council property, transport services, guest houses.","**Grants** from the Federal or State Government, frequently tied to a specified capital project.","**Loans**, with the approval of the State Government.","**Fines and penalties** imposed under council by-laws and by customary courts."]},{"h4":"(c) Duties of the Treasurer"},{"ol":["Maintaining the books of account of the council in the form prescribed by the Model Financial Memoranda.","Preparing the annual estimates in consultation with the departments and the Chairman, for approval by the legislative council.","Preparing the monthly and annual financial statements and the transcripts required by the State.","Controlling the collection of revenue and ensuring that it is receipted and banked promptly and intact.","Ensuring that every payment is properly authorised, supported by a voucher, and within the vote.","Maintaining the vote book and advising the Chairman when a vote is nearing exhaustion.","Ensuring the safe custody of council funds and the security of receipt books and other accountable documents.","Advising the Chairman and the council on all financial matters, including the financial implications of proposed decisions.","Liaising with the Auditor-General for Local Governments and responding to audit queries."]},{"h4":"(d) Problems and remedies"},{"p":"**Problems**"},{"ol":["**Over-dependence on statutory allocation.** Most councils generate less than a tenth of their revenue internally, so their finances follow federally collected revenue and ultimately the oil price. A council cannot plan a capital programme on an income it cannot forecast.","**Deductions from the Joint Account.** Because allocations pass through the State, first-line deductions for primary school teachers' salaries and for state-wide projects often absorb most of the allocation before it reaches the council, and the deductions are frequently not transparent.","**Undeveloped internally generated revenue.** Tenement rating, potentially the largest local source, requires enumeration and valuation of properties that most councils have never carried out.","**Shortage of qualified personnel.** Rural councils in particular cannot attract accountants, engineers and valuers, so neither the revenue nor the expenditure side is competently managed.","**Poor records and weak internal control**, so that revenue due is not identified and cash collections at markets and motor parks leak substantially.","**Political interference**, including the appointment of caretaker committees in place of elected councils contrary to section 7(1), which removes the accountability of councils to their own electorates.","**Dominance of recurrent expenditure.** Personnel costs consume the greater part of revenue in most councils, leaving little for the services that justify the tier.","**Weak audit and oversight.** Audit reports are often years in arrears and rarely acted upon, and legislative councils frequently lack the capacity to scrutinise them."]},{"p":"**Remedies**"},{"ol":["**Reform the Joint Account** so that allocations reach councils directly, or at least so that every deduction is agreed at the Joint Account Allocation Committee and published.","**Enumerate and value properties** and develop the tenement rate, which is the only substantial revenue source genuinely within a council's control.","**Automate revenue collection**, paying into a single council account through electronic channels and abolishing cash handling by collectors — the measure that closes leakage most effectively.","**Guarantee the autonomy and tenure of elected councils**, so that councils are answerable to their electorates rather than to the State executive.","**Strengthen the finance function** — recruit and train qualified accountants and revenue officers, and resource the internal audit unit properly.","**Enforce timely audit and legislative scrutiny**, with sanctions where accounts are not submitted.","**Control the recurrent burden** by verifying the payroll and eliminating ghost workers, so that a larger share of revenue reaches capital projects."]}],"src":"Chapter 12.1–12.5"}]}},{"n":13,"t":"Cash Book, Transcripts and Subsidiary Accounts","brief":"The treasury cash book and the distinction between above-the-line and below-the-line transactions, the subsidiary accounts for advances, deposits and remittances, the monthly transcript rendered by a sub-accounting officer, and bank reconciliation.","outcomes":["Distinguish above-the-line from below-the-line transactions","Record transactions in a treasury cash book and extract the balance","Explain and account for advances, deposits and remittances","State the contents of a monthly transcript and its supporting schedules","Prepare a bank reconciliation statement for a government office"],"secs":[{"n":"13.1","t":"Above the line and below the line","b":[{"p":"Every transaction passing through a government cash book is either **above the line** or **below the line**. The \"line\" is the boundary of the revenue and expenditure account: transactions above it change the surplus or deficit for the year, and transactions below it do not."},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Above the line","Below the line"],"rows":[["Nature","Revenue and expenditure of the year","Movements on personal and impersonal accounts that create or discharge an asset or a liability"],["Effect on the accounts","Enters the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments as revenue or expenditure","Enters the Statement of Assets and Liabilities; does not affect revenue or expenditure"],["Examples","Taxes, fees, fines, rents; personnel cost, overhead cost, capital expenditure","Advances, deposits, remittances, investments, unallocated stores"],["Recorded in","Revenue and expenditure heads and subheads","Subsidiary (below-the-line) accounts"]]}},{"key":"The test is whether the transaction is **the government's own income or expense**, or merely **money held for or owed by someone else**. A deposit received as security for a contract is not revenue: it must be returned. An advance to an officer is not expenditure: it must be repaid."}]},{"n":"13.2","t":"The subsidiary accounts","b":[{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["Account","Nature","Balance represents"],"rows":[["**Advances**","Money paid out that will be repaid or accounted for — salary advances, touring advances, advances to contractors, imprest advances","A **debit** balance: an **asset**, being amounts recoverable"],["**Deposits**","Money received that will be repaid or applied for a specified purpose — tender and performance deposits, retention money, court deposits, unclaimed salaries","A **credit** balance: a **liability**, being amounts repayable"],["**Remittances**","Transfers of funds between government offices — from the Treasury to a sub-treasury and back, or between sub-treasuries","A **contra** item that must clear on consolidation; a balance means a transfer is in transit or unrecorded at one end"]]}},{"h4":"Advances"},{"ul":["An advance may be granted only where the Financial Regulations authorise it, and within the limits prescribed.","Every advance must be **recorded in the Advances Ledger** in the name of the officer or body concerned.","It must be **retired or repaid** within the period stipulated — a touring advance within a set number of days of return, a salary advance by monthly deduction.","An advance not retired when due is recovered from salary, and an officer who fails to retire an advance may not be granted another.","At the year end, outstanding advances appear as an **asset** in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities, and irrecoverable advances are written off only with the authority competent to approve the amount."]},{"h4":"Deposits"},{"ul":["Deposits are recorded in the **Deposits Ledger** in the name of the depositor.","They may be applied only for the purpose for which they were received, and are otherwise refundable.","A deposit **unclaimed for the period prescribed** in the Financial Regulations is transferred to revenue, but remains repayable if the depositor subsequently establishes the claim.","Deposits outstanding at the year end appear as a **liability**."]},{"warn":"A deposit is **never** treated as revenue merely because the money is in hand, and an advance is never treated as expenditure merely because the money has gone out. Doing so overstates both the revenue and the expenditure of the year and misstates the assets and liabilities."}]},{"n":"13.3","t":"The treasury cash book","b":[{"p":"The cash book is the book of prime entry for all receipts and payments at a treasury or sub-treasury. It is columnar, with separate columns for cash and bank on each side, and analysis columns for revenue heads, expenditure heads and each of the below-the-line accounts. It is **balanced daily** and the balance agreed with the cash actually held."},{"eg":{"t":"Summarising a sub-treasury cash book","q":[{"p":"The following transactions passed through the cash book of the Ilorin Sub-Treasury during the month of March 2026:"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","₦"],"rows":[["Balance at 1 March — cash on hand","150,000"],["Balance at 1 March — bank","4,250,000"],["Fees collected","1,800,000"],["Fines collected","420,000"],["Rents collected","630,000"],["Deposits received from contractors","350,000"],["Advances repaid by officers","180,000"],["Remittances received from Headquarters Treasury","12,000,000"],["Personnel cost paid","8,400,000"],["Overhead cost paid","2,650,000"],["Advances granted to officers","500,000"],["Deposits refunded","120,000"],["Remittances to Headquarters Treasury","2,850,000"]]}},{"p":"Cash on hand at 31 March was ₦150,000. Prepare a summary of the cash book distinguishing above-the-line from below-the-line items, and determine the closing bank balance."}],"a":[{"h4":"Summary of receipts and payments"},{"stmt":{"t":"Cash book summary — March 2026","rows":[["Balance brought forward (cash 150,000 + bank 4,250,000)",4400000],["**Receipts**@t",null],["Fees",1800000],["Fines",420000],["Rents",630000],["Deposits received",350000],["Advances repaid",180000],["Remittances from Headquarters",12000000],["Total receipts@sub",15380000],["**Payments**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-8400000],["Overhead cost",-2650000],["Advances granted",-500000],["Deposits refunded",-120000],["Remittances to Headquarters",-2850000],["Total payments@sub",-14520000],["Balance carried forward@tot",5260000]]}},{"p":"Cash on hand at 31 March is ₦150,000, so the **closing bank balance** is $5{,}260{,}000 - 150{,}000 = ₦5{,}110{,}000$."},{"h4":"Classification"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Receipts (₦)","Payments (₦)"],"rows":[["**Above the line**","",""],["Revenue — fees, fines and rents","2,850,000",""],["Expenditure — personnel and overhead cost","","11,050,000"],["**Below the line**","",""],["Advances","180,000","500,000"],["Deposits","350,000","120,000"],["Remittances","12,000,000","2,850,000"],["**Total**","**15,380,000**","**14,520,000**"]]}},{"p":"The net movements below the line for the month are: advances **₦320,000 net granted** (an increase in the asset); deposits **₦230,000 net received** (an increase in the liability); and remittances **₦9,150,000 net received** from headquarters."},{"note":"A useful check: the net increase in cash of ₦860,000 must equal revenue less expenditure, adjusted for the below-the-line movements: $2{,}850{,}000 - 11{,}050{,}000 - 320{,}000 + 230{,}000 + 9{,}150{,}000 = ₦860{,}000$. ✓"}]}}]},{"n":"13.4","t":"The monthly transcript","b":[{"def":{"t":"Transcript","d":"The monthly return rendered by a sub-accounting officer to the Accountant-General (or by a self-accounting unit to its headquarters), consisting of a copy of the cash book for the month together with all supporting schedules and documents, by which the transactions of the outstation are brought into the central accounts."}},{"h4":"Contents of a transcript"},{"ol":["The **cash book** for the month, cast, balanced and certified.","A **schedule of revenue collected**, analysed by head and subhead, supported by the receipt vouchers and treasury receipts.","A **schedule of expenditure**, analysed by head and subhead, supported by the paid payment vouchers in serial order.","A **schedule of advances** — opening balance, advances granted, advances repaid, closing balance, with the names of the officers concerned.","A **schedule of deposits** — opening balance, deposits received, deposits refunded, closing balance, with the names of the depositors.","A **schedule of remittances** in and out, with the numbers of the transfer advices.","A **bank reconciliation statement** with the bank statement and, where required, a bank certificate of balance.","A **certificate of cash and stamps** on hand at the close of the month.","A statement of **unpaid and outstanding vouchers**.","The **certificate of the sub-accounting officer** that the transcript is correct and complete."]},{"h4":"Purposes of the transcript"},{"ul":["To bring the outstation's transactions into the **central accounts** of the Federation or of the ministry.","To enable the Treasury to **check and verify** the transactions and the balances of the outstation.","To provide the basis of the **consolidated financial statements**.","To provide **control** over sub-accounting officers, since the transcript must be rendered by a fixed date each month and delay is a disciplinary matter.","To supply the **audit evidence** on which the Auditor-General relies for the outstation."]},{"note":"The transcript is the mechanism by which the accounts of a geographically dispersed government are consolidated. Where transcripts are rendered late — a perennial complaint in the Auditor-General's reports — the annual accounts of the Federation cannot be closed on time, which is why the Financial Regulations treat late rendition as a serious offence."}]},{"n":"13.5","t":"Bank reconciliation","b":[{"p":"The balance in the cash book will rarely agree with the balance on the bank statement at the same date. The reconciliation identifies the reasons and establishes the correct balance."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Cause of difference","Treatment"],"rows":[["Cheques or mandates issued but not yet presented","Deduct from the bank statement balance"],["Lodgements made but not yet credited by the bank","Add to the bank statement balance"],["Bank charges, commission and interest debited by the bank","Deduct in the **adjusted cash book**"],["Interest or direct credits received by the bank","Add in the **adjusted cash book**"],["Dishonoured cheques","Deduct in the adjusted cash book"],["Errors in the cash book","Correct in the adjusted cash book"],["Errors by the bank","Adjust against the bank statement balance"]]}},{"eg":{"t":"Bank reconciliation for the sub-treasury","q":[{"p":"Continuing the Ilorin example, the cash book bank balance at 31 March 2026 was ₦5,110,000 and the bank statement showed ₦5,640,000. Investigation revealed: mandates issued but not presented ₦680,000; lodgements not yet credited ₦140,000; bank charges not recorded in the cash book ₦18,000; and interest credited by the bank and not recorded ₦8,000. Prepare the adjusted cash book and the bank reconciliation statement."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Adjusted cash book (bank column)","rows":[["Balance per cash book",5110000],["Add: Interest credited by the bank",8000],["Less: Bank charges not recorded",-18000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",5100000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Bank reconciliation statement as at 31 March 2026","rows":[["Balance per bank statement",5640000],["Less: Mandates issued but not presented",-680000],["Add: Lodgements not yet credited",140000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",5100000]]}},{"p":"The two statements agree at **₦5,100,000**, which is the figure to be carried into the transcript and into the Statement of Assets and Liabilities. The unadjusted cash book figure of ₦5,110,000 is not the correct balance, because the bank charges and interest were genuine transactions of the month that the office had not yet recorded."},{"key":"Two rules govern every reconciliation. **Items the office knows about but the bank has not yet processed** — unpresented mandates and uncredited lodgements — are adjusted against the **bank statement**. **Items the bank has processed but the office has not yet recorded** — charges, interest, direct credits, dishonoured cheques — are adjusted in the **cash book**, because they are real entries that were simply omitted."}]}}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Closing cash balance","tex":"\\text{Opening} + \\text{Receipts} - \\text{Payments}"},{"lb":"Net advance movement","tex":"\\text{Advances granted} - \\text{Advances repaid}"},{"lb":"Net deposit movement","tex":"\\text{Deposits received} - \\text{Deposits refunded}"},{"lb":"Adjusted cash book","tex":"\\text{Cash book} + \\text{credits omitted} - \\text{debits omitted}"},{"lb":"Reconciliation","tex":"\\text{Bank statement} - \\text{unpresented} + \\text{uncredited} = \\text{adjusted cash book}"}],"focus":"One of the two or three most reliable Section B preparation questions in the paper. The usual form is a list of transactions from which you must prepare a cash book summary or a transcript, classify items above and below the line, and produce a bank reconciliation. Marks are given line by line, so present the work in proper statement form with headings and a total, even where you are unsure of one or two classifications.","errors":["Treating deposits received as revenue, or advances granted as expenditure.","Adjusting bank charges against the bank statement instead of in the cash book.","Netting remittances in and out instead of showing both, which loses the audit trail.","Omitting the certificate of cash and stamps and the bank reconciliation from the contents of a transcript.","Failing to deduct cash on hand when deriving the bank balance from a combined cash book total.","Leaving the reconciliation to end at the unadjusted cash book figure rather than the adjusted one."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Which of the following is a below-the-line transaction?","o":["Payment of salaries","Receipt of a contractor's performance deposit","Collection of market fees","Payment for stationery consumed","Collection of tenement rates"],"a":1,"w":"A deposit is repayable, so it creates a liability rather than revenue and does not affect the surplus or deficit for the year.","src":"Chapter 13.1"},{"q":"An advance granted to an officer and outstanding at the year end appears in the financial statements as","o":["expenditure","an asset","a liability","revenue","a contingent liability"],"a":1,"w":"The advance is recoverable, so it is an asset until it is retired or repaid.","src":"Chapter 13.2"},{"q":"In a bank reconciliation, bank charges not recorded in the cash book are","o":["added to the bank statement balance","deducted in the adjusted cash book","deducted from the bank statement balance","added in the adjusted cash book","ignored"],"a":1,"w":"The bank has already processed the charge; it is the office that has not recorded it, so the cash book must be adjusted.","src":"Chapter 13.5"},{"q":"The monthly return by which a sub-accounting officer brings his transactions into the central accounts is called a","o":["warrant","transcript","voucher","mandate","schedule of losses"],"a":1,"w":"The transcript comprises the cash book for the month together with all supporting schedules and certificates.","src":"Chapter 13.4"},{"q":"A cash book shows a bank balance of ₦2,400,000. Unpresented cheques are ₦350,000 and uncredited lodgements ₦120,000. There are no other differences. The bank statement balance is","o":["₦2,170,000","₦2,630,000","₦2,870,000","₦1,930,000","₦2,400,000"],"a":1,"w":"Work backwards: the bank statement balance less unpresented cheques plus uncredited lodgements equals the cash book balance.","calc":"\\text{Bank} = 2{,}400{,}000 + 350{,}000 - 120{,}000 = ₦2{,}630{,}000","src":"Chapter 13.5"},{"q":"Remittances between government offices are recorded","o":["as revenue at the receiving office and expenditure at the sending office","below the line at both offices, clearing on consolidation","as expenditure at both offices","only at the Headquarters Treasury","in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments as revenue"],"a":1,"w":"A remittance is an internal transfer of the government's own money. Treating it as revenue and expenditure would inflate both figures on consolidation.","src":"Chapter 13.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"The following transactions passed through the cash book of the Bauchi Sub-Treasury for the month of June 2026:\n\nBalance at 1 June: cash ₦200,000, bank ₦3,600,000. Receipts: court fees ₦940,000; motor licence fees ₦1,260,000; fines ₦180,000; deposits received ₦420,000; advances repaid ₦150,000; remittances from Headquarters Treasury ₦9,000,000. Payments: personnel cost ₦6,800,000; overhead cost ₦1,940,000; advances granted ₦360,000; deposits refunded ₦95,000; remittances to Headquarters ₦1,500,000.\n\nCash on hand at 30 June was ₦200,000. The bank statement at 30 June showed ₦5,080,000. Mandates issued but not presented amounted to ₦480,000, lodgements not yet credited ₦230,000, and bank charges of ₦25,000 had not been recorded in the cash book.\n\n(a) Prepare a summary of the cash book for the month. (b) Classify the transactions into above-the-line and below-the-line items. (c) Prepare the adjusted cash book and the bank reconciliation statement at 30 June 2026. (d) State six items that must accompany the transcript for the month.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Cash book summary"},{"stmt":{"t":"Bauchi Sub-Treasury — cash book summary for June 2026","rows":[["Balance brought forward (200,000 + 3,600,000)",3800000],["**Receipts**@t",null],["Court fees",940000],["Motor licence fees",1260000],["Fines",180000],["Deposits received",420000],["Advances repaid",150000],["Remittances from Headquarters Treasury",9000000],["Total receipts@sub",11950000],["**Payments**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-6800000],["Overhead cost",-1940000],["Advances granted",-360000],["Deposits refunded",-95000],["Remittances to Headquarters Treasury",-1500000],["Total payments@sub",-10695000],["Balance carried forward@tot",5055000]]}},{"p":"Cash on hand at 30 June is ₦200,000, so the cash book **bank balance** is $5{,}055{,}000 - 200{,}000 = ₦4{,}855{,}000$."},{"h4":"(b) Classification"},{"table":{"align":"lrr","head":["","Receipts (₦)","Payments (₦)"],"rows":[["**Above the line — revenue**","",""],["Court fees","940,000",""],["Motor licence fees","1,260,000",""],["Fines","180,000",""],["","**2,380,000**",""],["**Above the line — expenditure**","",""],["Personnel cost","","6,800,000"],["Overhead cost","","1,940,000"],["","","**8,740,000**"],["**Below the line**","",""],["Advances","150,000","360,000"],["Deposits","420,000","95,000"],["Remittances","9,000,000","1,500,000"],["","**9,570,000**","**1,955,000**"],["**Total**","**11,950,000**","**10,695,000**"]]}},{"p":"The revenue of the month is therefore **₦2,380,000** and the expenditure **₦8,740,000**, giving a deficit for the month of ₦6,360,000 which was financed by the net remittance of ₦7,500,000 from headquarters. Below the line, advances increased by ₦210,000 (an asset) and deposits by ₦325,000 (a liability)."},{"p":"Check: $2{,}380{,}000 - 8{,}740{,}000 - 210{,}000 + 325{,}000 + 7{,}500{,}000 = ₦1{,}255{,}000$, which equals the increase in cash from ₦3,800,000 to ₦5,055,000. ✓"},{"h4":"(c) Adjusted cash book and reconciliation"},{"stmt":{"t":"Adjusted cash book (bank column)","rows":[["Balance per cash book",4855000],["Less: Bank charges not recorded",-25000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",4830000]]}},{"stmt":{"t":"Bank reconciliation statement as at 30 June 2026","rows":[["Balance per bank statement",5080000],["Less: Mandates issued but not presented",-480000],["Add: Lodgements not yet credited",230000],["Adjusted cash book balance@tot",4830000]]}},{"p":"Both statements arrive at **₦4,830,000**, which is the bank figure to be carried into the transcript and into the Statement of Assets and Liabilities. Together with cash on hand of ₦200,000, total cash and bank at 30 June is ₦5,030,000."},{"warn":"If the two sides of a reconciliation do not agree, the difference is never to be forced or written off. It means a further item exists that has not been identified — an unrecorded lodgement, a second unpresented mandate, or an arithmetical error — and the sub-accounting officer must find it before the transcript is rendered."},{"h4":"(d) Items accompanying the transcript"},{"ol":["The **cash book** for the month, cast, balanced and certified by the sub-accounting officer.","A **schedule of revenue collected**, analysed by head and subhead, with the treasury receipts and receipt vouchers.","A **schedule of expenditure**, analysed by head and subhead, with the paid payment vouchers in serial order.","A **schedule of advances** showing the opening balance, advances granted, advances repaid and the closing balance, with the names of the officers concerned.","A **schedule of deposits** showing the opening balance, deposits received, deposits refunded and the closing balance, with the names of the depositors.","A **schedule of remittances** in and out, with the transfer advice numbers.","The **bank statement, bank certificate and bank reconciliation statement**.","A **certificate of cash and postage stamps** on hand at the close of the month.","A statement of **unpaid and outstanding vouchers**."]}],"src":"Chapter 13.3–13.5"}]}},{"n":14,"t":"Statutory Financial Statements — Cash Basis IPSAS","brief":"The financial statements required under the cash basis IPSAS and the Nigerian templates: the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments, the Statement of Assets and Liabilities, the fund statements, the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts, and the notes.","outcomes":["List the general purpose financial statements required under cash basis IPSAS","Prepare a Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","Prepare a Statement of Assets and Liabilities","Prepare a Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts and interpret the variances","State the notes and disclosures required","Explain the limitations of cash basis reporting"],"secs":[{"n":"14.1","t":"The required statements","b":[{"p":"The **Cash Basis IPSAS**, *Financial Reporting Under the Cash Basis of Accounting*, requires an entity to present a Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments together with accounting policies and explanatory notes, and encourages comparison with the budget. The Nigerian templates issued under the National Chart of Accounts prescribe the following set."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Statement","Content"],"rows":[["**Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments** (Statement of Cash Flow)","All cash received and paid during the period, classified by nature, showing the opening and closing cash and cash equivalents"],["**Statement of Assets and Liabilities**","Cash, investments, advances and other assets against deposits and other liabilities, and the net assets represented by the fund balances"],["**Statement of Consolidated Revenue Fund**","Opening balance, revenue, recurrent expenditure and transfers, giving the closing balance on the CRF"],["**Statement of Capital Development Fund**","Opening balance, transfers from the CRF, capital receipts, capital expenditure and the closing balance"],["**Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts**","Original budget, final budget, actual amounts on a comparable basis, and the variance, with explanations of material differences"],["**Notes to the financial statements**","Statement of accounting policies and supporting analyses of every principal figure"]]}},{"key":"The **Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts** is peculiar to the public sector and is required under both cash and accrual IPSAS. Because the budget is a legal authority rather than a management plan, reporting against it is an obligation, not a convenience. Candidates who omit it from a list of required statements lose an easy mark."}]},{"n":"14.2","t":"The Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","b":[{"p":"This is the principal statement under the cash basis. It reports **only cash movements**: revenue is recognised when received and expenditure when paid, whatever period the transaction relates to."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments","q":[{"p":"The following balances were extracted from the records of the Government of Kwara State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (all figures in ₦'000):"},{"table":{"align":"lr","head":["","₦'000"],"rows":[["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account","48,600,000"],["Value added tax allocation","14,200,000"],["Internally generated revenue — taxes","9,800,000"],["Internally generated revenue — fees, fines and licences","3,400,000"],["Grants received","1,200,000"],["Other receipts","640,000"],["Personnel cost — salaries, wages and allowances","28,500,000"],["Social contributions — pension and gratuity","3,100,000"],["Overhead cost","12,700,000"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges — public debt service and statutory transfers","6,900,000"],["Capital expenditure","21,400,000"],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026","3,150,000"]]}},{"p":"Prepare the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year ended 31 December 2026."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Kwara State — Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**RECEIPTS**@t",null],["Statutory allocation",48600000],["Value added tax allocation",14200000],["Internally generated revenue — taxes",9800000],["Internally generated revenue — fees, fines and licences",3400000],["Grants received",1200000],["Other receipts",640000],["Total receipts@sub",77840000],["**PAYMENTS**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-28500000],["Social contributions",-3100000],["Overhead cost",-12700000],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges",-6900000],["Capital expenditure",-21400000],["Total payments@sub",-72600000],["Net cash flow for the year@t",5240000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026",3150000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December 2026@tot",8390000]]}},{"note":"Note the discipline of the statement: it begins with receipts, deducts payments, and reconciles to the movement in cash. The closing figure of ₦8,390,000 thousand must equal the cash and bank balances in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities — the articulation check to perform before submitting any answer."}]}}]},{"n":"14.3","t":"The Statement of Assets and Liabilities","b":[{"p":"Under the cash basis this statement is limited: it shows the **financial** assets and liabilities arising from cash transactions — cash, investments, advances, deposits — but **no property, plant and equipment, no receivables and no payables**, because those are not recognised on a cash basis."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Assets and Liabilities","q":[{"p":"Continuing the Kwara State example, the following further balances stood at 31 December 2026 (₦'000): advances outstanding ₦1,240,000; investments ₦2,600,000; deposits held ₦980,000. The Consolidated Revenue Fund balance carried forward was ₦6,850,000. Prepare the Statement of Assets and Liabilities."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Kwara State — Statement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**ASSETS**@t",null],["Cash and cash equivalents",8390000],["Investments",2600000],["Advances",1240000],["Total assets@sub",12230000],["**LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Deposits",-980000],["Total liabilities@sub",-980000],["NET ASSETS@tot",11250000],["**REPRESENTED BY**@t",null],["Consolidated Revenue Fund",6850000],["Capital Development Fund",4400000],["Total funds@tot",11250000]]}},{"p":"The Capital Development Fund balance of ₦4,400,000 thousand is the balancing figure: net assets of ₦11,250,000 less the CRF balance of ₦6,850,000."},{"warn":"A candidate who includes buildings, roads, vehicles or trade payables in a **cash basis** Statement of Assets and Liabilities has misunderstood the basis. Those items appear only under accrual IPSAS, in the Statement of Financial Position (Chapter 16). This is the single most common error in the question."}]}}]},{"n":"14.4","t":"The Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","b":[{"p":"The statement compares the **final budget** with the **actual amounts on a comparable basis** and discloses the variance. Where the original and final budgets differ — because of a supplementary appropriation or virement — both are shown. Material variances must be explained, either on the face of the statement or in the notes."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","q":[{"p":"The final budget of Kwara State for 2026 provided for receipts of ₦81,500,000 thousand and payments of the same amount, made up as follows (₦'000): statutory allocation 52,000,000; VAT 13,000,000; IGR taxes 11,000,000; IGR other 3,000,000; grants 2,000,000; other receipts 500,000. Payments: personnel cost 29,000,000; social contributions 3,200,000; overhead cost 13,500,000; CRF charges 7,000,000; capital expenditure 28,800,000. Prepare the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts and comment on the performance."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Final budget ₦'000","Actual ₦'000","Variance ₦'000"],"rows":[["**RECEIPTS**","","",""],["Statutory allocation","52,000,000","48,600,000","(3,400,000)"],["Value added tax allocation","13,000,000","14,200,000","1,200,000"],["IGR — taxes","11,000,000","9,800,000","(1,200,000)"],["IGR — fees, fines and licences","3,000,000","3,400,000","400,000"],["Grants received","2,000,000","1,200,000","(800,000)"],["Other receipts","500,000","640,000","140,000"],["**Total receipts**","**81,500,000**","**77,840,000**","**(3,660,000)**"],["**PAYMENTS**","","",""],["Personnel cost","29,000,000","28,500,000","500,000"],["Social contributions","3,200,000","3,100,000","100,000"],["Overhead cost","13,500,000","12,700,000","800,000"],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges","7,000,000","6,900,000","100,000"],["Capital expenditure","28,800,000","21,400,000","7,400,000"],["**Total payments**","**81,500,000**","**72,600,000**","**8,900,000**"],["**Net**","**—**","**5,240,000**","**5,240,000**"]]}},{"p":"A variance on **receipts** is favourable when actual exceeds budget; a variance on **payments** is favourable when actual is below budget. Both are shown here as budget less actual for payments and actual less budget for receipts, with adverse amounts in brackets."},{"h4":"Comment on performance"},{"ul":["**Revenue underperformed by ₦3.66 billion (4.5%).** The shortfall is concentrated in statutory allocation, which came in ₦3.4 billion below budget, reflecting lower federally collected revenue than the benchmark assumed. VAT, in contrast, exceeded budget by ₦1.2 billion.","**Internally generated revenue reached 94.3% of budget** ($13{,}200 \\div 14{,}000$), with the shortfall in taxes partly offset by better performance on fees and licences. This is respectable but leaves the State drawing about 81% of its revenue from federation sources.","**Capital expenditure achieved only 74.3% of budget** ($21{,}400 \\div 28{,}800$), an underspend of ₦7.4 billion. This is by far the largest variance and is the one requiring explanation: it is the classic pattern in which the revenue shortfall is absorbed entirely by the capital budget, because recurrent commitments — salaries, pensions and debt service — cannot be deferred.","**Recurrent expenditure was contained within budget** on every line, with an aggregate saving of ₦1.5 billion (3.0%), so the underspend was not the result of general austerity but of deliberate protection of recurrent items.","**The reported surplus of ₦5.24 billion is therefore misleading** if read as good performance. It arises from failing to execute a quarter of the capital programme, not from revenue outperformance. A user should read the capital variance and the revenue variance together before drawing any conclusion."]},{"key":"Examiners award most of the marks in this question for the **commentary**, not the table. The commentary that scores is the one that connects two variances — here, that the revenue shortfall was met entirely out of capital — rather than describing each line in turn."}]}}]},{"n":"14.5","t":"Notes and disclosures","b":[{"ol":["A **statement of accounting policies**, including that the statements are prepared on the cash basis in accordance with the Cash Basis IPSAS, the reporting currency, and the basis of consolidation.","The **composition of cash and cash equivalents**, and a reconciliation with the amounts in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments.","Any **restrictions** on cash balances and access to borrowing facilities.","The **basis of the budget** and the period it covers, and an explanation of material variances.","Amounts of **payments made by third parties on behalf of the entity** — for example a donor paying a contractor directly.","**Undrawn borrowing facilities** and the terms attached.","Detailed **analyses supporting each principal line** of the statements — revenue by source, expenditure by ministry, advances and deposits by category.","The **entities consolidated** where the statements cover a group of entities.","**Related party disclosures** and the emoluments of key management personnel."]},{"h4":"Limitations of cash basis reporting"},{"ul":["It **ignores receivables and payables**, so unpaid contractors' certificates and uncollected taxes are invisible and the true financial position is not disclosed.","It **omits non-current assets and depreciation**, so the cost of using government assets never enters the accounts and the asset base is unknown.","It permits **manipulation by timing** — deferring payments past the year end improves the reported surplus without any change in substance.","It **does not disclose the full cost of a service**, so economy and efficiency cannot be assessed.","It shows only **cash liabilities**, understating public debt where obligations have been incurred but not paid.","It is **not comparable** with accrual-based reporting elsewhere."]},{"p":"These limitations are the reason for the move to accrual basis IPSAS, dealt with in Chapters 15 and 16."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Net cash flow","tex":"\\text{Total receipts} - \\text{Total payments}"},{"lb":"Closing cash","tex":"\\text{Opening cash} + \\text{Net cash flow}"},{"lb":"Net assets","tex":"\\text{Total assets} - \\text{Total liabilities}"},{"lb":"Budget variance — receipts","tex":"\\text{Actual} - \\text{Budget}"},{"lb":"Budget variance — payments","tex":"\\text{Budget} - \\text{Actual}"},{"lb":"Budget performance","tex":"\\frac{\\text{Actual}}{\\text{Budget}} \\times 100"}],"focus":"The most reliable preparation question in the paper. A Section B question in most diets asks for a Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments, a Statement of Assets and Liabilities, or both, from a trial balance or a list of balances, often with a budget comparison and a commentary. Marks follow the lines of the statement, so use the proper headings and reach a total even where one or two items are uncertain — and always check that closing cash in the cash statement equals cash in the assets statement.","errors":["Including property, plant and equipment, receivables or payables in a cash basis Statement of Assets and Liabilities.","Failing to reconcile closing cash in the cash statement to cash in the assets statement.","Treating advances as expenditure and deposits as revenue instead of as below-the-line items.","Computing payment variances in the same direction as receipt variances, so that an underspend appears adverse.","Omitting the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts from the list of required statements.","Describing an underspend on capital as a favourable outcome without noting that projects were not delivered."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under the cash basis IPSAS, which of the following would NOT appear in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities?","o":["Cash and cash equivalents","Property, plant and equipment","Advances","Deposits","Investments"],"a":1,"w":"Non-current assets are recognised only under accrual IPSAS. A cash basis statement shows financial assets and liabilities arising from cash transactions.","src":"Chapter 14.3"},{"q":"Total receipts were ₦92,400,000 and total payments ₦86,150,000. Opening cash was ₦4,300,000. Closing cash is","o":["₦6,250,000","₦10,550,000","₦1,950,000","₦14,850,000","₦2,150,000"],"a":1,"w":"Net cash flow is ₦6,250,000; add the opening balance.","calc":"4{,}300{,}000 + (92{,}400{,}000 - 86{,}150{,}000) = ₦10{,}550{,}000","src":"Chapter 14.2"},{"q":"The statement peculiar to the public sector and required under both cash and accrual IPSAS is the","o":["Statement of Cash Flows","Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts","Statement of Financial Position","Statement of Financial Performance","Statement of Changes in Net Assets"],"a":1,"w":"The budget is a legal authority to spend, so reporting against it is a statutory obligation rather than a management convenience.","src":"Chapter 14.1"},{"q":"Actual capital expenditure of ₦18,000,000 against a budget of ₦24,000,000 represents a budget performance of","o":["133%","75%","25%","60%","80%"],"a":1,"w":"Divide actual by budget. The 25% underspend means a quarter of the capital programme was not executed.","calc":"\\frac{18{,}000{,}000}{24{,}000{,}000} \\times 100 = 75\\%","src":"Chapter 14.4"},{"q":"A principal limitation of cash basis reporting is that it","o":["is difficult to audit","does not disclose receivables and payables","requires actuarial valuation","cannot be compared with the budget","is expensive to prepare"],"a":1,"w":"Recognising only cash means unpaid obligations and uncollected revenue never appear, so the true financial position is not shown.","src":"Chapter 14.5"},{"q":"Closing cash in the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments must agree with","o":["net assets in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities","cash and cash equivalents in the Statement of Assets and Liabilities","the Consolidated Revenue Fund balance","total receipts for the year","the budgeted surplus"],"a":1,"w":"This articulation check should be performed on every answer before it is submitted.","src":"Chapter 14.2"}],"theory":[{"q":"The following balances were extracted from the records of the Government of Ondo State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000):\n\nReceipts: statutory allocation 39,200,000; VAT allocation 11,600,000; internally generated revenue 8,750,000; grants 900,000; other receipts 350,000.\n\nPayments: personnel cost 24,100,000; social contributions 2,650,000; overhead cost 10,300,000; Consolidated Revenue Fund charges 5,400,000; capital expenditure 16,800,000.\n\nOther balances at 31 December 2026: advances outstanding 1,050,000; investments 3,200,000; deposits held 760,000. Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026 were 2,480,000, and the Consolidated Revenue Fund balance carried forward is 5,900,000.\n\n(a) Prepare the Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year. (b) Prepare the Statement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31 December 2026. (c) State four limitations of financial statements prepared on the cash basis.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Cash Receipts and Payments for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**RECEIPTS**@t",null],["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account",39200000],["Value added tax allocation",11600000],["Internally generated revenue",8750000],["Grants received",900000],["Other receipts",350000],["Total receipts@sub",60800000],["**PAYMENTS**@t",null],["Personnel cost",-24100000],["Social contributions",-2650000],["Overhead cost",-10300000],["Consolidated Revenue Fund charges",-5400000],["Capital expenditure",-16800000],["Total payments@sub",-59250000],["Net cash flow for the year@t",1550000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026",2480000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December 2026@tot",4030000]]}},{"h4":"(b) Statement of Assets and Liabilities"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Assets and Liabilities as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**ASSETS**@t",null],["Cash and cash equivalents",4030000],["Investments",3200000],["Advances",1050000],["Total assets@sub",8280000],["**LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Deposits",-760000],["Total liabilities@sub",-760000],["NET ASSETS@tot",7520000],["**REPRESENTED BY**@t",null],["Consolidated Revenue Fund",5900000],["Capital Development Fund (balancing figure)",1620000],["Total funds@tot",7520000]]}},{"p":"Two checks confirm the answer. Closing cash of ₦4,030,000 thousand appears identically in both statements; and net assets of ₦7,520,000 thousand equal the sum of the fund balances, the Capital Development Fund being the balancing figure of $7{,}520{,}000 - 5{,}900{,}000 = ₦1{,}620{,}000$ thousand."},{"p":"It is worth noting for the reader that the State spent ₦59.25 billion against receipts of ₦60.8 billion, a surplus of only 2.5% of receipts, and that recurrent expenditure of ₦42.45 billion consumed 70% of receipts before a single capital project was funded. Internally generated revenue of ₦8.75 billion represents just 14.4% of total receipts, so the State depends on federation sources for more than four-fifths of its income."},{"h4":"(c) Limitations of cash basis financial statements"},{"ol":["**Receivables and payables are not recognised.** Taxes assessed but not collected, and contractors' certificates approved but not paid, do not appear anywhere in the statements. A government with substantial unpaid liabilities can therefore report a healthy cash surplus while being in serious financial difficulty.","**Non-current assets and depreciation are omitted.** Roads, buildings, plant and vehicles are treated as expenditure of the year of purchase and then disappear from the records. Government does not know what assets it owns, no charge is made for their consumption, and the full cost of delivering a service is never known — which makes any assessment of economy or efficiency impossible.","**Results can be manipulated by timing.** Deferring payments until after the year end, or accelerating collections into it, improves the reported position without any change in the underlying substance. The cash basis rewards precisely the behaviour that creates arrears.","**Public debt is understated.** Only cash movements on borrowing appear; obligations incurred and not yet paid, guarantees, and long-term commitments such as pension liabilities are outside the statements altogether.","**Comparability is poor.** The statements cannot be compared with those of private-sector entities, of international organisations, or of governments reporting on the accrual basis."]},{"note":"These limitations are the reason Nigeria adopted accrual basis IPSAS with effect from 1 January 2016, with the transitional relief provided by IPSAS 33."}],"src":"Chapter 14.2–14.5"}]}},{"n":15,"t":"IPSAS 33 — First-Time Adoption of Accrual Basis IPSAS","brief":"The transitional standard: the date of adoption and the opening statement of financial position, the three-year relief period, deemed cost, the exemptions that affect fair presentation and those that do not, and the disclosures required.","outcomes":["State the objective and scope of IPSAS 33","Define first-time adopter, date of adoption and transitional IPSAS financial statements","Prepare the opening statement of financial position","Distinguish exemptions that affect fair presentation from those that do not","Explain the three-year transitional relief period and the use of deemed cost","State when an entity may assert compliance with accrual basis IPSAS"],"secs":[{"n":"15.1","t":"Objective and key terms","b":[{"p":"**IPSAS 33, First-time Adoption of Accrual Basis IPSAS**, was issued to ease the transition from another basis of accounting — typically the cash basis — to full accrual IPSAS. Its objective is to provide **transitional exemptions** so that an entity can adopt accrual accounting without being required to do everything at once, while ensuring that users are told exactly what has and has not yet been done."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Term","Meaning"],"rows":[["**First-time adopter**","An entity that presents its first transitional IPSAS financial statements or its first IPSAS financial statements for the reporting period"],["**Date of adoption of IPSAS**","The date on which the entity adopts accrual basis IPSAS for the first time — the beginning of the period for which the first statements are prepared"],["**Transitional IPSAS financial statements**","The first statements prepared under accrual IPSAS in which the entity takes one or more of the exemptions that **affect fair presentation and compliance**"],["**First IPSAS financial statements**","The first statements in which the entity is able to make an **explicit and unreserved statement of full compliance** with accrual basis IPSAS"],["**Opening statement of financial position**","The statement of financial position prepared at the date of adoption, being the starting point for accrual accounting"],["**Deemed cost**","An amount used as a surrogate for cost or depreciated cost at a given date, where the actual cost cannot be determined reliably"]]}},{"key":"The distinction between **transitional** IPSAS financial statements and **first** IPSAS financial statements is the heart of the standard. An entity may not claim compliance with accrual IPSAS while it is still relying on an exemption that affects fair presentation — and the exemption may not be relied on for more than three years."}]},{"n":"15.2","t":"The opening statement of financial position","b":[{"p":"At the date of adoption, the entity prepares an opening statement of financial position. In doing so it must:"},{"ol":["**Recognise** all assets and liabilities whose recognition is required by IPSAS, subject to the exemptions taken.","**Derecognise** items previously recognised that IPSAS does not permit as assets or liabilities.","**Reclassify** items into the categories IPSAS requires — for example separating investment property from property, plant and equipment.","**Measure** all recognised assets and liabilities in accordance with IPSAS, subject to the exemptions."]},{"p":"The resulting adjustments are recognised directly in **opening accumulated surplus or deficit** (or another category of net assets/equity where appropriate), not in the surplus or deficit for the period."},{"warn":"Adjustments on transition go to **opening accumulated surplus or deficit**, not through the Statement of Financial Performance. Running them through performance would make the year of transition look catastrophic or spectacular for reasons that have nothing to do with the year's operations."}]},{"n":"15.3","t":"The exemptions","b":[{"p":"IPSAS 33 divides the reliefs into two classes, and the distinction determines whether the entity may claim compliance."},{"h4":"A. Exemptions that AFFECT fair presentation and compliance"},{"p":"These allow the entity a **three-year transitional relief period** from the date of adoption in which it need not recognise, or need not measure in accordance with IPSAS, certain items. While any of them is relied on, the entity presents **transitional IPSAS financial statements** and may **not** assert compliance with accrual IPSAS."},{"ul":["**Property, plant and equipment** (IPSAS 17) — three-year relief from recognition and/or measurement. This is the exemption Nigerian entities have relied on most heavily, because identifying and valuing legacy infrastructure acquired over decades, often without records, is the single largest practical obstacle to accrual adoption.","**Investment property** (IPSAS 16).","**Intangible assets** (IPSAS 31).","**Financial instruments** (IPSAS 41) — recognition and measurement.","**Inventories** (IPSAS 12).","**Service concession assets and the related liabilities** (IPSAS 32).","**Biological assets and agricultural produce** (IPSAS 27).","**Employee benefits — the defined benefit obligation** (IPSAS 39), where actuarial valuation cannot be obtained immediately.","**Interests in controlled entities, associates and joint ventures**, and the preparation of consolidated financial statements.","**Provisions, contingent liabilities and contingent assets** (IPSAS 19)."]},{"h4":"B. Exemptions that DO NOT affect fair presentation and compliance"},{"p":"These are practical simplifications that do not prevent an entity from asserting full compliance."},{"ul":["Not restating **comparative information** for the periods before adoption, where it is not available.","Using **deemed cost** for property, plant and equipment, investment property or intangible assets where the actual cost is not determinable — fair value at the date of adoption, or a reliable revaluation, becomes the cost from that date.","Not applying **IPSAS 5** to capitalise borrowing costs on qualifying assets in progress at the date of adoption.","Relief in respect of **related party disclosures** for periods before adoption.","Relief in respect of the **disclosure of key management personnel** emoluments for prior periods.","Not applying **IPSAS 34–38** retrospectively to business combinations occurring before the date of adoption."]},{"def":{"t":"Deemed cost","d":"Where an entity cannot determine the historical cost of an asset — very common for government property acquired long ago, or transferred without consideration — it may use fair value at the date of adoption as the deemed cost. That amount then becomes the basis for depreciation and for any subsequent carrying amount, exactly as if it were cost."}}]},{"n":"15.4","t":"The three-year relief period","b":[{"ol":["The relief period runs for **three years from the date of adoption**.","During it the entity presents **transitional IPSAS financial statements** and must state that it has adopted accrual basis IPSAS **subject to the transitional exemptions taken**.","The entity must disclose **which exemptions it has taken**, the **progress made** towards recognition and measurement, and the **date by which it expects to comply in full**.","Where an item is recognised **during** the relief period, the adjustment is made against opening accumulated surplus or deficit in the period of recognition.","At the end of the three years the entity must have recognised and measured everything, and its statements become **first IPSAS financial statements** carrying an explicit and unreserved statement of compliance.","An entity that adopts accrual IPSAS without relying on any exemption affecting fair presentation may make that statement of compliance **immediately**."]},{"p":"In Nigeria the accrual basis was adopted with effect from **1 January 2016**, so a federal entity taking the property, plant and equipment exemption from that date had until the end of **2018** to complete recognition and measurement. In practice many entities have taken longer, principally because the identification and valuation of legacy assets has proved slower than the roadmap assumed."}]},{"n":"15.5","t":"Presentation and disclosure","b":[{"ol":["A statement that the financial statements are the entity's **transitional IPSAS financial statements** or its **first IPSAS financial statements**, as the case may be.","The **date of adoption** of accrual basis IPSAS.","The **exemptions taken** and, for each, the reason and the effect on the statements.","For items not yet recognised, the **progress towards recognition** and the date by which the entity expects to recognise them.","Where **deemed cost** has been used, the fact, the amounts involved and the basis on which fair value was determined.","A **reconciliation** of the amounts reported under the previous basis of accounting to the amounts under accrual IPSAS, for both net assets and surplus or deficit, at the date of adoption and at the end of the comparative period.","Where comparative information has **not been restated**, that fact and the reason.","The **accounting policies** adopted, in full."]},{"h4":"Challenges of adoption in practice"},{"ul":["**Identification and valuation of legacy assets**, many of them acquired decades ago, without title documents, records of cost or reliable registers.","**Shortage of valuers and of accountants trained in accrual concepts**, particularly at state and local government level.","**Weak information systems** unable to capture accruals, and the cost of upgrading them.","**The persistence of a cash budget.** Appropriation and control remain on a cash basis while reporting moves to accrual, so two parallel sets of figures must be maintained and reconciled.","**Institutional resistance**, since accrual reporting discloses liabilities and asset shortfalls previously invisible.","**Cost**, which for a small entity may be difficult to justify against the benefit."]},{"key":"The purpose of IPSAS 33 is not to relax the standards but to make the transition honest. An entity may take longer to recognise its assets, but it must say so, say which assets, and say when it will finish — and it may not describe its statements as IPSAS-compliant until it has."}]}],"formulas":[],"focus":"Regularly examined since Nigeria adopted accrual IPSAS. Section A tests the length of the transitional relief period, the meaning of deemed cost, and the date of adoption. Section B asks for the exemptions available, the distinction between transitional and first IPSAS financial statements, or the challenges of implementation. Learn the three-year period, the two classes of exemption, and at least five items in each class.","errors":["Giving the transitional relief period as five years; it is three.","Treating all exemptions alike; only those in class A prevent an assertion of compliance.","Putting transition adjustments through the Statement of Financial Performance instead of opening accumulated surplus or deficit.","Saying deemed cost means fair value must be used every year; it fixes an amount at the date of adoption which is then treated as cost.","Asserting compliance with accrual IPSAS while still relying on a class A exemption."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"The transitional relief period allowed by IPSAS 33 for the recognition and measurement of certain assets and liabilities is","o":["one year","three years","five years","two years","ten years"],"a":1,"w":"Three years from the date of adoption, after which everything must be recognised and measured in accordance with IPSAS.","src":"Chapter 15.4"},{"q":"An amount used as a surrogate for cost where the actual cost of an asset cannot be determined reliably is called","o":["fair value","deemed cost","residual value","recoverable amount","depreciated replacement cost"],"a":1,"w":"Deemed cost is fixed at the date of adoption and thereafter treated exactly as if it were cost, for depreciation and carrying amount.","src":"Chapter 15.3"},{"q":"Adjustments arising on transition to accrual basis IPSAS are recognised in","o":["surplus or deficit for the period","opening accumulated surplus or deficit","the statement of cash flows","a revaluation reserve only","the notes only"],"a":1,"w":"Taking them through performance would distort the results of the year of transition for reasons unconnected with its operations.","src":"Chapter 15.2"},{"q":"Financial statements in which an entity relies on an exemption that affects fair presentation are described as","o":["first IPSAS financial statements","transitional IPSAS financial statements","modified IPSAS financial statements","qualified financial statements","cash basis financial statements"],"a":1,"w":"Only when no such exemption remains may the entity present first IPSAS financial statements with an explicit and unreserved statement of compliance.","src":"Chapter 15.1"},{"q":"Which of the following exemptions does NOT affect fair presentation and compliance with accrual basis IPSAS?","o":["Non-recognition of property, plant and equipment","Non-restatement of comparative information","Non-recognition of the defined benefit obligation","Non-recognition of inventories","Non-recognition of service concession assets"],"a":1,"w":"Not restating comparatives is a practical simplification. The other four withhold recognition of items IPSAS requires, and so prevent an assertion of full compliance.","src":"Chapter 15.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"Nigeria adopted the accrual basis IPSAS with effect from 1 January 2016. (a) Explain the objective of IPSAS 33 and define \"first-time adopter\", \"date of adoption\" and \"deemed cost\". (b) Distinguish between transitional IPSAS financial statements and first IPSAS financial statements. (c) State five exemptions that affect fair presentation and three that do not. (d) Discuss the challenges facing public sector entities in Nigeria in adopting the accrual basis.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Objective and definitions"},{"p":"**Objective.** IPSAS 33 provides transitional exemptions to entities adopting accrual basis IPSAS for the first time, so that the change of basis can be made in an orderly way without requiring every asset and liability to be identified, valued and recognised on a single date. At the same time it requires the entity to disclose exactly which exemptions it has taken, so that users are not misled about the completeness of the statements."},{"ul":["**First-time adopter** — an entity presenting its first transitional IPSAS financial statements or its first IPSAS financial statements for the reporting period.","**Date of adoption of IPSAS** — the date on which the entity adopts accrual basis IPSAS for the first time, being the beginning of the period for which the first such statements are prepared. For Nigerian federal entities, 1 January 2016.","**Deemed cost** — an amount used as a surrogate for cost, or for depreciated cost, at a given date, where the actual cost cannot be determined reliably. Fair value at the date of adoption is then treated exactly as though it were cost for all subsequent purposes."]},{"h4":"(b) Transitional against first IPSAS financial statements"},{"table":{"align":"lll","head":["","Transitional IPSAS financial statements","First IPSAS financial statements"],"rows":[["Exemptions relied on","One or more that affect fair presentation and compliance","None that affect fair presentation and compliance"],["Statement of compliance","Cannot assert compliance; states that accrual IPSAS has been adopted subject to the exemptions taken","Explicit and unreserved statement of full compliance with accrual basis IPSAS"],["Completeness","Some assets or liabilities are not yet recognised or measured","All assets and liabilities recognised and measured in accordance with IPSAS"],["Duration","Permitted for up to three years from the date of adoption","Thereafter, and permanently"],["Additional disclosure","Which exemptions were taken, progress made, and the date by which full compliance is expected","The normal IPSAS disclosures"]]}},{"p":"The relationship is sequential: an entity that takes a class A exemption presents transitional statements and moves to first IPSAS financial statements only when the last such exemption has been discharged, and in any event by the end of the three-year relief period."},{"h4":"(c) The exemptions"},{"p":"**Exemptions that affect fair presentation and compliance** (three-year relief):"},{"ol":["Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **property, plant and equipment** under IPSAS 17.","Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **investment property** under IPSAS 16.","Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **intangible assets** under IPSAS 31.","Non-recognition and/or non-measurement of **financial instruments**.","Non-recognition of **inventories** under IPSAS 12.","Non-recognition of **service concession assets and the related liabilities** under IPSAS 32.","Non-recognition of the **defined benefit obligation for employee benefits** under IPSAS 39.","Non-recognition of **provisions and contingent liabilities** under IPSAS 19.","Relief from **consolidation** of controlled entities and from equity accounting for associates and joint ventures."]},{"p":"**Exemptions that do not affect fair presentation and compliance:**"},{"ol":["**Non-restatement of comparative information** for periods before the date of adoption, where that information is not available.","Use of **deemed cost** for property, plant and equipment, investment property or intangible assets whose cost cannot be determined reliably.","Relief from **capitalising borrowing costs** on qualifying assets in progress at the date of adoption.","Relief from **related party disclosures** and from the disclosure of **key management personnel emoluments** for periods before adoption.","Relief from applying the **business combination standards retrospectively** to combinations occurring before the date of adoption."]},{"h4":"(d) Challenges of adoption in Nigeria"},{"ol":["**Identification and valuation of legacy assets.** This is by a wide margin the largest obstacle. Government owns roads, bridges, schools, hospitals, land and buildings acquired over many decades, frequently without title documents, purchase records or asset registers. Establishing what exists, who controls it and what it is worth is a physical exercise of enormous scale, which is precisely why the property, plant and equipment exemption has been the most widely used.","**Shortage of skills.** Accrual accounting requires judgement about recognition, measurement, impairment and depreciation that a cash-based service never had to exercise. There are too few accountants trained in accrual IPSAS, and too few professional valuers, especially at state and local government level.","**Weak information systems.** Many entities operate manual or partially computerised records incapable of maintaining an asset register, tracking receivables and payables, or supporting consolidation. GIFMIS addresses this at federal level but coverage is incomplete and states vary widely.","**A cash budget against accrual reporting.** Appropriation, warrants and vote control remain firmly on the cash basis, while reporting has moved to accrual. Two parallel measurement bases must therefore be maintained and reconciled, and the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts must bridge them.","**Institutional resistance.** Accrual reporting discloses unpaid liabilities, pension obligations and the deterioration of assets that cash reporting concealed. Officials whose performance will be judged against those figures have limited incentive to accelerate the process.","**Cost and competing priorities.** Valuation exercises, system upgrades and training are expensive and compete against service delivery for scarce funds, and the benefits accrue slowly and diffusely.","**Uneven adoption across tiers.** Federal progress has outrun that of many states and most local governments, which limits the comparability and consolidation that were among the principal objectives of adopting IPSAS in the first place."]},{"p":"The proper conclusion is that IPSAS 33 is doing what it was designed to do. It does not relax the standards; it makes the transition honest, by allowing an entity more time while requiring it to disclose what remains outstanding and when it will be completed. The risk is not the exemption itself but its indefinite extension, and the answer to that is enforcement of the three-year limit."}],"src":"Chapter 15.1–15.5"}]}},{"n":16,"t":"Accrual Basis Financial Statements","brief":"The general purpose financial statements required under accrual basis IPSAS: the Statement of Financial Performance, the Statement of Financial Position, the Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity, the Statement of Cash Flows and the notes — with a fully worked set.","outcomes":["List the general purpose financial statements required under accrual basis IPSAS","Prepare a Statement of Financial Performance","Prepare a Statement of Financial Position","Prepare a Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","Prepare a Statement of Cash Flows by the indirect method","Explain the advantages of accrual over cash reporting"],"secs":[{"n":"16.1","t":"The required statements","b":[{"p":"**IPSAS 1, Presentation of Financial Statements**, requires a complete set of financial statements to comprise the following."},{"table":{"align":"ll","head":["Statement","What it reports"],"rows":[["**Statement of Financial Performance**","Revenue earned and expenses incurred during the period, whether or not cash has moved, giving the surplus or deficit for the year"],["**Statement of Financial Position**","Assets, liabilities and net assets/equity at the reporting date, including property, plant and equipment, receivables and payables"],["**Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity**","The movement in each component of net assets — accumulated surplus, revaluation reserve, other reserves — between the opening and closing dates"],["**Statement of Cash Flows** (IPSAS 2)","Cash flows classified into operating, investing and financing activities, reconciling to the movement in cash and cash equivalents"],["**Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts** (IPSAS 24)","Original budget, final budget, actual amounts on a comparable basis and the variance, with explanations of material differences"],["**Notes**","A summary of significant accounting policies and other explanatory information, including analyses of every principal figure"]]}},{"key":"The essential difference from the cash basis is **recognition**. Revenue is recognised when it is **earned** and expenses when they are **incurred**, so the accounts show what the government owns and owes rather than merely what passed through its bank account."}]},{"n":"16.2","t":"The Statement of Financial Performance","b":[{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Financial Performance","q":[{"p":"The following balances were extracted from the accrual-based records of the Government of Ondo State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000): statutory allocation 39,200,000; VAT allocation 11,600,000; tax revenue 6,300,000; fees, fines and licences 2,900,000; grants 900,000; investment income 420,000; other revenue 350,000; salaries, wages and allowances 24,100,000; social benefits and pension contributions 2,650,000; depreciation and amortisation 4,850,000; use of goods and services 10,300,000; finance cost 1,900,000; grants and subsidies 1,400,000; impairment loss 260,000. Prepare the Statement of Financial Performance."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Financial Performance for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**REVENUE**@t",null],["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account",39200000],["Value added tax allocation",11600000],["Tax revenue",6300000],["Fees, fines and licences",2900000],["Grants",900000],["Investment income",420000],["Other revenue",350000],["Total revenue@sub",61670000],["**EXPENSES**@t",null],["Salaries, wages and allowances",-24100000],["Social benefits and pension contributions",-2650000],["Use of goods and services",-10300000],["Depreciation and amortisation",-4850000],["Grants and subsidies",-1400000],["Finance cost",-1900000],["Impairment loss",-260000],["Total expenses@sub",-45460000],["SURPLUS FOR THE YEAR@tot",16210000]]}},{"note":"Compare this with the cash basis statement for the same government in Chapter 14.5, which reported a net cash inflow of only ₦1,550,000 thousand. The difference is not error but basis: the accrual statement includes revenue earned but not yet received and excludes the ₦16,800,000 thousand spent on capital assets, which is not an expense but the acquisition of an asset — charged instead through the ₦4,850,000 thousand depreciation."}]}}]},{"n":"16.3","t":"The Statement of Financial Position","b":[{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Financial Position","q":[{"p":"At 31 December 2026 the following balances stood in the books of the Government of Ondo State (₦'000): property, plant and equipment 128,400,000; investment property 6,200,000; intangible assets 1,150,000; long-term investments 3,200,000; inventories 2,340,000; receivables 5,680,000; prepayments 480,000; cash and cash equivalents 4,030,000; payables 6,720,000; deposits 760,000; current portion of borrowings 3,500,000; long-term borrowings 28,600,000; employee benefit obligations 9,400,000; revaluation reserve 14,200,000. Prepare the Statement of Financial Position."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**NON-CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Property, plant and equipment",128400000],["Investment property",6200000],["Intangible assets",1150000],["Long-term investments",3200000],["@sub",138950000],["**CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Inventories",2340000],["Receivables",5680000],["Prepayments",480000],["Cash and cash equivalents",4030000],["@sub",12530000],["TOTAL ASSETS@t",151480000],["**CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Payables",-6720000],["Deposits",-760000],["Current portion of borrowings",-3500000],["@sub",-10980000],["**NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Long-term borrowings",-28600000],["Employee benefit obligations",-9400000],["@sub",-38000000],["TOTAL LIABILITIES@t",-48980000],["NET ASSETS@tot",102500000],["**NET ASSETS / EQUITY**@t",null],["Accumulated surplus",88300000],["Revaluation reserve",14200000],["TOTAL NET ASSETS / EQUITY@tot",102500000]]}},{"p":"The accumulated surplus of ₦88,300,000 thousand is the balancing figure: net assets of ₦102,500,000 less the revaluation reserve of ₦14,200,000."},{"key":"Everything in this statement that would be absent from a cash basis statement is the point of accrual accounting. **₦128.4 billion of property, plant and equipment**, **₦5.68 billion of receivables**, **₦6.72 billion of payables** and **₦38 billion of long-term obligations** — including a ₦9.4 billion pension liability — are all invisible under the cash basis."}]}}]},{"n":"16.4","t":"The Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","b":[{"p":"This statement explains the movement in each component of net assets between the opening and closing dates. It captures the surplus or deficit for the year together with items recognised directly in net assets, such as revaluation gains and the remeasurements of defined benefit plans."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","q":[{"p":"At 1 January 2026 the accumulated surplus was ₦72,090,000 thousand and the revaluation reserve ₦11,900,000 thousand. During the year a revaluation gain of ₦2,300,000 thousand was recognised. Prepare the statement."}],"a":[{"table":{"align":"lrrr","head":["","Accumulated surplus ₦'000","Revaluation reserve ₦'000","Total ₦'000"],"rows":[["Balance at 1 January 2026","72,090,000","11,900,000","83,990,000"],["Surplus for the year","16,210,000","—","16,210,000"],["Gain on revaluation of property","—","2,300,000","2,300,000"],["**Balance at 31 December 2026**","**88,300,000**","**14,200,000**","**102,500,000**"]]}},{"p":"The closing total of ₦102,500,000 thousand agrees with net assets in the Statement of Financial Position, and the closing accumulated surplus of ₦88,300,000 thousand with the figure shown there. **Perform both checks before submitting an answer.**"},{"warn":"A revaluation gain goes to the **revaluation reserve**, not to the surplus for the year. So do the remeasurements of a defined benefit obligation (IPSAS 39). Routing either through the Statement of Financial Performance is a standard error and misstates both statements."}]}}]},{"n":"16.5","t":"The Statement of Cash Flows","b":[{"p":"IPSAS 2 requires cash flows to be classified into **operating**, **investing** and **financing** activities. Operating cash flows may be presented by the **direct** method (gross receipts and payments) or the **indirect** method (surplus adjusted for non-cash items and working capital movements). IPSAS encourages the direct method; the indirect method is the one usually set in examinations because it tests understanding of the difference between the two bases."},{"eg":{"t":"Statement of Cash Flows — indirect method","q":[{"p":"During 2026 the Government of Ondo State: purchased property, plant and equipment for ₦16,800,000 thousand and intangible assets for ₦350,000 thousand; received investment income of ₦420,000 thousand; raised new borrowings of ₦2,000,000 thousand and repaid ₦5,770,000 thousand; and paid finance costs of ₦1,900,000 thousand. Inventories rose by ₦340,000 thousand, receivables by ₦1,180,000 thousand, payables by ₦890,000 thousand, deposits by ₦120,000 thousand and employee benefit obligations by ₦1,600,000 thousand, while prepayments fell by ₦60,000 thousand. Cash at 1 January 2026 was ₦2,480,000 thousand. Prepare the Statement of Cash Flows."}],"a":[{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ondo State — Statement of Cash Flows for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**CASH FLOWS FROM OPERATING ACTIVITIES**@t",null],["Surplus for the year",16210000],["Adjustments for non-cash items:@t",null],["Depreciation and amortisation",4850000],["Impairment loss",260000],["Finance cost",1900000],["Investment income",-420000],["Operating surplus before working capital changes@sub",22800000],["Increase in inventories",-340000],["Increase in receivables",-1180000],["Decrease in prepayments",60000],["Increase in payables",890000],["Increase in deposits",120000],["Increase in employee benefit obligations",1600000],["Cash generated from operations@sub",23950000],["Finance cost paid",-1900000],["Net cash from operating activities@t",22050000],["**CASH FLOWS FROM INVESTING ACTIVITIES**@t",null],["Purchase of property, plant and equipment",-16800000],["Purchase of intangible assets",-350000],["Investment income received",420000],["Net cash used in investing activities@t",-16730000],["**CASH FLOWS FROM FINANCING ACTIVITIES**@t",null],["Proceeds from borrowings",2000000],["Repayment of borrowings",-5770000],["Net cash used in financing activities@t",-3770000],["NET INCREASE IN CASH AND CASH EQUIVALENTS@t",1550000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 1 January 2026",2480000],["Cash and cash equivalents at 31 December 2026@tot",4030000]]}},{"key":"The net increase of ₦1,550,000 thousand is **exactly the net cash flow reported under the cash basis in Chapter 14.5**, and the closing figure of ₦4,030,000 thousand is the cash in the Statement of Financial Position. Cash is cash whatever the basis of accounting: what accrual reporting changes is everything *around* the cash, not the cash itself. Demonstrating that articulation is worth stating explicitly in an answer."},{"note":"Note the two-step treatment of finance cost and investment income. Both are removed from the surplus in the adjustments — finance cost added back because it is not an operating item, investment income deducted for the same reason — and then shown at the point where the cash actually moved: finance cost paid within operating (IPSAS permits either operating or financing), investment income received within investing."}]}}]},{"n":"16.6","t":"Advantages of accrual reporting","b":[{"ol":["**It discloses the full financial position.** Assets, receivables, payables and long-term obligations all appear, so the reader can see what government owns and owes rather than only what passed through the bank.","**It shows the true cost of services.** Depreciation charges the consumption of assets to the period that benefited, so the cost per pupil, per patient or per kilometre can be computed and value for money assessed.","**It improves asset management.** Recognising and depreciating assets forces the maintenance of asset registers, which most governments previously lacked, and makes deterioration visible.","**It discloses the real level of public debt**, including obligations incurred but not yet paid and pension liabilities.","**It removes the incentive to manipulate by timing.** Deferring a payment past the year end no longer improves the reported result, because the expense was recognised when incurred.","**It improves comparability** with other governments, with international organisations and with the private sector.","**It supports better decisions** on whether to buy or lease, to maintain or replace, and to contract out or provide directly — none of which can be evaluated properly on cash information."]},{"p":"Against these must be set the cost and complexity of accrual accounting, the judgement it requires and the scope for manipulation that judgement creates, and the difficulty of valuing government assets such as heritage assets and infrastructure for which no market exists. These are the arguments a balanced answer should acknowledge before concluding in favour of accrual."}]}],"formulas":[{"lb":"Surplus for the year","tex":"\\text{Total revenue} - \\text{Total expenses}"},{"lb":"Accounting equation","tex":"\\text{Assets} - \\text{Liabilities} = \\text{Net assets/Equity}"},{"lb":"Closing accumulated surplus","tex":"\\text{Opening} + \\text{Surplus for the year}"},{"lb":"Cash generated from operations","tex":"\\text{Surplus} + \\text{non-cash items} \\pm \\text{working capital changes}"},{"lb":"Net movement in cash","tex":"\\text{Operating} + \\text{Investing} + \\text{Financing}"}],"focus":"Together with Chapter 14, the core preparation chapter of the paper and increasingly the one set in Section B as accrual adoption beds in. Expect a list of balances from which two or three statements must be prepared. Present them in the proper IPSAS form with headings, subtotals and totals, and always perform the three articulation checks: net assets equals total net assets/equity; the closing accumulated surplus agrees with the changes statement; and closing cash in the cash flow statement equals cash in the position statement.","errors":["Treating capital expenditure as an expense in the Statement of Financial Performance; it is the acquisition of an asset, charged through depreciation.","Recognising a revaluation gain or a defined benefit remeasurement in surplus or deficit instead of in net assets/equity.","Omitting depreciation, which is the single largest accrual adjustment in most public sector statements.","Adding back a decrease in receivables and deducting an increase — the direction is the reverse for assets and for liabilities.","Classifying the purchase of property, plant and equipment as an operating rather than an investing cash flow.","Failing to reconcile closing cash in the cash flow statement to cash in the statement of financial position."],"quiz":{"mcq":[{"q":"Under accrual basis IPSAS, revenue is recognised when it is","o":["received in cash","earned","budgeted","assessed and collected","approved by the legislature"],"a":1,"w":"Revenue is recognised when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of when cash moves.","src":"Chapter 16.1"},{"q":"Total assets are ₦180,000,000 and total liabilities ₦52,000,000. Net assets are","o":["₦232,000,000","₦128,000,000","₦52,000,000","₦180,000,000","₦104,000,000"],"a":1,"w":"Net assets are total assets less total liabilities.","calc":"180{,}000{,}000 - 52{,}000{,}000 = ₦128{,}000{,}000","src":"Chapter 16.3"},{"q":"A gain on the revaluation of property is recognised in","o":["the Statement of Financial Performance","the Statement of Changes in Net Assets/Equity","the Statement of Cash Flows","the notes only","the Statement of Comparison of Budget and Actual Amounts"],"a":1,"w":"The gain goes to the revaluation reserve within net assets, not through surplus or deficit for the year.","src":"Chapter 16.4"},{"q":"In a cash flow statement prepared by the indirect method, an increase in receivables is","o":["added to the surplus","deducted from the surplus","shown as an investing outflow","shown as a financing outflow","ignored"],"a":1,"w":"Revenue has been recognised but the cash has not arrived, so cash is lower than the surplus suggests.","src":"Chapter 16.5"},{"q":"The purchase of property, plant and equipment is classified in the cash flow statement as","o":["an operating outflow","an investing outflow","a financing outflow","a non-cash item","an adjustment to net assets"],"a":1,"w":"Acquisition and disposal of non-current assets are investing activities.","src":"Chapter 16.5"},{"q":"Which item would appear in an accrual basis but NOT in a cash basis Statement of Financial Position?","o":["Cash and cash equivalents","Employee benefit obligations","Advances","Deposits","Investments"],"a":1,"w":"A pension obligation is incurred but unpaid, so it is recognised only under the accrual basis. The other four arise from cash transactions and appear under both.","src":"Chapter 16.3"}],"theory":[{"q":"The following balances were extracted from the accrual-based records of the Government of Ekiti State for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000):\n\nRevenue: statutory allocation 27,400,000; VAT allocation 8,300,000; tax revenue 4,100,000; fees and licences 1,850,000; grants 650,000; investment income 300,000.\n\nExpenses: salaries and allowances 17,600,000; pension contributions 1,940,000; use of goods and services 7,250,000; depreciation 3,400,000; finance cost 1,260,000; grants and subsidies 900,000.\n\nAssets at 31 December 2026: property, plant and equipment 94,500,000; investments 2,100,000; inventories 1,480,000; receivables 3,920,000; cash and cash equivalents 2,760,000.\n\nLiabilities: payables 4,610,000; deposits 540,000; borrowings — current 2,400,000, non-current 19,300,000; employee benefit obligations 6,850,000. The revaluation reserve stands at 9,700,000.\n\n(a) Prepare the Statement of Financial Performance for the year. (b) Prepare the Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2026. (c) Explain four advantages of accrual basis reporting over cash basis reporting for a state government.","marks":20,"a":[{"h4":"(a) Statement of Financial Performance"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ekiti State — Statement of Financial Performance for the year ended 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**REVENUE**@t",null],["Statutory allocation from the Federation Account",27400000],["Value added tax allocation",8300000],["Tax revenue",4100000],["Fees and licences",1850000],["Grants",650000],["Investment income",300000],["Total revenue@sub",42600000],["**EXPENSES**@t",null],["Salaries and allowances",-17600000],["Pension contributions",-1940000],["Use of goods and services",-7250000],["Depreciation",-3400000],["Grants and subsidies",-900000],["Finance cost",-1260000],["Total expenses@sub",-32350000],["SURPLUS FOR THE YEAR@tot",10250000]]}},{"h4":"(b) Statement of Financial Position"},{"stmt":{"t":"Government of Ekiti State — Statement of Financial Position as at 31 December 2026 (₦'000)","rows":[["**NON-CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Property, plant and equipment",94500000],["Investments",2100000],["@sub",96600000],["**CURRENT ASSETS**@t",null],["Inventories",1480000],["Receivables",3920000],["Cash and cash equivalents",2760000],["@sub",8160000],["TOTAL ASSETS@t",104760000],["**CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Payables",-4610000],["Deposits",-540000],["Current portion of borrowings",-2400000],["@sub",-7550000],["**NON-CURRENT LIABILITIES**@t",null],["Borrowings",-19300000],["Employee benefit obligations",-6850000],["@sub",-26150000],["TOTAL LIABILITIES@t",-33700000],["NET ASSETS@tot",71060000],["**NET ASSETS / EQUITY**@t",null],["Accumulated surplus (balancing figure)",61360000],["Revaluation reserve",9700000],["TOTAL NET ASSETS / EQUITY@tot",71060000]]}},{"p":"The accumulated surplus of ₦61,360,000 thousand is derived as net assets of ₦71,060,000 less the revaluation reserve of ₦9,700,000. Note also that total liabilities of ₦33.7 billion are equal to about 79% of a full year's revenue, of which the pension obligation of ₦6.85 billion would not have appeared at all under the cash basis."},{"h4":"(c) Advantages of accrual reporting"},{"ol":["**The full financial position is disclosed.** The statement above shows ₦94.5 billion of property, plant and equipment, ₦3.92 billion owed to the State and ₦33.7 billion owed by it, including a ₦6.85 billion pension obligation. Under the cash basis none of these would appear, and a reader could not tell whether the State was solvent. Disclosing accumulated liabilities is what makes it possible to hold a government to account for obligations it incurs but leaves to its successors.","**The true cost of services is measured.** Depreciation of ₦3.4 billion charges the consumption of assets to the year that benefited from them. Without it, a school built in 2010 appears to cost nothing to use in 2026, and no meaningful cost per pupil, per patient or per kilometre of road can be computed. Value for money cannot be assessed on information that omits the largest input.","**Manipulation by timing is removed.** Under the cash basis, deferring payment of contractors past the year end converts a deficit into a surplus without any change in substance, which is precisely how arrears accumulate. Under the accrual basis the expense is recognised when incurred and the unpaid amount appears as a payable, so the tactic achieves nothing.","**Asset management improves.** Recognising and depreciating assets requires an asset register that most governments have never maintained. The discipline of compiling and keeping it is frequently worth more than the accounting entries themselves, because it reveals what the State actually owns and what condition it is in.","**Comparability is improved**, with other states, with other countries and with the private sector, which strengthens the State's standing with lenders and rating agencies.","**Better decisions become possible** on buying against leasing, maintaining against replacing, and providing directly against contracting out — none of which can be evaluated on cash information alone."]},{"note":"A complete answer acknowledges the costs: accrual accounting is more expensive, requires skills scarce in the public service, and rests on judgements — asset lives, impairment, actuarial assumptions — that themselves create scope for manipulation. The conclusion is nevertheless that the information gained outweighs those costs, which is why IPSAS and Nigeria have both moved that way."}],"src":"Chapter 16.2–16.6"}]}}]});
